<h1 data-start="368" data-end="401"><strong data-start="370" data-end="401">Summary of the Conversation</strong></h1>
<h2 data-start="403" data-end="476"><strong data-start="406" data-end="474">1. You requested a summary of a 2025 Nature Communications study</strong></h2>
<p data-start="477" data-end="617"><strong data-start="477" data-end="487">Paper:</strong> <em data-start="488" data-end="617">Temporal structure of natural language processing in the human brain corresponds to layered hierarchy of large language models.</em></p>
<p data-start="619" data-end="633">Main findings:</p>
<ul data-start="635" data-end="1040">
<li data-start="635" data-end="720">
<p data-start="637" data-end="720">Deep LLM layers align with later neural processing in IFG and temporal pole (TP).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="721" data-end="803">
<p data-start="723" data-end="803">Temporal windows expand along the language hierarchy (mSTG → aSTG → IFG → TP).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="804" data-end="870">
<p data-start="806" data-end="870">Nonlinear transformations are necessary; symbolic models fail.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="871" data-end="928">
<p data-start="873" data-end="928">Effect persists even for incorrectly predicted words.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="929" data-end="1040">
<p data-start="931" data-end="1040">Suggests human language processing is a recursive, hierarchical, multi-time-scale refinement similar to LLMs.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1042" data-end="1072">I provided a detailed summary.</p>
