</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{abstract}
This essay develops a unified theoretical model of cortical computation based on
Recursive Semantic Vector Processing (RSVP), a relativistic field-theoretic
framework in which cognition arises from the interaction of scalar, vector, and
entropy fields. We propose that cortical columns implement nonlinear
transformations of these fields via </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplistwistors}, local operators that
generalize the complex-analytic amplitwist to high-dimensional semantic spaces.
Temporal evolution is driven by the TARTAN recursion engine
(Trajectory-Aware Recursive Tiling with Annotated Noise), while global
synchronization occurs through cymatic resonance modes---stationary eigenmodes
of the cortical sheet demonstrated in ultrafast fMRI.
The resulting system describes cortical computation as
recursive, resonance-gated field dynamics, aligning with high-temporal-resolution
ECoG evidence showing hierarchical temporal processing analogous to layered
transformations in large language models.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{abstract}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Introduction}

Cortical computation displays a profound temporal hierarchy: sensory responses
emerge within tens of milliseconds, whereas semantic integration unfolds across
hundreds. Recent electrocorticography (ECoG) studies reveal that deeper layers
of large language models (LLMs) map onto later neural latencies during natural
language comprehension. This finding suggests that the brain, like LLMs, refines
representations through recursive sequences of nonlinear transformations.

The present work interprets this hierarchy through the
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum} (RSVP), a field-theoretic description
of cognition. In RSVP, knowledge states correspond to the configuration of
three interacting fields: a scalar semantic potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, a vector flow
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> encoding directed inference, and an entropy field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> regulating
uncertainty. These fields evolve under coupled nonlinear dynamics.

We propose that cortical columns instantiate </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplistwistors},
nonlinear local operators that modify RSVP fields through amplification,
rotation, and projection. TARTAN (Trajectory-Aware Recursive Tiling with
Annotated Noise) implements the recursive update of these fields, while
cymatic resonance---standing eigenmodes of the cortical sheet---provides global
temporal synchronization. The combined system defines cortical computation as a
recursive field process modulated by global resonance structure.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{RSVP: Relativistic Field Foundations}

RSVP models cognition on a semantic manifold endowed with three coupled fields:
a scalar potential </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{x},t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, a vector field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{x},t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and an
entropy field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{x},t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Their dynamics obey:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{align}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>= c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>
+ c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v})
- c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>
+ </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
