Here </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote structured stochastic sources
interpretable as prediction error. The fields collectively define a
continuous, relativistic substrate of meaning in which local interactions
propagate through nonlinear couplings.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{TARTAN: The Recursive Update Engine}

While RSVP defines the semantic substrate, cognition requires recursive
computation. TARTAN (Trajectory-Aware Recursive Tiling with Annotated Noise)
implements this recursion. Its operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> updates the RSVP field
configuration from time </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> by:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}[noitemsep]
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> applying multiscale tiling of the field,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> injecting annotated noise to regularize inference,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> smoothing temporal trajectories,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and composing amplistwistor transformations across scales.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Thus TARTAN serves as the dynamical engine that transforms RSVP fields through
context-aware recursion, analogous to recurrent unrolling or depth-wise
application of transformer blocks in LLMs.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Amplistwistors: Local Nonlinear Transformations}

Amplistwistors generalize Needham's amplitwist (the derivative as a local
amplification and rotation) to high-dimensional field manifolds.
An amplistwistor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> consists of three nonlinear maps:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A} = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> modulates amplitude, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> enacts rotations or shears in
semantic space, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> projects onto low-dimensional subspaces or
entropy-minimizing manifolds. A single update takes the form

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{k+1} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span> = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v},S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Recursively composed, amplistwistors form
universal approximators, satisfying the conditions of classical superposition
theorems. They correspond to the fundamental computational unit of cortical
columns in this framework.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Cymatic Resonance: Global Temporal Structure}

Recent ultrafast fMRI experiments reveal that the cortex supports
macroscale stationary eigenmodes---standing waves of neural activity described
by

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{r},t) = u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{r})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m t}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

These modes synchronize distant regions without requiring direct propagating
signals. Under anesthesia, mode count and frequency decrease, consistent with
observed reductions in conscious processing.

We interpret these eigenmodes as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{cymatic resonance}, providing temporal
gating for TARTAN updates. The global resonance field is

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
R(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{r},t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>M c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{r}) e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{i </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m t},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

which modulates the timing and gain of local amplistwistor cascades.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Unified Dynamics: Recursive, Resonance-Gated Fields}

The combined RSVP--TARTAN--amplistwistor--resonance system yields:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span>}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> t}
= </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>M
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Big</span></span><span>( </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Big</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\odot</span></span><span>
u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{r}) e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m t}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This equation captures cortical computation as recursive field evolution:
local nonlinear transformations, globally aligned by eigenmode structure.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Empirical Alignment}

ECoG studies show that early LLM layers correlate with early neural latencies,
and deeper layers correlate with responses up to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> 500</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> ms later. This
matches the temporal unfolding expected from recursive amplistwistor cascades
modulated by slow eigenmodes in high-order regions such as IFG and TP.

Ultrafast fMRI confirms the presence of standing resonant modes enabling
synchronization across distant cortical sites. These modes match the predicted
cymatic resonance structure required for global alignment of recursive local
transformations.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Conclusion}

Viewing cortical computation through recursive field dynamics unifies
observations from neuroscience, artificial intelligence, and nonlinear
dynamics. Cortical columns act as amplistwistor cascades, transforming RSVP
fields through TARTAN recursion, while cymatic resonance establishes global
temporal structure. This framework offers a coherent account of the deep
temporal hierarchy in neural language processing and provides avenues for new
neurocomputational architectures that exploit resonance-gated recursion.

