</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% Part 2: Mathematical Analysis of the RSVP Field Equations</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ---------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Well-Posedness of the Tri-Field System}

To establish the mathematical legitimacy of the field-theoretic model, it is
necessary to show that the coupled evolution equations for </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> admit well-defined solutions. The objective is to demonstrate that the system
constitutes a well-posed initial-value problem in the sense of Hadamard: a
solution exists, is unique, and depends continuously on the initial data.
Throughout this section we impose the assumption that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is compact,
connected, and without boundary, ensuring that elliptic operators such as
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> have discrete spectra and that Sobolev embeddings
are compact.

We begin by rewriting the tri-field dynamics concisely as

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{equation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> t} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{equation}

where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}, S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a linear differential
operator defined by

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}, S) =
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>5 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>
- c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>9 S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the nonlinear operator

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)
=
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}) - c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>4 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> - c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>6 S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>7 |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}| + c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>8 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

The stochastic forcing </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> consists of square-integrable fields satisfying
the usual martingale conditions.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Local Existence and Uniqueness}

We now prove a local existence theorem for the system.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k &gt; n/2 + 1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
Then there exists a time </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T &gt; 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and a unique solution
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> C([0,T], H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}))</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to the tri-field system.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
The proof proceeds by applying the Picard iteration argument to the mild
form of the equation. We write

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t) = e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0
+ </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(t-s)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(s))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> ds
+ </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(t-s)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span>(s)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> ds.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

The operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a contraction on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> due to
the smoothing properties of the heat semigroup and the boundedness of the curl
term on compact manifolds. The nonlinear operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is locally
Lipschitz in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> because multiplication and composition are
bounded operations in Sobolev spaces of order </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k &gt; n/2 + 1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Thus the mapping
defined by the right-hand side is a contraction for sufficiently small </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
establishing existence and uniqueness.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Global Existence via Energy Estimates}

Local solutions can be extended globally in time if uniform a priori bounds
can be established. We construct an energy functional

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
E(t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>S(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Differentiating and applying standard Sobolev inequalities yields

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{dE}{dt}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span>
- </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span> E(t) + C E(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{3/2} + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span>(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span> &gt; 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> depends on the decay terms </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>9</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}
Suppose the initial data </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> satisfies </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E(0) &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
Then for sufficiently small nonlinear coefficients </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2, c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>4, c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>7, c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>8</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, or
under a smallness assumption on initial data, the solution to the tri-field
system exists for all </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
The differential inequality above is of Riccati type. If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C E(0)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{1/2} &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
then </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>E(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> remains uniformly bounded for all time. Standard continuation
arguments extend the local solution globally.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

This establishes the mathematical well-posedness of the field substrate.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Energy Functionals and Semantic Stability}

Energy methods not only establish global existence but also allow the definition
of notions such as semantic stability. We define a generalized Lyapunov
functional

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) =
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>) d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i &gt; 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the Riemannian measure.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proposition}
If the entropy coupling coefficients satisfy </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 &gt; 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>9 &gt; 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, then
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a Lyapunov functional: </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> in expectation.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proposition}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Differentiating under the integral sign and using the field equations produces
terms of the form </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>- c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>- c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>9 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, both
nonpositive. The remaining terms integrate to zero due to divergence theorems on
compact manifolds.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

Thus the entropy field acts to stabilize the dynamics by damping extreme
deviations in both </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Invariant Sets and Attractor Structure}

The existence of a Lyapunov functional implies that solutions flow toward
invariant sets of reduced energy. Let

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mid</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = 0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}
The set </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> consists of the field configurations satisfying:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> = 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v} = 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S = 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{theorem}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
The only nonpositive contributions to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> arise from entropy
damping terms. Equality requires both </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S=0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}=0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
eliminating divergence-driven entropy production. The remaining curl and
gradient terms follow from the structure of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

The invariant set corresponds to “predictive silence’’ states in which semantic
potential is harmonic, flows are divergence-free, and uncertainty vanishes.

This completes the mathematical foundation for the RSVP field substrate.

