</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% Part 3: Amplistwistors and Universal Approximation</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% ---------------------------------------------------------------------</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Amplistwistors as Local Nonlinear Operators}

The recursive structure of cortical computation requires local operators capable
of shaping the tri-field system through amplification, rotation, projection, and
nonlinear mixing. Motivated by the geometric derivative formalism of complex
analysis—where the derivative of a holomorphic function acts as an
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplitwist}, simultaneously scaling and rotating infinitesimal vectors—we
generalize this notion to a class of operators defined on the semantic field
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}, S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

An </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplistwistor} is defined as a smooth map

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}: H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

having the decomposition

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where each component is defined as follows.

The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplitude operator} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a positive, smooth functional

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>: H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{&gt;0}),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

that rescales field magnitudes according to semantic salience or contextual gain.

The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{twist operator} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a smooth mapping

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>: H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{SO}(d),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

acting pointwise on the field representation embedded in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
This operator generalizes the notion of a local rotation or shear in
representation space.

Finally, the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{projection operator} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a smooth, idempotent map

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>,
