compact subsets of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> arbitrarily well. The operators
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> in amplistwistors play analogous roles to
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i, w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i, b</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, but in infinite-dimensional function spaces.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Density Lemma}

We begin with a key lemma.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{lemma}
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subset</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be compact and convex. Suppose the projection
operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> has dense range in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and suppose </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> spans
a dense subset of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{SO}(d)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> varies over </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Then finite
