</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\date</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\today</span></span><span>}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{document}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\maketitle</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{abstract}
This work develops a unified mathematical framework for cortical computation
based on a field-theoretic model comprising a scalar potential field,
a vector flow field, and an entropy field, collectively termed the
Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP). The dynamics of these fields are
augmented by a recursive update operator derived from trajectory-aware tiling
and annotated noise (TARTAN), local nonlinear transformations implemented by
amplistwistors, and global synchronization mediated by stationary eigenmodes of
the cortical manifold, a phenomenon known as cymatic resonance. The resulting
formalism describes cortical information processing as a resonance-gated
recursive dynamical system on a curved Riemannian manifold. The mathematical
analysis includes well-posedness of the PDE system, spectral theory,
universal approximation theorems, stochastic regularity, and long-time
stability. Empirical connections are drawn to electrocorticography,
ultrafast fMRI, MEG/EEG frequency bands, and hierarchical temporal behavior
observed in human language processing. The model predicts mode-specific
impairments, cross-frequency coupling signatures, and offers implications for
computational neuroscience, control theory, and biologically inspired
artificial intelligence.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{abstract}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\newpage</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%=====================================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% SECTION 1 — INTRODUCTION</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%=====================================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Introduction}

Cortical computation exhibits a hierarchical temporal structure spanning
multiple scales, from fast sensory encoding to slow semantic integration.
Neural recordings consistently reveal oscillatory patterns, long-range
synchronization, nonlinear population dynamics, and structured variability.
Recent work in electrocorticography has demonstrated that activity in higher
cortical areas unfolds over extended temporal windows, with deeper processing
corresponding to longer latencies. Ultrafast functional MRI has further shown
that large-scale cortical activity can be decomposed into stationary eigenmodes
determined by the geometry of the cortical surface.

These observations suggest that cortical function emerges from the interaction
of three key features: distributed field-like activity, nonlinear local
transformations, and globally coordinated oscillatory modes. The present work
develops a mathematical framework that unifies these principles through the
interaction of (i) a triplet of coupled fields defined on a Riemannian
manifold, (ii) a recursive update operator incorporating multiscale spatial
tiling and stochastic perturbations, (iii) nonlinear amplistwistor operators
generalizing complex derivatives to high-dimensional fields, and (iv) spectral
eigenmodes of the cortical surface that confer global synchronization through
damped oscillatory dynamics.

The resulting theory models cortical computation as a resonance-gated recursive
engine operating on a geometric substrate. Recursive updates produce the
layered structure characteristic of hierarchical processing, while eigenmodes
govern the temporal unfolding of these layers. The analysis reveals conditions
for well-posedness, regularity, stability, universality of function
approximation, and explicit spectral behavior. This provides a rigorous
foundation for interpreting cortical computation as a coupled
geometric–spectral–nonlinear system.

The remainder of this paper proceeds as follows. Section 2 formalizes the
cortical manifold and its geometric structure. Section 3 introduces the RSVP
field equations. Section 4 develops the functional-analytic framework and proves
well-posedness of the system. Subsequent sections introduce amplistwistors,
universal approximation results, spectral theory, resonance-gated recursion,
stochastic dynamics, empirical predictions, computational methods, and
theoretical implications.

