</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% SECTION 2 — GEOMETRIC STRUCTURE OF THE CORTEX</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%=====================================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Geometric Structure of the Cortical Manifold}

The cortical surface may be modeled as a compact, connected, oriented
two-dimensional Riemannian manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}, g)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> smoothly embedded in
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>3</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The Riemannian metric </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is induced by the embedding, with
volume form </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{vol}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sqrt</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\det</span></span><span> g}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> dx</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1 dx</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> in local coordinates.
The manifold is characterized by a number of geometric quantities vital for the
analysis of partial differential equations and spectral operators.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Curvature and Regularity}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the Gaussian curvature. For the human cortical sheet, empirical
measurements show that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> varies within a bounded range:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> K(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span>} &lt; 0 &lt; K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> due to the characteristic folding patterns of
gyri and sulci. We assume further that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> has bounded derivatives of all
orders, ensuring the smoothness of geometric operators.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Injectivity Radius}

The injectivity radius </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{inj}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is defined as the largest </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>r
&gt; 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that the exponential map </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exp</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a diffeomorphism on the open ball
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>r(0) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subset</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for all </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Anatomically,
folding imposes constraints, but </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{inj}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}) &gt; 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> always holds
for compact manifolds. This lower bound is crucial for the validity of normal
coordinate expansions and the derivation of Sobolev inequalities.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Sobolev Constants and Functional Inequalities}

The manifold admits standard Sobolev constants </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{S,k,p}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> satisfying
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> u </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span>
C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{S,k,p} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> u </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{W</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{k,p}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
whenever the Sobolev embedding </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>W</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{k,p}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\hookrightarrow</span></span><span>
L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>q(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> holds. Since </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dim</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M} = 2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, we have
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
W</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{1,2}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\hookrightarrow</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>p(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{for all } p &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
and the critical embedding
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
W</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{1,2}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\hookrightarrow</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
fails but admits a Moser–Trudinger inequality. These estimates will be used in
Sections 4 and 6 for regularity and global existence of solutions.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Boundary Conditions}

Although the cortex is topologically close to a genus-</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> surface without
boundary, in the PDE analysis one may impose either:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Intrinsic formulation:} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> has no boundary.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Computational formulation:} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is approximated by a
mesh with boundary, requiring boundary conditions.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

In this work we adopt the boundaryless formulation consistent with anatomical
geometry, but the theory extends naturally to Neumann or Robin boundary
conditions.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%=====================================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% SECTION 3 — RSVP FIELD EQUATIONS</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%=====================================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{RSVP Field Equations}

The Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum (RSVP) describes cortical computation as
the evolution of three interacting fields defined on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}, g)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a scalar potential field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> [0,T] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a vector flow field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v} : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> [0,T] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> an entropy or uncertainty field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> [0,T] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>+</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

These fields satisfy a system of coupled nonlinear partial differential
equations encoding diffusion, advection, nonlinearity, and dissipation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Field Equations}

We define the RSVP system as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{align}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>=
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>
+
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>( </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>)
-
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>
+
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:rsvp-Phi}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>=
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>4 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>
+
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>5 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{curl}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v})
-
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>6 S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}
+
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:rsvp-v}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t S
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>=
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>7 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\abs</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}}
+
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>8 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2
-
c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>9 S
+
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:rsvp-S}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{align}

Here </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the Laplace–Beltrami operator,
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{curl}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denotes the intrinsic vector curl (well-defined on
2-manifolds), and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are noise processes.
The coefficients </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>, c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>9</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are positive constants governing diffusion,
gain, damping, and coupling.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Biological Interpretation of Terms}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Diffusion (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).}
Represents spatial smoothing of activity across the cortical sheet, comparable
to classical neural field models.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Divergence coupling (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).}
Captures the interaction between scalar activation and local expansion or
compression of flow, analogous to gain modulation or modulation of local
population firing rates.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Entropy suppression (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).}
Models inhibitory effects of uncertainty or noise on activation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Gradient excitation (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>4 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).}
Produces flows driven by gradients of activation, consistent with
gradient-following dynamics in distributed networks.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Vorticity (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>5 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{curl}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).}
Generates local rotational patterns, analogous to cortical traveling waves and
spiral dynamics observed experimentally.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Entropy loading (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>7 |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).}
Identifies divergence as a source of uncertainty, consistent with variable
population responses during expansion or contraction of neural assemblies.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Activation-driven entropy (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>8 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).}
Reflects increased uncertainty due to strong activation, modeling the
nonlinearity of cortical responses.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Entropy decay (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>9 S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).}
Represents natural dissipation of uncertainty.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Dimensional Analysis}

Let time have dimension </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>[T]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, space </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>[L]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and field units be arbitrary.
The terms in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eqref</span></span><span>{eq:rsvp-Phi} give:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>] = T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1} [</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>], </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> [</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>] = L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-2} [</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>],
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
so
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
[c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1] = L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Similarly,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
[c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2] = T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> [c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3] = T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> [c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>4] = L T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span>
[c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>5] = T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> [c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>6] = T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
[c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>7] = T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> [c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>8] = T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> [c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>9] = T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Thus all coefficients correspond to characteristic time scales of tens to
hundreds of milliseconds, consistent with cortical integration windows.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Noise Processes}

We assume </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\vb</span></span><span>{v}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-valued stochastic
processes with:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> zero mean,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> covariance kernel </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>Q(x,y)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> smooth on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> Itô interpretation for stochastic integrals,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> pathwise uniqueness,
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> finite quadratic variation.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

A precise formulation appears in Section 5, where we construct the stochastic
integrals and prove well-posedness for the RSVP system under biologically
reasonable assumptions.

