</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% SECTION 7 — AMPLISTWISTORS: FORMAL DEFINITION AND EXAMPLES</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%=====================================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Amplistwistors: Local Nonlinear Transformation Operators}

We now formalize the notion of an </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplistwistor} acting on fields
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. These operators generalize the classical
``amplitwist'' of complex analysis to high-dimensional nonlinear maps on a
Riemannian manifold, designed to capture local geometric transformations in
cortical computation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Definition}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote a local field
value at </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. An </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplistwistor} is a triple
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A} = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span> : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>+</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a smooth amplitude modulator.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span> : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> SO(d)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a smooth map assigning an orthogonal
    transformation to each field value.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span> : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a smooth projection or
    normalization map.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

The amplistwistor acts pointwise as:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)(x)
=
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

A cascade of amplistwistors is defined by composition:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(n)} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Examples}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Example 1: Gain Modulation.}
Let
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exp</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>( </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>),</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = I</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>d,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This implements nonlinear gain modulation proportional to local field magnitude.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Example 2: Rotational Alignment.}
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> rotate </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> toward a preferred direction </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>u </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\theta</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>, u</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> small.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Example 3: Sparsifying Projection.}
Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> be a soft-thresholding operator:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
[</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)]</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{sign}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span>(|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i|- </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>, 0).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This models local sparse coding or winner-take-all competition.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Biological Interpretation}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> corresponds to gain modulation, divisive normalization,
    or neuromodulatory control.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> models orientation selectivity, phase preference, or rotationally
    symmetric feature transformations.
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> corresponds to nonlinear thresholding, sparsification,
    lateral inhibition, or local normalization.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

These operators mimic microcircuit transformations performed within a cortical
column, and their composition approximates arbitrarily complex local
computations.

</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%=====================================================================</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% SECTION 8 — NONLINEAR ANALYSIS OF AMPLISTWISTORS</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%=====================================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Nonlinear Analysis of Amplistwistors}

We study continuity, bounds, regularity, and contraction properties of
amplistwistors acting on Sobolev spaces.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Boundedness on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{lemma}
If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are smooth with bounded derivatives on bounded sets,
then
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A} : H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M};</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M};</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
is locally bounded.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{lemma}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Smooth functions with bounded derivatives preserve </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> norms under
composition. Using the chain rule for Sobolev maps and compactness of
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> yields the result.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{proof}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Lipschitz Continuity}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{lemma}[Local Lipschitz Property]
For any bounded set </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>B</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>R </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subset</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, there exists </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>R&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such
that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1) - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span>
L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>R
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{lemma}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{proof}
Differentiating </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> using the product rule and chain rule:
