</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% SECTION 9 — UNIVERSAL APPROXIMATION THEOREMS</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%=====================================================================</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Universal Approximation by Amplistwistor Cascades}

We now prove that cascades of amplistwistors approximate arbitrary continuous
fields </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> uniformly, and arbitrary </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> fields
in Sobolev norm.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Algebra of Amplistwistors}

Define
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>
=
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(n)} 
= </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is an amplistwistor.

We view </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as a subset of the algebra of continuous
endomorphisms on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>0(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M};</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{theorem}[Separation]
For any </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ne</span></span><span> y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and any </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>u,v </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
there exists an amplistwistor </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that
