<p data-start="1670" data-end="1822">I am prepared to continue immediately, but before I begin generating <strong data-start="1739" data-end="1789">~12–15 pages of dense spectral theory in LaTeX</strong>, I need to ask you one question:</p>
<hr data-start="1824" data-end="1827">
<h1 data-start="1829" data-end="1882"><strong data-start="1831" data-end="1882">Which Spectral Boundary Condition Should I Use?</strong></h1>
<p data-start="1884" data-end="1992">For a cortical surface <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">M</span></span></span></span> (2D manifold embedded in <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="double-struck">R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^3</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8141em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathbb">R</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8141em;"><span style="top: -3.063em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">3</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>), the standard choices are:</p>
<h3 data-start="1994" data-end="2034"><strong data-start="1998" data-end="2032">A. Neumann Boundary Conditions</strong></h3>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mtext>on&nbsp;</mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi mathvariant="script">M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}=0 \quad\text{on }\partial\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 2.0574em; vertical-align: -0.686em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mopen nulldelimiter"></span><span class="mfrac"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 1.3714em;"><span style="top: -2.314em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">∂</span><span class="mord mathnormal">n</span></span></span><span style="top: -3.23em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="frac-line" style="border-bottom-width: 0.04em;"></span></span><span style="top: -3.677em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">∂</span><span class="mord mathnormal">u</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.686em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose nulldelimiter"></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class="mord">0</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 1em;"></span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">on&nbsp;</span></span><span class="mord" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">∂</span><span class="mord mathcal">M</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="2110" data-end="2153">Meaning zero normal derivative (no flux).</p>
<p data-start="2155" data-end="2170">Biologically:</p>
<ul data-start="2171" data-end="2315">
<li data-start="2171" data-end="2207">
<p data-start="2173" data-end="2207">consistent with continuous sheet</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2208" data-end="2259">
<p data-start="2210" data-end="2259">appropriate for approximating cortical surfaces</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2260" data-end="2315">
<p data-start="2262" data-end="2315">used in many eigenmode studies (e.g., Roberts et al.)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="2317" data-end="2359"><strong data-start="2321" data-end="2357">B. Dirichlet Boundary Conditions</strong></h3>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width="1em"></mspace><mtext>on&nbsp;</mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi mathvariant="script">M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=0 \quad\text{on }\partial\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">u</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class="mord">0</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 1em;"></span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">on&nbsp;</span></span><span class="mord" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">∂</span><span class="mord mathcal">M</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="2407" data-end="2450">Less realistic, but mathematically simpler.</p>
<h3 data-start="2452" data-end="2493"><strong data-start="2456" data-end="2491">C. Closed Surface (No Boundary)</strong></h3>
<p data-start="2494" data-end="2619"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi mathvariant="script">M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial \mathcal{M}=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class="mord" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">∂</span><span class="mord mathcal">M</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6633em; vertical-align: -0.0817em;"></span><span class="mord amsrm">∅</span></span></span></span>.<br data-start="2531" data-end="2534">
This is common if we treat cortex as a closed genus-2 surface or use inflated meshes.</p>
<p data-start="2621" data-end="2641">This choice changes:</p>
<ul data-start="2643" data-end="2773">
<li data-start="2643" data-end="2665">
<p data-start="2645" data-end="2665">eigenvalue spacing</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2666" data-end="2694">
<p data-start="2668" data-end="2694">eigenfunction regularity</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2695" data-end="2744">
<p data-start="2697" data-end="2744">whether constant functions are eigenfunctions</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2745" data-end="2773">
<p data-start="2747" data-end="2773">behavior of resonant modes</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2775" data-end="2778">
<h1 data-start="2780" data-end="2847"><strong data-start="2782" data-end="2847">Which one should I use for the official version of the paper?</strong></h1>
<p data-start="2849" data-end="2861"><strong data-start="2849" data-end="2861">Options:</strong></p>
<p data-start="2863" data-end="2945">A. Neumann<br data-start="2873" data-end="2876">
B. Dirichlet<br data-start="2888" data-end="2891">
C. Closed surface (no boundary)<br data-start="2922" data-end="2925">
D. Mixed (explain)</p>
