<p data-start="3381" data-end="3493">where $\mathbf{e}$ encodes the cross-product direction. Projection onto mode $u_m$ yields integrals of the form:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{(\Phi\times\mathbf{v})}_{m,j,k}
