<p data-start="4752" data-end="4847">These govern the baseline dynamics around which nonlinear coupling induces recursive structure.</p>
<p data-start="4849" data-end="4892">\subsection{Nonlinear Resonance Conditions}</p>
<p data-start="4894" data-end="4999">From the quadratic terms in (\ref{17-phi-modal})–(\ref{17-S-modal}), nonlinear resonances occur whenever:</p>
<p data-start="5001" data-end="5151">\begin{equation}<br>
\omega_m \approx \omega_j \pm \omega_k,<br>
\label{17-res-cond}<br>
\end{equation}<br>
with nonzero coupling coefficients $N_{mjk}$ or $C_{mjk}$.</p>
<p data-start="5153" data-end="5206">This gives rise to three primary resonance manifolds:</p>
<p data-start="5208" data-end="5433">\begin{enumerate}<br>
\item \textbf{Sum resonance}: $\omega_m = \omega_j + \omega_k$.<br>
\item \textbf{Difference resonance}: $\omega_m = |\omega_j - \omega_k|$.<br>
\item \textbf{Self-resonance}: $2\omega_j = \omega_m$.<br>
\end{enumerate}</p>
<p data-start="5435" data-end="5637">Because eigenfrequencies increase as $\omega_m\sim m^{1/2}$, low-index modes have comparatively large spacing, while high-index modes have dense resonance conditions, enabling complex spectral cascades.</p>
<p data-start="5639" data-end="5707">\subsection{Recursive Amplification Through Nonlinear Mode Coupling}</p>
<p data-start="5709" data-end="5712">Let</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}(t) = (\{\phi_m(t)\},\{\mathbf{v}_m(t)\},\{s_m(t)\}) 
