This section expands the theory into nonlinear stability, bifurcations, and selective activation of resonant eigenmodes, incorporating rigorous mathematical results and their cognitive interpretations.</p>
<hr data-start="349" data-end="352">
<p data-start="354" data-end="426">\section{Nonlinear Stability, Bifurcations, and Mode-Selective Dynamics}</p>
<p data-start="428" data-end="1006">The previous section demonstrated that the RSVP system, when projected onto the eigenbasis of the Laplace–Beltrami operator, naturally decomposes into a small set of resonant modes responsible for sustained recursive oscillations and an exponentially decaying remainder. In this section, we analyze the nonlinear stability properties of these resonant subsystems, characterize bifurcations that give rise to new time scales and oscillatory patterns, and formalize how the brain selectively activates particular eigenmodes depending on task demands, attention, or cognitive load.</p>
<p data-start="1008" data-end="1066">\subsection{Nonlinear Stability of the Resonant Subsystem}</p>
<p data-start="1068" data-end="1292">Let $S$ be the resonant modal subset identified previously. The evolution restricted to $S$ is governed by the finite-dimensional nonlinear system<br>
\begin{equation}<br>
\partial_t \mathbf{a}_S(t)<br>
= \mathcal{L}_S \mathbf{a}_S(t)</p>
<ul data-start="1293" data-end="1445">
<li data-start="1293" data-end="1445">
<p data-start="1295" data-end="1445">\mathcal{N}_S(\mathbf{a}_S(t)),<br>
\label{18-res-subsystem}<br>
\end{equation}<br>
where $\mathcal{L}_S = P_S \mathcal{L}$ and $\mathcal{N}_S = P_S \mathcal{N}$.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1447" data-end="1830">The stability of the equilibrium $\mathbf{a}_S=0$ determines whether recursive semantic dynamics can sustain themselves or collapse to triviality. Because the linear part is diagonal, stability depends on the signs of the eigenvalues $-c_1\lambda_m$, $-c_5\lambda_m$, and $-c_9$. Since $c_1,c_5,c_9&gt;0$ and $\lambda_m&gt;0$ for $m\ge1$, the trivial equilibrium is always linearly stable.</p>
<p data-start="1832" data-end="1995">However, nonlinear interactions can destabilize this equilibrium by producing effective negative damping through resonant energy transfer. We formalize this below.</p>
<p data-start="1997" data-end="2114">\begin{lemma}[Effective Damping and Nonlinear Destabilization]<br>
Let $m \in S$ and assume the quadratic terms satisfy</p>
