<p data-start="4984" data-end="5045">where $\kappa_{mjk} = c_2 N^{(\Phi\times \mathbf{v})}_{mjk}$.</p>
<p data-start="5047" data-end="5282">\begin{lemma}[Quadratic Frequency Mixing]<br>
If $|\omega_m - \omega_{jk}^{\pm}| &lt; \epsilon$, then $\phi_m$ exhibits a driven oscillatory component at frequency $\omega_{jk}^{\pm}$ with amplitude proportional to $\kappa_{mjk}$.<br>
\end{lemma}</p>
<p data-start="5284" data-end="5440">\begin{proof}<br>
Follows from standard perturbation theory for weakly nonlinear oscillators, treating the coupling as an external periodic forcing.<br>
\end{proof}</p>
<p data-start="5442" data-end="5724">This explains the experimentally observed cross-frequency coupling phenomena:<br>
\begin{itemize}<br>
\item theta–gamma coupling in working memory tasks,<br data-start="5587" data-end="5590">
\item delta–beta interactions during long-sentence integration,<br data-start="5653" data-end="5656">
\item phase–amplitude coupling in semantic prediction.<br>
\end{itemize}</p>
<p data-start="5726" data-end="5773">\subsection{Selective Activation of Eigenmodes}</p>
<p data-start="5775" data-end="5936">We now formalize a principle central to RSVP and TARTAN: only particular eigenmodes activate under sensory or cognitive input, forming sparse resonant ensembles.</p>
<p data-start="5938" data-end="6051">Let $I(t)$ represent an external input field, projected spectrally as $I_m(t)$. The forced modal dynamics become:</p>
