="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="6439" data-end="6533">Thus modes with lower $\lambda_m$ (larger scale) are preferentially excited.<br>
\end{proposition}</p>
<p data-start="6535" data-end="6726">\begin{proof}<br>
Linearize the forced system and solve the resulting Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process. The stationary variance is inversely proportional to the damping rate $c_1\lambda_m$.<br>
\end{proof}</p>
<p data-start="6728" data-end="6760">This proves mathematically that:</p>
<ol data-start="6762" data-end="6959">
<li data-start="6762" data-end="6831">
<p data-start="6765" data-end="6831">large-scale cortical eigenmodes dominate resting-state activity,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6832" data-end="6898">
<p data-start="6835" data-end="6898">fine-scale modes require strong or precise input to activate,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6899" data-end="6959">
<p data-start="6902" data-end="6959">temporal hierarchies emerge from scale-dependent damping.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="6961" data-end="7031">\subsection{Nonlinear Gain Control and Suppression of Competing Modes}</p>
<p data-start="7033" data-end="7255">The entropy field $S$ couples to $\Phi$ and $\mathbf{v}$ via $-c_3 S\Phi$ and $-c_6 S\mathbf{v}$. Because $S$ increases with divergence of $\mathbf{v}$ or with $\Phi^2$, we obtain nonlinear inhibitory feedback of the form:</p>
