<p data-start="5620" data-end="5809">Thus modes with small $\lambda_m$ (large spatial scale) receive disproportionately large stochastic energy, explaining why low-frequency cortical eigenmodes dominate resting-state activity.</p>
<p data-start="5811" data-end="5842">We now state a stronger result:</p>
<p data-start="5844" data-end="6013">\begin{theorem}[Noise-Induced Resonance]<br>
Let $I_m(t)$ be a weak deterministic input oscillating near $\omega_m = \sqrt{\lambda_m}$. Then the expected amplitude satisfies</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="double-struck">E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}|a_m(t)|^2
\approx 
\frac{|I_m|^2}{(\lambda_m - \omega^2)^2}
+
