<p data-start="221" data-end="301">\section{Averaging Theory and Multiple Timescales in Resonant Cortical Dynamics}</p>
<p data-start="303" data-end="1219">The reduction to an invariant resonant manifold, established in the previous section, reveals the RSVP–TARTAN system as a finite-dimensional nonlinear oscillator whose components evolve on multiple intrinsic timescales. The origin of these timescales lies in the eigenvalue distribution of the Laplace–Beltrami operator, the nonlinear couplings induced by amplistwistor actions, and the stochastic perturbations that modulate the amplitudes of the resonant modes. The purpose of the present section is to develop a systematic theory of timescale separation and averaging for these dynamics, allowing one to extract effective equations that govern the long-term evolution of semantic fields. Such reductions are indispensable for understanding how large-scale cognitive processes, evolving on the order of hundreds of milliseconds to several seconds, emerge from microscopic neural interactions and cortical geometry.</p>
<p data-start="1221" data-end="1392">To establish the formal decomposition, consider the modal representation of the field on the resonant manifold, where a finite set of modes satisfies equations of the form</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_t a_m(t) = f_m(a(t)) + \epsilon\,g_m(a(t),t),</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">∂</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2806em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0556em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">t</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span>