<p data-start="4877" data-end="5233">where $\sigma_m$ is the diffusion coefficient obtained from the projection of the noise field. The resulting amplitude equation becomes a stochastic differential equation whose drift term is the averaged nonlinear vector field and whose diffusion term arises from the interaction between noise and the resonant structure. The averaged system takes the form</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}r_m = \epsilon \bar{R}_m(r)\,\mathrm{d}t 
+ \epsilon\,\Sigma_m(r)\,\mathrm{d}W(t),</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8444em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord mathrm">d</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">r</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0278em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.0701em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">ϵ</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord accent"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8201em;"><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span></span><span style="top: -3.2523em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="accent-body" style="left: -0.1667em;"><span class="mord">ˉ</span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0077em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">r</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathrm">d</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">ϵ</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">Σ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">r</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathrm">d</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">W</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mpunct">,</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="5333" data-end="5719">where $\Sigma_m(r)$ is an effective noise term reflecting the way in which stochastic fluctuations, once averaged over rapid oscillations, contribute to the long-term amplitude dynamics. This yields a quantitative account of stochastic resonance, where noise selectively enhances those modes that are close to resonance conditions and suppresses modes for which no such conditions hold.</p>
<p data-start="5721" data-end="6490">On the invariant manifold derived in Section 20, the slow variables characterize the evolution of semantic content or cognitive state, while the fast variables correspond to oscillatory microstructure necessary for stability but irrelevant at macroscopic scales. Averaging eliminates the latter, yielding a reduced system of ordinary differential equations capturing the evolution of semantic fields over behaviorally relevant durations. Because these reduced equations retain contributions only from resonant mode couplings, the slow dynamics inherit a structure uniquely determined by the geometry of the cortical manifold. This connects the topology, curvature, and Laplace–Beltrami spectrum of the cortical sheet with the temporal structure of cognitive processing.</p>
<p data-start="6492" data-end="7175">The separation of timescales also clarifies the nature of recursion in the TARTAN formalism. Recursive updates correspond to slow transformations of amplitude and phase patterns on the resonant manifold. Each recursion step refines predictions and internal representations, but the underlying fast oscillatory activity maintains stability, synchrony, and temporal alignment across distant cortical sites. The slow envelope of oscillatory modes encodes the semantic content, while the fast oscillations implement coordination across space. The mathematics of averaging thus provides the formal foundation tying together semantic recursion, cortical eigenmodes, and temporal hierarchy.</p>
<p data-start="7177" data-end="7888">Finally, the multiple-timescale structure gives rise to averaging-induced bifurcations. A parameter regime may be stable in the full unaveraged system, yet unstable in the averaged dynamics. This occurs because averaging suppresses stabilizing high-frequency terms or reinforces specific nonlinear interactions. Such averaging-induced instabilities account for the emergence of slower oscillatory envelopes during cognitive effort, fatigue, or neuromodulatory shifts. They also explain transitions between cognitive states without requiring changes in structural connectivity, since the spectral geometry and nonlinear interactions alone can produce regime changes when modulated by phase-averaged interactions.</p>
<p data-start="7890" data-end="8409">The conclusions of this section emphasize that the RSVP–TARTAN system possesses an inherently hierarchical temporal structure, generated not by architectural imposition but by the intrinsic interplay between geometry, recursion, and nonlinear resonance. Averaging theory illuminates how semantics unfolds on long timescales filtering out rapid fluctuations, how recursive computations accumulate across oscillatory cycles, and how the cortical manifold constrains and shapes the long-term evolution of cognitive states.</p>
