<p data-start="2419" data-end="2822">The first term is the dynamical phase, determined solely by the intrinsic frequency of the mode. The second term is the geometric phase, which depends on the evolution of the state along the invariant manifold. In particular, if the state evolves along a closed curve <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>:</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="script">M</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma: [0,T] \to \mathcal{M}_S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">γ</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">:</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">[</span><span class="mord">0</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mclose">]</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8333em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal">M</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, where <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="script">M</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8333em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal">M</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> is the resonant manifold, the geometric phase accumulated over the loop is</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∮</mo><mi>γ</mi></msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_m(\gamma) = \oint_\gamma \epsilon\,\Gamma_m(a)\,\mathrm{d}t,</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">Φ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">γ</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 2.4081em; vertical-align: -1.0481em;"></span><span class="mop"><span class="mop op-symbol large-op" style="margin-right: 0.44445em; position: relative; top: -0.0011em;">∮</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist"><span style="top: -1.7881em; margin-left: -0.4445em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">γ</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 1.0481em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">ϵ</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">Γ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathrm">d</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mpunct">,</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="2895" data-end="2990">which in general does not vanish even though the dynamical phase is a simple multiple of <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span></span></span></span>.</p>
<p data-start="2992" data-end="3249">To interpret this geometrically, one introduces a connection on the resonant manifold whose connection one-form is given by <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="script">A</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_m(a) = \epsilon\,\Gamma_m(a)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal">A</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">ϵ</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">Γ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>. The geometric phase is then the holonomy of this connection around the loop <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">γ</span></span></span></span>:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∮</mo><mi>γ</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="script">A</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_m(\gamma) = \oint_\gamma \mathcal{A}_m.</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">Φ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">γ</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 2.4081em; vertical-align: -1.0481em;"></span><span class="mop"><span class="mop op-symbol large-op" style="margin-right: 0.44445em; position: relative; top: -0.0011em;">∮</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist"><span style="top: -1.7881em; margin-left: -0.4445em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">γ</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 1.0481em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal">A</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="3301" data-end="3696">The curvature associated with the connection, defined by <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="script">A</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_m = \mathrm{d}\mathcal{A}_m</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8333em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">F</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.1389em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8444em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord mathrm">d</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal">A</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, encapsulates the extent to which closed loops fail to return the system to the same oscillatory phase. Because <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_m(a)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">Γ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> arises from nonlinear mode coupling, the curvature <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_m</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8333em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">F</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.1389em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> is intimately tied to the geometry of cortical eigenmodes and the structure of amplistwistor-induced transformations.</p>
<p data-start="3698" data-end="4497">The existence of a nontrivial geometric phase has profound consequences for semantic processing. Since the slow evolution of resonant modes tracks the accumulation of semantic evidence and context over time, the geometric phase provides an additional, path-dependent variable that encodes aspects of temporal order and long-range dependencies that cannot be captured solely by instantaneous state variables. In many cognitive processes, the meaning of a sentence, phrase, or discourse segment depends critically on the order in which elements appear. The geometric phase provides a continuous analog of this phenomenon: the trajectory of the resonant amplitudes across the cortical manifold induces a rotation in phase space whose magnitude depends on the path taken through semantic configurations.</p>
<p data-start="4499" data-end="5192">To make this precise, consider two different input sequences that lead to the same endpoint in the resonant manifold but traverse different paths. The final state of the amplitudes may coincide, yet the geometric phase may differ, producing distinct neural responses or behavioral outputs. This mathematically formalizes a phenomenon well-documented in psycholinguistics: context and ordering effects that influence comprehension even when the same lexical items appear. The RSVP–TARTAN dynamics capture this through holonomy. The non-vanishing curvature <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_m</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8333em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">F</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.1389em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> of the connection ensures that the accumulated phase around a loop is non-zero unless the mode couplings are trivially integrable.</p>
<p data-start="5194" data-end="6115">The geometric structure also clarifies how the cortex maintains temporal alignment across spatially separated regions. The resonant manifold inherits a natural Riemannian metric from the ambient function space and a connection from the nonlinear coupling terms. Parallel transport along curves in this manifold provides a definition of semantic alignment across time. When the system transports a semantic vector along a path on the manifold, the connection dictates how its orientation changes. The geometric phase is precisely the discrepancy accumulated due to curvature during this transport. In cognitive terms, the brain maintains coherence of meaning by transporting semantic content along the path defined by the unfolding dynamics, and the geometric phase measures the residual displacement introduced by curvature. This displacement corresponds to shifts in expectation, inference, or predictive interpretation.</p>
<p data-start="6117" data-end="6577">In the presence of stochastic fluctuations, the geometric phase becomes a random variable whose distribution reflects both the underlying curvature and the structure of noise. The expectation of the geometric phase captures the mean path-dependent transformation, while higher moments encode variability introduced by fluctuations in the resonant dynamics. Averaging techniques reduce the noisy phase evolution to a stochastic differential equation of the form</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\Phi_m = \langle \Gamma_m(a) \rangle \,\mathrm{d}t 
+ \Sigma_m(a)\,\mathrm{d}W(t),</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8444em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord mathrm">d</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">Φ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">⟨</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">Γ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)⟩</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathrm">d</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">Σ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathrm">d</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">W</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mpunct">,</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="6677" data-end="7038">illustrating that noise influences phase dynamics by broadening the distribution of holonomy. This provides a principled account of how attention, arousal, or neurological disruptions affect the stability of semantic integration over time: increased noise leads to greater dispersion in the geometric phase, producing variability in comprehension or prediction.</p>
<p data-start="7040" data-end="7575">Furthermore, geometric phase phenomena suggest a new interpretation of syntactic and semantic reanalysis. When the trajectory of the system in the resonant manifold encounters curvature that induces a substantial geometric phase shift, the system may need to re-align its semantic configuration to restore coherence. This manifests as reanalysis in language processing, corresponding to well-known ERP signatures such as the P600. The mathematics thus provides a continuum dynamical mechanism underlying discrete linguistic constructs.</p>
<p data-start="7577" data-end="8364">Finally, from an architectural perspective, the geometric phase contributes an essential component to recursion. TARTAN recursion modifies the trajectory on the resonant manifold through multiscale tiling and annotated noise, thereby altering the curvature encountered by the semantic state. Each recursive iteration thus accumulates additional geometric phase, enabling deep contextual integration. In systems where recursion unfolds over many cycles of background oscillation, the total geometric phase may encode complex temporal dependencies. This is analogous to how holonomy in gauge theories encodes global structural information through local interactions, suggesting a unification of cognitive dynamics with geometric principles that extend beyond classical neural field models.</p>
<p data-start="8366" data-end="8875">In summary, the resonant dynamics of the RSVP–TARTAN system naturally give rise to geometric phase and holonomy on the invariant manifold governing long-term semantic evolution. These geometric quantities encode path-dependent temporal structure essential for comprehension, prediction, and cognitive integration. They emerge from nonlinear coupling, cortical geometry, and recursive dynamics, offering a mathematically principled account of temporal binding and semantic coherence across multiple timescales.</p>
