-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="aad31076-db50-4e7d-b2bb-0325779b6cbd" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-1"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="429">Below is <strong data-start="9" data-end="23">Section 23</strong>, written in continuous, formal academic prose and pure LaTeX style, extending the monograph seamlessly. This section develops the information-geometric structure associated with the RSVP–TARTAN resonant manifold, defines semantic curvature as an emergent quantity derived from the nonlinear connection, and establishes the relationship between cortical geometry, recursive inference, and information flow.</p>
<hr data-start="431" data-end="434">
<p data-start="436" data-end="489">\section{Information Geometry and Semantic Curvature}</p>
<p data-start="491" data-end="1352">The preceding sections established the geometric phase and holonomy arising from nonlinear coupling among resonant cortical eigenmodes. The present section develops a complementary information-geometric perspective that formalizes how the RSVP–TARTAN system encodes semantic structure through the differential geometry of the resonant manifold. In particular, the resonant amplitudes define an evolving probabilistic representation, and the nonlinear connection induced by amplistwistor dynamics yields a Riemannian metric closely related to the Fisher information metric. This metric governs the curvature of the manifold, thereby determining the efficiency and stability of recursive inference. The resulting semantic curvature quantifies the degree to which recursive updates amplify, distort, or compress information during the evolution of cognitive state.</p>
<p data-start="1354" data-end="2344">To motivate this construction, consider again the resonant decomposition of the field. At any time <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6151em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span></span></span></span>, the state on the invariant manifold is specified by the vector of modal amplitudes <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a = (a_m)_{m \in S}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose"><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span><span class="mrel mtight">∈</span><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1774em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. These coefficients determine the macroscopic configuration of the field and implicitly define a probability distribution over semantic hypotheses or representational states. Although this distribution is not explicitly encoded as a parametric family, the amplitudes may be interpreted as coordinates on a manifold of latent semantic structures. To equip this manifold with information geometry, one introduces a Riemannian metric <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">g</span></span></span></span> that quantifies the distinguishability of nearby states. A natural choice is the pullback of the Fisher information metric induced by the mapping from modal amplitudes to probability distributions. Concretely, if <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x|a)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mord">∣</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> denotes the distribution over representational features parameterized by <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span></span></span></span>, then the Fisher metric is defined as</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{ij}(a) = \int \frac{\partial \log p(x|a)}{\partial a_i}\, 
\frac{\partial \log p(x|a)}{\partial a_j}\, p(x|a)\,\mathrm{d}x.</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.0361em; vertical-align: -0.2861em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">g</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3117em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0359em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05724em;">ij</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2861em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 2.3991em; vertical-align: -0.9721em;"></span><span class="mop op-symbol large-op" style="margin-right: 0.44445em; position: relative; top: -0.0011em;">∫</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mopen nulldelimiter"></span><span class="mfrac"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 1.427em;"><span style="top: -2.314em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">∂</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3117em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">i</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style="top: -3.23em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="frac-line" style="border-bottom-width: 0.04em;"></span></span><span style="top: -3.677em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">∂</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mop">lo<span style="margin-right: 0.01389em;">g</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mord">∣</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.836em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose nulldelimiter"></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mopen nulldelimiter"></span><span class="mfrac"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 1.427em;"><span style="top: -2.314em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">∂</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3117em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05724em;">j</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2861em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style="top: -3.23em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="frac-line" style="border-bottom-width: 0.04em;"></span></span><span style="top: -3.677em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">∂</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mop">lo<span style="margin-right: 0.01389em;">g</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mord">∣</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.9721em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose nulldelimiter"></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mord">∣</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathrm">d</span><span class="mord mathnormal">x</span><span class="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="2478" data-end="2708">The modality of the mapping <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∣</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a \mapsto p(\cdot|a)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.522em; vertical-align: -0.011em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">↦</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">⋅</span><span class="mord">∣</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> depends on the details of the cortical representation, but for the analysis that follows, it suffices that the mapping be smooth and injective in a neighborhood of the manifold.</p>
<p data-start="2710" data-end="3435">The Riemannian metric induces a Levi-Civita connection, and the curvature associated with this connection provides a quantitative measure of the semantic complexity encoded in the resonant dynamics. If the manifold is flat, recursive updates proceed in a linear, distortion-free manner; if the curvature is nonzero, recursive transformations accumulate nonlinear distortions that manifest as inference amplification, semantic integration, or ambiguity resolution. The curvature tensor <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span></span></span></span> encodes the degree to which parallel transport depends on the path, reflecting the same holonomy structure introduced in Section 22, but now derived from the information-geometric metric rather than the nonlinear dynamical connection.</p>
<p data-start="3437" data-end="3885">To connect semantic curvature to the RSVP–TARTAN equations, one introduces a mapping from the modal amplitudes to the RSVP field configuration <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(\mathbf{r}) = \sum_{m \in S} a_m u_m(\mathbf{r})</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord">Ψ</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathbf">r</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.0771em; vertical-align: -0.3271em;"></span><span class="mop"><span class="mop op-symbol small-op" style="position: relative; top: 0em;">∑</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1786em;"><span style="top: -2.4003em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span><span class="mrel mtight">∈</span><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3271em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">u</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathbf">r</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>. The dynamics on the resonant manifold induce changes in the distribution of field values across the cortical surface, and through this distribution, changes in the induced information metric. The metric on amplitude space may thus be expressed as</p>
