<p data-start="4494" data-end="4907">where <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta g_{ij}(a)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.0361em; vertical-align: -0.2861em;"></span><span class="mord">Δ</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">g</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3117em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0359em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05724em;">ij</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2861em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> encodes contributions from nonlinear interactions, amplistwistor effects, and state-dependent distortions. At leading order, the orthogonality of <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_i</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.5806em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">u</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3117em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">i</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> and <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_j</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7167em; vertical-align: -0.2861em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">u</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3117em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05724em;">j</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2861em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> ensures that the manifold is approximately flat in the modal coordinates. Curvature arises primarily through the higher-order term <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta g_{ij}(a)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.0361em; vertical-align: -0.2861em;"></span><span class="mord">Δ</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">g</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3117em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0359em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.05724em;">ij</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2861em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>, reflecting the nonlinear dependence of the field on the amplitudes.</p>
<p data-start="4909" data-end="5592">A central insight emerges from this structure: semantic curvature is negligible when amplitudes remain small and interactions weak, but grows significantly when the resonant modes engage in strong coupling. This provides a mathematical account of how semantic complexity arises during tasks requiring deep integration, prediction, or inferential bridging. When curvature is large, parallel transport along the manifold yields significant geometric phase, as described previously, and recursive updates amplify these distortions. Thus curvature and geometric phase are two manifestations of the same underlying structure: one local and infinitesimal, the other global and accumulated.</p>
<p data-start="5594" data-end="6215">The curvature tensor also determines the sensitivity of recursive inference to perturbations. In regions of high curvature, small changes in input produce disproportionately large changes in the outcome of recursive updates, reflecting phenomena such as priming, semantic shifts, or context amplification. This sensitivity is consistent with the observation that the brain exhibits strong context effects during language comprehension, where initial interpretations shape subsequent processing even when initial evidence is ambiguous. Formally, if <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> denotes the resonant trajectory, the geodesic deviation equation</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{D^2 \xi}{\mathrm{d}t^2} + R(\dot{X}, \xi)\dot{X} = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 2.1771em; vertical-align: -0.686em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mopen nulldelimiter"></span><span class="mfrac"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 1.4911em;"><span style="top: -2.314em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathrm">d</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.7401em;"><span style="top: -2.989em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style="top: -3.23em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="frac-line" style="border-bottom-width: 0.04em;"></span></span><span style="top: -3.677em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">D</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8141em;"><span style="top: -3.063em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04601em;">ξ</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.686em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose nulldelimiter"></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.1702em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord accent"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.9202em;"><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span></span><span style="top: -3.2523em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="accent-body" style="left: -0.0556em;"><span class="mord">˙</span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04601em;">ξ</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mord accent"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.9202em;"><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span></span><span style="top: -3.2523em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="accent-body" style="left: -0.0556em;"><span class="mord">˙</span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">0</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="6281" data-end="6580">governs the divergence of nearby trajectories, with <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span></span></span></span> the curvature tensor and <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.04601em;">ξ</span></span></span></span> the deviation vector. Positive curvature induces focusing of trajectories, corresponding to semantic convergence, while negative curvature induces divergence, corresponding to semantic branching or ambiguity.</p>
<p data-start="6582" data-end="7371">The role of amplistwistors becomes particularly clear in this geometric framework. The local amplitude, twist, and projection transformations alter the modal coefficients in a state-dependent manner. Viewed through the induced metric, these transformations implement nonlinear coordinate changes, each of which alters the metric and contributes to curvature. The twist component, in particular, corresponds to a rotation in modal space, and the cumulative effect of repeated twists yields curvature in a manner analogous to non-abelian gauge fields. The projection component enforces constraints such as sparsity or normalization, which in turn modify the Jacobian of the coordinate transformation and alter the curvature. Thus amplistwistors act as local generators of semantic curvature.</p>
<p data-start="7373" data-end="8153">Recursive computation emerges naturally from this structure. Because recursive updates proceed along paths determined by both the RSVP flow and TARTAN tiling, the trajectory is shaped by the local information geometry. Regions of high curvature correspond to semantic bottlenecks, where the system must expend additional computational resources to refine or disambiguate representations. Conversely, regions of low curvature permit rapid propagation of semantic state. This provides a geometric justification for the temporal hierarchy observed in empirical studies: integration over long timescales corresponds to traversal through high curvature regions of the manifold, while rapid lexical or syntactic processing corresponds to near-flat regions where curvature is negligible.</p>
<p data-start="8155" data-end="8727">The information-geometric viewpoint also suggests an interpretation of semantic prediction. When the system anticipates future states, it follows geodesics of the information metric, selecting directions that minimize expected distortion of semantic content. Deviations from geodesic motion correspond to prediction errors, which induce corrective flows that realign the trajectory with the manifold’s geometric structure. Prediction error thus corresponds to geodesic deviation, and semantic coherence corresponds to adherence to geodesic paths under the evolving metric.</p>
<p data-start="8729" data-end="9378">Finally, semantic curvature provides a powerful theoretical link between cortical geometry and information flow. Because the Laplace–Beltrami eigenfunctions determine the geometry of the resonant manifold, the spectral properties of the cortical sheet directly determine the shape of the information-geometric space in which cognitive dynamics unfold. Thus, the structure of sulci, gyri, and cortical connectivity manifests indirectly in the curvature of semantic dynamics. This formalizes the longstanding intuition that the brain’s geometry shapes its representational capabilities, now expressed through precise differential geometric constructs.</p>
<p data-start="9380" data-end="9970">In summary, the information-geometric structure induced by the RSVP–TARTAN system provides a rigorous account of how semantic complexity, curvature, and holonomy emerge from the interplay of cortical geometry and nonlinear recursive computation. Semantic curvature quantifies the distortion or amplification of information during recursive updates, and its associated geometric phase encodes global path dependence. Together, these constructs provide a unified framework for understanding semantic processing as a geometric phenomenon embedded in the resonant manifold of cortical dynamics.</p>
