 min-w-0 flex-col agent-turn" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="e10580b3-917e-47bf-afe6-633e3e7979c3" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-1"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="435">Below is <strong data-start="9" data-end="23">Section 24</strong>, written in continuous academic prose in pure LaTeX style, fully consistent with the preceding sections. This section develops the thermodynamic and entropic structure underlying the RSVP–TARTAN system, formalizing predictive uncertainty as a dynamical field, deriving entropy production inequalities, and establishing the connection between cortical resonance, thermodynamic efficiency, and semantic inference.</p>
<hr data-start="437" data-end="440">
<p data-start="442" data-end="514">\section{Entropy, Predictive Uncertainty, and Thermodynamic Constraints}</p>
<p data-start="516" data-end="1239">The RSVP framework incorporates an explicit entropy field <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\mathbf{r},t)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathbf">r</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>, representing predictive uncertainty, dispersion of semantic hypotheses, or disorder in the underlying representational configuration. In previous sections, the entropy field entered the dynamics through dissipative terms, modulating both the scalar potential and the vector field. In the present section, we develop a formal connection between this entropy field and the thermodynamic and informational constraints governing cortical computation. We show that entropy acts simultaneously as a state variable, a control parameter for stability, and a fundamental quantity governing the efficiency of recursive inference on the resonant manifold.</p>
<p data-start="1241" data-end="1291">The entropy field satisfies the evolution equation</p>
