<p data-start="4864" data-end="5349">where <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">A</span></span></span></span> is the connection form previously introduced in the analysis of geometric phase. This term expresses the entropy produced by transporting semantic content along a curved manifold. When the manifold is flat, the geometric contribution vanishes. When curvature is significant, the system must expend additional predictive resources to maintain coherence, generating entropy that is subsequently dissipated. The stochastic component follows from stochastic calculus:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">r</mi></mrow><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⊤</mi></msup><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">∇</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow><mo separator="true">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_\mathrm{stoch}(a)
= \frac{1}{2}\mathrm{Tr}\left(
