mord">fluctuation&nbsp;terms</span></span><span class="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="6492" data-end="6684">In the absence of noise, entropy decays, and the system evolves toward lower free energy. With noise, entropy fluctuates, but the expected decay still holds under the conditions of Section 19.</p>
<p data-start="6686" data-end="7401">These observations connect the RSVP–TARTAN dynamics to fundamental principles of thermodynamics and statistical inference. The scalar, vector, and entropy fields together constitute a thermodynamic system in which semantic states emerge as low-free-energy configurations shaped by nonlinear mode interactions, geometric constraints, and stochastic fluctuations. The entropy field imposes a limit on recursive inference: deep recursion incurs higher entropy, and excessive entropy destabilizes the resonant manifold. Conversely, moderate noise can reduce entropy by enhancing resonance and aiding discrimination among interpretations, explaining noise-induced facilitation observed in perceptual and cognitive tasks.</p>
<p data-start="7403" data-end="8263">At a broader conceptual level, the entropy field integrates the geometric and thermodynamic aspects of semantic processing. The geometric phase measures path-dependent transformations of semantic state, while semantic curvature measures the intrinsic complexity of the manifold. Both of these influence entropy production, and the interplay among these quantities determines the efficiency and stability of inference. Semantic integration corresponds to a flow toward low-entropy, low-curvature regions of the manifold. Narrative comprehension requires excursions into high-curvature regions, with entropy tracking the accumulation of uncertainty. Prediction requires alignment with geodesics of the information metric, minimizing entropy generation. These phenomena collectively establish a unified geometric–thermodynamic foundation for cortical computation.</p>
<p data-start="8265" data-end="8943">Finally, because entropy quantifies the uncertainty associated with semantic trajectories, it provides a direct bridge to behavioral and neural measurements such as reaction time, error rates, and oscillatory power. High entropy correlates with slower responses, increased variability, and broad-band increases in neural power. Low entropy correlates with rapid responses, low variability, and narrow-band resonant activity. This correspondence suggests experimental predictions, to be developed formally in subsequent sections, linking the theoretical structure of the RSVP–TARTAN system with empirical observations in neuroscience, psycholinguistics, and cognitive psychology.</p>
