"pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7651em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">ω</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0359em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">ϕ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> denote the instantaneous phase of the <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">m</span></span></span></span>-th mode. At any given moment, the inference machinery is influenced by the configuration of phases <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(t)=(\theta_1(t),\dots,\theta_M(t))</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord">Θ</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">θ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0278em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="minner">…</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mpunct">,</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">θ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0278em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.10903em;">M</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">))</span></span></span></span>, which collectively determine the alignment of the resonant modes and hence the effective geometry of the manifold. When the phases align constructively, the resulting superposition creates a configuration of peaks and troughs that amplifies certain directions of semantic change while suppressing others. When the phases are misaligned or drifting, the coherence of the global field diminishes, diminishing the efficacy of long-range coupling.</p>
<p data-start="1956" data-end="2671">The phase geometry is encoded in the differential structure of the phase vector field <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord">Θ</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>. Since each component evolves linearly in time, the geometry is trivial in one sense; however, the coupling of amplistwistors and correction flows to the resonant field introduces nontrivial geometric consequences. In particular, the impact of a prediction error is governed not solely by its magnitude or decomposition into parallel and orthogonal components, but also by the phase at which it enters the system. This observation aligns with empirical results showing that sensory input arriving at specific oscillatory phases produces stronger neural responses, sharper semantic alignment, and faster integration.</p>
<p data-start="2673" data-end="2901">To formalize this phenomenon, suppose that the corrected field update is gated by a phase-dependent multiplier <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\Theta(t))</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord">Γ</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">Θ</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">))</span></span></span></span>, where <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Γ</span></span></span></span> is a smooth functional on the torus <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^M</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8413em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8413em;"><span style="top: -3.063em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.10903em;">M</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>. The update equation then becomes</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">∇</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial_t \Psi = -\Gamma(\Theta(t))\nabla_\Psi \mathcal{F}(\Psi) + \eta.</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8444em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord" style="margin-right: 0.05556em;">∂</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2806em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0556em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">t</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord">Ψ</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord">−</span><span class="mord">Γ</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">Θ</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">))</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord">∇</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">Ψ</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.09931em;">F</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord">Ψ</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">η</span><span class="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="2982" data-end="3593">The function <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Γ</span></span></span></span> determines how readily the system incorporates correction flows at each phase configuration. When <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Γ</span></span></span></span> is large, the correction flows act strongly, causing the system to realign rapidly in response to prediction errors. When <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Γ</span></span></span></span> is small, the fields resist alteration, producing a form of inertial stability. The simplest form of <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord">Γ</span></span></span></span> depends only on the phase of the dominant mode, but a more accurate model incorporates interactions among modes, including phase differences <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_m-\theta_n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8444em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">θ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0278em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">−</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8444em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">θ</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0278em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">n</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> that determine the degree of constructive or destructive interference.</p>
<p data-start="3595" data-end="4343">This phase-dependent modulation provides a natural mechanism for temporal segmentation during perception. At phases where the system is receptive to incoming signals, external input exerts a strong influence on the internal fields. At complementary phases, the internal dynamics dominate, allowing the system to complete ongoing recursive computations without disruption. The alternation between these regimes corresponds to a form of rhythmic gating, analogous to how sensory and predictive processes in the cortex are known to be modulated by oscillations in the alpha, theta, and gamma bands. In the present framework, such phenomena arise naturally from the modulation of the free-energy gradient by the phase geometry of the resonant manifold.</p>
<p data-start="4345" data-end="5167">The geometry of phase relationships also determines how information propagates across the cortical surface. Since the resonant eigenfunctions span the manifold, any local perturbation decomposes into contributions across all modes. The evolution of these contributions is governed by their respective phases; consequently, information propagates through the system not as a wave traversing space, but as a rotation through the space of modes. This rotation explains how distant cortical regions can become synchronized without requiring signals to traverse anatomical pathways in real time. When the phases of two regions are aligned in the dominant modes, their dynamics become locked, and information encoded in one region becomes immediately interpretable by the other. When the phases diverge, the alignment dissolves.</p>
<p data-start="5169" data-end="5912">This understanding also sheds light on the apparent coexistence of standing and traveling waves in cortical activity. The resonant decomposition produces stationary eigenfunctions, but when the phases of the modes evolve asynchronously, their superposition produces patterns that appear to drift across the surface. The drift velocity depends on the phase gradients and therefore varies across regions and conditions. Under anesthesia, for example, the reduction in mode count and the dominance of low-frequency modes lead to a more homogeneous phase structure, eliminating the phase gradients that would give rise to traveling waves. This observation accords with experimental findings that traveling waves diminish during unconscious states.</p>
<p data-start="5914" data-end="6598">In the context of recursive inference, phase geometry determines not only the timing of updates but also the relational structure of the semantic manifold. Since the vector field <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4444em;"></span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.01597em;">v</span></span></span></span> encodes directed inference and semantic transitions, its coupling to the phase geometry determines which semantic directions are favoured or suppressed at each moment. When the phases of modes encoding a particular semantic dimension align, the vector field rotates toward that dimension, amplifying the internal expectation of the corresponding meaning. When phases diverge, the semantic direction becomes unstable, leading to heightened entropy and increased susceptibility to reanalysis.</p>
<p data-start="6600" data-end="7104">The interaction between phase geometry and entropy thus forms a dynamic balance between stability and flexibility. Low entropy and high phase coherence produce stable expectations and efficient integration. High entropy and phase dispersion produce flexible, exploratory dynamics that allow the system to consider alternative interpretations. This interplay provides a mechanistic account of how the brain navigates ambiguity, resolves conflicting cues, and transitions between semantic states over time.</p>
<p data-start="7106" data-end="7475" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">The present section establishes phase geometry as a core structural element of the recursive field model. In the next section, this structure will be embedded within a larger theoretical framework of information geometry and curvature-driven inference, enabling a deeper analysis of how the manifold structure adapts to the statistical regularities of the input stream.</p></div></div></div></div><div class="z-0 flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none touch:pointer-events-auto pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.440