<h3 data-start="1127" data-end="1187">2. <strong data-start="1134" data-end="1159">Use citations in-line</strong>, embedded in natural prose:</h3>
<p data-start="1188" data-end="1196">Example:</p>
<blockquote data-start="1197" data-end="1447">
<p data-start="1199" data-end="1447">The presence of structured intrinsic oscillatory modes suggests a low-dimensional manifold underlying resting-state dynamics, consistent with ultrafast fMRI decomposition into global eigenmodes \cite{Cabral2019,Roberts2019,Breakspear2017,Deco2009}.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3 data-start="1449" data-end="1502">3. <strong data-start="1456" data-end="1500">Avoid citation clusters that feel forced</strong></h3>
<p data-start="1503" data-end="1627">I will avoid simply adding “\cite{…} \cite{…}” mechanically. Instead, citations will be woven meaningfully and academically.</p>
<h3 data-start="1629" data-end="1691">4. <strong data-start="1636" data-end="1689">Repeat citations across sections when appropriate</strong></h3>
<p data-start="1692" data-end="1869">Classic works (Needham; Cybenko; Hornik; Poggio; Friston; Singer; Buzsáki; Deco and Jirsa; Honey–Sporns; Elman) will appear <em data-start="1816" data-end="1832">multiple times</em> across different conceptual domains.</p>
