7 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Abstract}

Recent work in systems neuroscience and machine learning has converged on a view of cognition as a deeply hierarchical, temporally extended process implemented over distributed neural substrates. High-temporal-resolution electrocorticography has revealed a monotonic relationship between representational depth in large language models and the latency of neural responses in high-order language areas, suggesting that cortical processing unfolds through a sequence of nonlinear transformations analogous to model layers </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{goldstein2025temporal}. In parallel, ultrafast functional magnetic resonance imaging has demonstrated that large-scale interaction patterns in the brain can be decomposed into a small number of macroscale oscillatory eigenmodes, which behave as standing waves constrained by anatomy and are strongly modulated by loss of consciousness </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{cabral2023intrinsic, roberts2019geometry, atasoy2016connectome}. These findings point toward a unified picture in which local cortical microcircuits instantiate nonlinear operators on high-dimensional state fields, while global connectivity and oscillatory structure coordinate these operations across space and time.

This paper develops a field-theoretic framework in which cortical columns are interpreted as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplitwistor cascades}: stacks of local operators that generalize the complex-analytic notion of an amplitwist to high-dimensional semantic fields in the cortex </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{needham1997visual, ahlfors1979complex}. Each amplitwistor implements a locally defined amplification and twist of a vector field representing latent semantic or perceptual content, and cascades of such operators provide the representational expressivity required to approximate arbitrary smooth mappings on compact domains </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{cybenko1989approximation, hornik1991approximation, poggio1990networks}. These local transformations are embedded in a continuous triplet of fields defined over a cortical manifold, comprising a scalar potential, a directed flow field, and an entropy-like measure that regulates uncertainty and gain. The resulting system resembles a neural field model with additional structure capturing the geometry of semantic computation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{wilson1972excitatory, amari1977dynamics, bressloff2012spatiotemporal, breakspear2017dynamic}.

The global organization of these amplitwistor cascades is mediated by the eigenmodes of anatomical and effective connectivity, which define a set of spatial basis functions on the cortical surface and subcortical structures </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{honey2010can, deco2009key, roberts2019geometry, atasoy2021harmonic}. When combined with realistic conduction delays and synaptic time constants, these eigenmodes generate structured oscillatory dynamics whose frequencies and spatial profiles map onto known electrophysiological bands and resting-state networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006rhythms, fries2015rhythms, sporns2010networks}. The theory developed here treats these modes as the global carriers that synchronize local amplitwistor cascades, thereby realizing a form of communication through coherence </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{fries2005communication, singer1999neuronal, varela2001brainweb}. Temporal receptive windows in higher-order areas emerge as a consequence of slow eigenmodes and recurrent field dynamics, rather than as an emergent property of discrete symbolic stages </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{hasson2008hierarchy, kiebel2008hierarchy}.

Mathematically, the framework is expressed as a system of coupled nonlinear partial differential equations evolving on a Riemannian manifold that approximates cortical geometry </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{evans2010partial, brezis2010functional, chavel1984eigenvalues}. A linear operator encodes diffusion and propagation along the cortical sheet, while nonlinear terms capture local amplification, rotational shear, and entropy-dependent gain control. These terms are organized into amplitwistor operators that act fiberwise on the field bundle, and their compositions are shown to approximate arbitrary continuous transformations under mild regularity conditions. The analysis draws on semigroup theory, spectral decompositions of the Laplace--Beltrami operator, and the theory of infinite-dimensional dynamical systems </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{pazy1983semigroups, temam1997infinite, ermentrout2010mathematical}. A well-posedness result for the resulting semilinear evolution problem is sketched, and the role of noise and stochastic forcing is discussed in relation to stochastic resonance and information transmission </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{deco2009key, bialek2001predictability}.

From a neuroscientific perspective, the proposed model integrates several strands of evidence. It accounts for the graded hierarchy of temporal integration windows observed in human cortex during naturalistic language processing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{hasson2008hierarchy, huth2016natural, hagoort2014brain, poeppel2014neuroanatomy}, explains why deep nonlinear language models provide a superior account of electrocorticographic signals compared with shallower or more symbolic alternatives </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{goldstein2025temporal, hale2018finding, toneva2019interpreting}, and aligns with observations that large-scale oscillatory modes are sensitive to both anesthesia and pathology </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{cabral2023intrinsic, decocringelbach2021dynamics}. In this view, cortical computation arises from the interaction between local amplitwistor cascades that implement context-sensitive nonlinear transformations and global eigenmodes that provide the temporal scaffolding and phase structure required for coherent long-range integration.

The framework also has implications for artificial intelligence. It suggests architectural motifs that combine local complex-analytic transformations with globally defined spectral modes, providing a bridge between transformer-like architectures </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{vaswani2017attention, saxe2013hierarchical} and neural field models of the brain </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{breakspear2017dynamic, bressloff2012spatiotemporal}. By emphasizing the role of continuous fields, eigenmodes, and nonlinear local operators, the theory offers a path toward machine systems that more faithfully capture the temporal and geometric organization of cortical computation, particularly in domains such as language where deep hierarchical processing and long-range dependencies are essential.

</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
