37 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Introduction}

The last two decades have witnessed an increasingly detailed empirical and theoretical picture of how the brain supports complex cognition. At the macroscopic level, theories of large-scale brain dynamics describe cortical and subcortical structures as nodes in a coupled dynamical system that exhibits oscillations, synchronization, and multistable states </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{breakspear2017dynamic, deco2009key, sporns2010networks}. At the mesoscopic level, neural field theories model the average activity of neuronal populations as continuous fields evolving over anatomical manifolds according to integro-differential or partial differential equations </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{wilson1972excitatory, amari1977dynamics, bressloff2012spatiotemporal}. At the microscopic level, decades of work on cortical microcircuits and columns have revealed a rich repertoire of laminar connectivity, inhibitory and excitatory balance, and canonical computations, including normalization and gain control </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{mountcastle1997columnar, soltesz2015cortical, carandini2012normalization, harris2013cortical}. 

In parallel, machine learning has produced artificial systems that achieve remarkable competence in domains such as language, vision, and control. Deep neural networks, including feedforward architectures, recurrent networks, and transformers, perform complex tasks by composing many layers of simple nonlinear transformations </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{cybenko1989approximation, hornik1991approximation, poggio1990networks, vaswani2017attention, saxe2013hierarchical}. Analyses of these networks have shown that depth, nonlinearity, and hierarchical feature extraction are essential for their representational power, particularly when learning structured data such as sentences or natural images </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{saxe2013hierarchical, raghu2017expressive}. The success of deep models has spurred a renewed effort to draw precise correspondences between their internal computations and neural activity in the brain, both at the level of representational similarity and at the level of temporal dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{hasson2008hierarchy, hale2018finding, toneva2019interpreting}.

A particularly striking development in this direction is the recent demonstration that the temporal structure of natural language processing in the human brain closely corresponds to the layered hierarchy of large language models </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{goldstein2025temporal}. Using high-temporal-resolution electrocorticography in epileptic patients listening to continuous narrative speech, Goldstein and colleagues showed that prediction performance of linear encoding models peaks at progressively later time lags as one moves from shallow to deep layers of a transformer-based language model. This layer--time alignment is especially pronounced in high-order language areas such as the inferior frontal gyrus and temporal pole, whereas it is absent in early auditory regions. The result implies a monotonic mapping between the sequential nonlinear transformations in an artificial language model and the temporal unfolding of cortical responses during comprehension. Importantly, the effect is not reproduced when layer representations are replaced by linear interpolations or by more symbolic linguistic features, underscoring the necessity of nonlinearity and depth.

Concurrently, work in ultrafast functional magnetic resonance imaging has revealed that spontaneous and task-related patterns of brain activity can be closely approximated by superpositions of a small number of macroscale oscillatory modes that behave as standing waves on the anatomical graph of the brain </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{cabral2023intrinsic, roberts2019geometry, atasoy2016connectome}. These modes, which are determined by the eigenstructure of structural or effective connectivity, give rise to characteristic spatial patterns and frequencies that organize large-scale interactions among regions. Their expression changes systematically with alterations in consciousness and pathology, suggesting that they are not merely statistical regularities but play a mechanistic role in constraining the flow of information </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{cabral2023intrinsic, deco2021dynamics, mahajan2023brain}. This perspective has deep roots in the theory of dynamical systems and spectral graph theory, where eigenmodes of Laplace-type operators provide a natural basis for describing diffusion, waves, and pattern formation on manifolds and networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{chavel1984eigenvalues, chung1997spectral, coifman2006diffusion}.

The convergence of these empirical and mathematical perspectives motivates the central question of this paper. Can one construct a unified field-theoretic account of cortical computation in which local microcircuits implement nonlinear transformations analogous to layers in a deep neural network, while global eigenmodes of anatomical connectivity provide the oscillatory scaffold that synchronizes these transformations across space and time? Answering this question requires a framework that respects the continuous geometry of cortical tissue, the discrete and laminar structure of columns, the nonlinear character of synaptic and dendritic integration, and the oscillatory and resonant nature of large-scale brain dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006rhythms, fries2015rhythms, singer1999neuronal, varela2001brainweb}. It also requires an account of how such a system can implement hierarchical semantic processing, particularly in the domain of language, where empirical data now constrain both spatial and temporal aspects of neural computation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{friederici2011brain, huth2016natural, hagoort2014brain, poeppel2014neuroanatomy}.

In this work, cortical columns are interpreted as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplitwistor cascades}: stacks of local operators acting on high-dimensional vector fields that represent semantic, perceptual, or motor states. The term ``amplitwistor'' is inspired by Needham's geometric interpretation of complex differentiation as a local operation that combines amplification and twist in the complex plane </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{needham1997visual}. In one complex dimension, the derivative of a holomorphic function at a point can be understood as the unique linear map that locally scales and rotates infinitesimal vectors. This provides an intuitively rich picture of analytic functions as fields of amplifications and twists. By generalizing this concept to higher-dimensional vector fields defined over cortical manifolds, one obtains a family of local operators that modulate the amplitude and orientation of latent activity patterns while respecting the underlying geometry.

The first component of the framework is a continuous field model defined on a Riemannian manifold that approximates the geometry of the cortex. At each point on this manifold, one defines a scalar potential, a vector field representing local flow or directed inference, and an entropy-like scalar that encodes uncertainty and gain. These fields evolve according to coupled partial differential equations that include both linear propagation terms and nonlinear interactions. The linear terms capture diffusion and anisotropic spread constrained by white-matter connectivity and cortical folding, while the nonlinear terms implement local amplification, rotational shear, and entropy-dependent modulation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{breakspear2017dynamic, bressloff2012spatiotemporal, ermentrout2010mathematical}. The combination of these terms yields a laminar neural field model capable of supporting waves, attractors, and oscillations.

The second component introduces amplitwistors as structured nonlinear operators acting fiberwise on this field bundle. Each amplitwistor is defined as a composition of an amplitude mapping, a twist mapping, and a projection that enforces constraints such as normalization or sparsity. Cascades of these operators, arranged in depth along the columnar axis, yield a family of transformations with universal approximation properties on the space of square-integrable fields, under conditions closely related to those that guarantee the expressivity of multilayer neural networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{cybenko1989approximation, hornik1991approximation, poggio1990networks}. Because the operators are defined in geometric rather than symbolic terms, they naturally support context-sensitive, graded computations that can be interpreted as semantic or perceptual inference.

The third component exploits the spectral properties of the cortical manifold and its connectivity graph. By decomposing the field dynamics into eigenmodes of the Laplace--Beltrami operator and related graph Laplacians, one obtains a set of global modes with associated eigenvalues and eigenfrequencies </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{chavel1984eigenvalues, roberts2019geometry, atasoy2016connectome}. These modes form a natural basis for describing resting-state networks and task-evoked patterns, and they provide a principled account of how long-range coherence arises from local interactions. In the proposed framework, these eigenmodes act as global carriers that modulate the gain and timing of amplitwistor cascades across the cortex, thereby implementing a form of modal synchronization analogous to communication through coherence </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{fries2005communication, singer1999neuronal, varela2001brainweb}. Temporal receptive windows and hierarchical processing emerge from the interaction between mode-specific time scales and the depth of local cascades.

The remainder of the paper is structured as follows. A background section reviews relevant aspects of cortical anatomy, neural field theory, oscillations, and language neuroscience, emphasizing empirical results that constrain any viable theory of hierarchical semantic processing. This is followed by a formal definition of the field model on a cortical manifold, including the scalar, vector, and entropy fields and their coupling through linear and nonlinear operators. The amplitwistor construction is then introduced and related to complex analysis and universal approximation results. Subsequent sections develop the spectral decomposition of the dynamics into eigenmodes, analyze the resulting semilinear evolution equations using semigroup theory, and establish conditions for well-posedness and stability. A dedicated section connects the theory to empirical findings on temporal receptive windows, large language model--brain alignment, and ultrafast fMRI eigenmodes. The final sections discuss implications for our understanding of cortical computation and outline how the framework may guide the design of artificial systems that more faithfully reflect the temporal and geometric organization of the brain.

</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
