6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Background: Cortical Architecture, Neural Fields, and Hierarchical Computation}

A comprehensive theory of cortical computation must engage simultaneously with anatomical structure, physiological dynamics, and the mathematical frameworks that most naturally describe them. At the anatomical level, the cortex is organized as a highly folded two-dimensional sheet endowed with a laminar architecture that exhibits both local microcircuitry and long-range connectivity. Classical work by Mountcastle and successors proposed that cortical tissue is partitioned into repeating columnar units, each containing a stereotyped configuration of excitatory and inhibitory neurons coupled vertically across layers and horizontally across nearby columns. Although the strict modularity of columns has been debated, the laminar arrangement and recurrent connectivity motifs have been repeatedly confirmed across species </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{mountcastle1997columnar, harris2013cortical, soltesz2015cortical}. The resulting architecture gives rise to canonical computations, including normalization, gain control, pooling, lateral inhibition, and pattern completion </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{carandini2012normalization, douglas2004recurrent}, which are thought to underlie perception and cognition across modalities.

At the mesoscopic scale, spatially distributed neural activity is naturally modeled as a continuous field defined on the cortical manifold. Early work by Wilson and Cowan formalized neural population dynamics using nonlinear integro-differential equations that captured excitatory and inhibitory interactions across space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{wilson1972excitatory}. Amari subsequently developed the theory of neural fields with translationally invariant kernels, leading to mathematically tractable analyses of bumps, traveling waves, and pattern formation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{amari1977dynamics}. Contemporary neural field theory extends these models to anatomically realistic geometries and incorporates laminar structure, synaptic delays, and anisotropic connectivity </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{bressloff2012spatiotemporal, ermentrout2010mathematical, break17}. These models have provided deep insights into how patterns of cortical activity emerge, stabilize, and propagate, and how they are modulated by noise, neuromodulation, and structural connectivity.

Large-scale brain dynamics further depend on oscillations across a broad range of frequencies, including delta, theta, alpha, beta, and gamma bands. Classical electrophysiology studies identified oscillatory rhythms as a fundamental organizing principle for neuronal communication and integration </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006rhythms}. Subsequent work demonstrated that coherence and phase relationships among oscillators can facilitate selective communication between neural populations, a principle now known as communication through coherence </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{fries2005communication}. The broader enterprise of understanding large-scale coordination has emphasized the role of phase synchronization, cross-frequency coupling, and metastability </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{varela2001brainweb, canoltyKnight2010, deco2009key}. These insights have been formalized mathematically using dynamical systems theory, where the cortex is interpreted as a high-dimensional oscillator network capable of generating rich trajectories, attractor landscapes, and transitions driven by noise or external input.

Recent empirical studies have transformed our understanding of how these oscillatory dynamics interact with structural connectivity. Graph-theoretic and spectral analyses have revealed that functional networks can often be understood as expressions of underlying eigenmodes of the anatomical connectome. Eigenmode-based approaches show that large-scale patterns of activity correspond to spatial harmonics determined by the Laplacian of the structural network </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{atasoy2016connectome, roberts2019geometry, jobst2020invivo}. These modes function as basis functions that shape both resting-state fluctuations and task-evoked responses. Their organization is consistent with long-established principles from spectral geometry, where eigenfunctions of Laplace-type operators describe standing waves and diffusion processes on curved surfaces </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{chavel1984eigenvalues}. The discovery that these modes are modulated by anesthesia, sleep, and neurological disorders suggests that they constitute a mechanistic substrate for global coordination </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{cabral2023intrinsic, deco2021dynamics}.

Cognition, and language in particular, emerges from the interaction between local microcircuit computations and these global constraints. Evidence from electrocorticography, magnetoencephalography, and intracranial recordings demonstrates that cortical processing is temporally hierarchical: early sensory regions respond within tens of milliseconds, intermediate regions integrate information over hundreds of milliseconds, and high-order regions, such as the inferior frontal gyrus and temporal pole, integrate over even longer windows </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{hasson2008hierarchy, huth2016natural, friederici2011brain, poeppel2014neuroanatomy}. This gradient of temporal receptive windows has been theorized to reflect an information-processing hierarchy akin to that found in deep neural networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{elman1990finding, hasson2008hierarchy}. Recent work has made this correspondence explicit by showing a near-monotonic relationship between layer depth in large language models and the latency of peak neural predictions in ECoG recordings </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{goldstein2025temporal}. Such findings suggest that cortical computation unfolds through a sequence of nonlinear transformations analogous to the layers of deep networks, coordinated by oscillatory and structural constraints.

A complete theory of cortical computation must therefore reconcile the continuous geometry of cortical fields, the nonlinear transformations performed locally by microcircuits, and the global spectral constraints imposed by connectivity. It must also explain how semantic processing emerges from these interactions, particularly in light of empirical evidence that deep nonlinear language models provide the best current match to human neural recordings </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{hale2018finding, toneva2019interpreting}. The framework developed in this paper aims to synthesize these observations by interpreting cortical columns as cascades of structured nonlinear operators acting on fields of latent representations, and by interpreting large-scale oscillatory eigenmodes as the global mechanism that coordinates these operations over time.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
