7 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Cortical State Fields and the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum}

The formulation of cortical computation in this paper begins with the representation of neural activity as a triplet of fields defined on a Riemannian manifold that approximates the geometry of the cortical sheet. These fields capture complementary aspects of neural state: a scalar potential encoding local activation or semantic density, a vector field representing directed flow or propagation of information, and an entropy-like scalar quantifying uncertainty, variability, or gain control. This triplet forms a geometric and dynamical substrate upon which local nonlinear operators, such as amplitwistors, act pointwise or locally to transform representations.

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denote the cortical manifold, a compact two-dimensional Riemannian surface embedded in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>3</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> with metric </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The choice of a Riemannian manifold is motivated by the laminar architecture of the cortex and its extensive folding, which gives rise to a complex geometry that constrains diffusion, wave propagation, and connectivity. The Laplace--Beltrami operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> associated with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> provides a canonical generator of local interactions, capturing both isotropic and anisotropic spread of activity along the cortical sheet </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{chavel1984eigenvalues}. Anatomical connectivity, white-matter tracts, and long-range projections can be incorporated by augmenting this operator with graph-Laplacian or connectivity kernels defined over a structural connectome </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{honey2010can, coifman2006diffusion}.

The first component of the state is a scalar function </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> : M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> [0,T] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, which encodes local activation, potential, or semantic density. This field is analogous to the mean membrane potential or firing rate in classical neural field models </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{wilson1972excitatory, amari1977dynamics}, but with additional interpretive flexibility afforded by its role in modulating nonlinear operators. The second component is a vector field </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} : M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> [0,T] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> TM</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, representing directed flow of activity, feature propagation, or context-sensitive inference. Vector fields have long been used to represent directional interactions and wavefront propagation in excitable media and neural fields </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{bressloff2012spatiotemporal, ermentrout2010mathematical}. The third component is an entropy-like scalar </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>S : M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> [0,T] </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, which models uncertainty, variability, and gain control. This field can be interpreted as a local inverse temperature regulating the responsiveness of the system, an idea with precedents in both stochastic neural field theory and predictive coding models </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{friston2005theory, bialek2001predictability}.

The evolution of these fields is governed by a system of coupled partial differential equations of the form
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> t} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{curl}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> + F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> t} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>4 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>5 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>6 S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} + F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> S}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> t} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>7 |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{div}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}| + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>8 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>9 S + F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where the constants </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> encode diffusion rates, coupling strengths, and decay terms associated with uncertainty, gain, and vorticity. The nonlinear functions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}}, F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> represent local interactions arising from synaptic dynamics, dendritic nonlinearities, or microcircuit mechanisms. Their specific functional forms may be derived from biophysically grounded models </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{ermentrout2010mathematical, break17} or defined abstractly to capture higher-level computational roles.

The structure of these equations draws on several strands of mathematical neuroscience. Diffusion terms reflect the spatial smoothing and local averaging characteristic of cortical microcircuits. Curl and divergence terms capture the rotational and compressive aspects of flow that give rise to waves, vortices, and other structured spatiotemporal patterns in neural tissue. Multiplicative interactions with the entropy field implement gain modulation, consistent with the role of neuromodulatory systems in regulating responsiveness, variability, and integration time scales </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{harris2013cortical, carandini2012normalization}. Quadratic interactions in the entropy equation reflect local accumulation of uncertainty or competition among signals, consistent with normative interpretations based on predictive coding </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{friston2005theory, raoBallard1999}.

The resulting system is a semilinear evolution equation on a product bundle over the cortical manifold. It admits a natural interpretation in terms of energy landscapes, entropy production, and information processing. The scalar potential </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> contributes to local energy wells or attractor states; the vector field </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> defines trajectories of directed inference or propagation; and the entropy field </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> modulates the depth and curvature of the potential landscape. This triplet structure provides a flexible substrate upon which local nonlinear operators—specifically amplitwistors—may act to reshape and refine representations in a context-dependent manner.

The interpretation of these fields as semantic rather than purely biophysical variables is motivated by empirical results showing strong correspondences between high-dimensional neural embeddings and deep network representations in tasks such as language processing. Electrocorticographic studies demonstrate that cortical activity patterns in high-order regions reflect distributed, gradient representations of lexical, syntactic, and semantic features </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{huth2016natural, hagoort2014brain}. The nonlinear dynamics of the fields, particularly the interactions between </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, give rise to hierarchical temporal integration windows analogous to those observed in language comprehension and predicted by deep neural networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{goldstein2025temporal}. In subsequent sections, amplitwistors will be introduced as local operators that act upon this field structure, providing the nonlinear transformations required to realize context-sensitive semantic computation.
