6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Iterative Field Evolution and Multiscale Decomposition: The TARTAN Framework}

The nonlinear evolution of cortical state fields requires a mathematical mechanism capable of expressing how local transformations accumulate over time, how representations are refined across multiple temporal scales, and how the influence of past states is selectively retained or attenuated. In this work, these requirements are addressed through an iterative evolution operator derived from a framework inspired by multiscale geometric decomposition and dynamical systems theory. This mechanism, termed TARTAN (Trajectory-Aware Recursive Tiling with Annotated Noise) in its original computational formulation, is reinterpreted here without reliance on the term “recursion'' and instead described as a structured sequence of operator unfoldings acting upon the triplet of cortical fields.

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t, S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denote the full cortical state at time </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The iterative evolution of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is defined through a family of nonlinear operators </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> acting on a Hilbert space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> of vector-valued fields on the cortical manifold. The update takes the form
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> represents an infinitesimal time step or, in discrete simulations, the unit of temporal progression. The operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> comprises several components: a local nonlinear transformation acting pointwise or in small neighborhoods on the manifold; a spatial smoothing or diffusion component; a temporal memory operator that selectively retains information about past states; and an adaptive noise term that introduces stochastic perturbations dependent on the state of the system.

The construction of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> can be motivated by the need to model the progressive elaboration of semantic representations observed in neural recordings. Electrocorticographic evidence demonstrates that activity in high-order language regions evolves over several hundred milliseconds, with successive transformations capturing increasingly abstract aspects of linguistic structure </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{goldstein2025temporal, huth2016natural, friederici2011brain}. This temporal unfolding mirrors the layered transformations in deep neural networks, which are known to approximate arbitrary nonlinear functions under appropriate conditions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{hornik1991approx, cybenko1989superpositions}. In the cortical context, such layered transformations are not spatial but temporal: the brain performs a series of nonlinear updates on its own internal fields, with each update depending on the previous field configuration.

The operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> incorporates a multiscale decomposition through a tiling of the manifold into overlapping patches </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>U</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> at varying characteristic sizes. Within each tile, local operators act with distinct integration windows, capturing the empirical gradient in temporal receptive fields across the cortical hierarchy </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{hasson2008hierarchy, honey2010can}. Small tiles execute rapid, high-frequency updates akin to those in early sensory regions, while larger tiles evolve more slowly and integrate information over broader temporal spans, consistent with the behavior of anterior temporal and prefrontal regions.

In addition to spatial tiling, the operator includes a temporal buffer or history term. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denote a functional depending on the past </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> field states. The full update then takes the form
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{D}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{D}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> represents diffusion and geometric propagation determined by the PDEs governing the fields; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denotes the local nonlinear transformation induced by amplitwistors; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> encodes temporally extended influences; and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a stochastic term. This decomposition is reminiscent of operator-splitting methods used in numerical analysis </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{leveque2007finite}, but here it serves as an explicit model for cortical computation rather than a computational tool.

The history operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> plays a crucial role in maintaining stability in the presence of noise and in sustaining representations over time. Neurophysiological studies demonstrate that cortical circuits exhibit a form of inertial integration, in which representations evolve smoothly and rarely undergo abrupt changes unless driven by strong external input </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{deco2009key, break17}. This property can be formalized by choosing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> to implement an exponentially weighted average of past field states:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{j=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t - j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where coefficients satisfy </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j = 1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> decaying geometrically. This structure is analogous to heavy-ball or momentum methods in optimization </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{polyak1964some}, which accelerate convergence by exploiting the inertia of the system.

The noise term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is modeled as a stochastic process with state-dependent covariance, reflecting the fact that neural noise is not white but structured by synaptic dynamics, cortical geometry, and network state </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{faisal2008noise, deco2009key}. State-dependent noise plays a fundamental role in generating metastable trajectories and facilitating transitions between attractors </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{bryngelson1987funnels, rabinovich2008transient}. The presence of noise also aligns with empirical evidence showing variability in neural responses even under identical stimuli </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{shadlen1998variable}.

The iterative application of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> thus defines a time-evolving trajectory </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> in the manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The trajectory is shaped by local nonlinearities, global geometric constraints, multiscale integration, and stochastic fluctuations. In the limit </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, the update rule converges to a continuous-time semilinear evolution equation driven by the PDEs describing the cortical fields. In simulations and empirical modeling, discrete-time evolution offers additional flexibility, permitting the incorporation of learned or experimentally derived operators.

This iterative framework provides a mathematically coherent and biologically grounded account of how cortical representations evolve over time. It reconciles the empirically observed temporal hierarchy of language processing with the theoretical structure of deep nonlinear transformations. It also furnishes a foundation for the subsequent introduction of amplitwistors as the local operators that enact the nonlinear transformation component of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
