L15.959 12.3975C16.2466 12.2211 16.4808 11.9682 16.6348 11.666L16.6914 11.5361C16.7428 11.3924 16.7797 11.2026 16.8027 10.9209C16.8341 10.5374 16.835 10.0439 16.835 9.33301V6.5C16.835 5.78896 16.8341 5.29563 16.8027 4.91211C16.7797 4.63025 16.7428 4.44063 16.6914 4.29688L16.6348 4.16699C16.4807 3.86474 16.2466 3.61186 15.959 3.43555L15.833 3.36524C15.675 3.28473 15.4636 3.22797 15.0879 3.19727C14.7044 3.16593 14.211 3.16504 13.5 3.16504H10.667C9.9561 3.16504 9.46259 3.16595 9.0791 3.19727C8.79739 3.22028 8.6076 3.2572 8.46387 3.30859L8.33399 3.36524C8.03176 3.51923 7.77886 3.75343 7.60254 4.04102L7.53125 4.16699C7.4508 4.32498 7.39397 4.53655 7.36328 4.91211C7.33985 5.19893 7.33562 5.54719 7.33399 6.00195H9.33301C10.022 6.00195 10.5791 6.00131 11.0293 6.03809C11.4873 6.07551 11.8937 6.15471 12.2705 6.34668L12.4883 6.46875C12.984 6.7728 13.3878 7.20854 13.6533 7.72949L13.7197 7.87207C13.8642 8.20859 13.9292 8.56974 13.9619 8.9707C13.9987 9.42092 13.998 9.97799 13.998 10.667V12.665ZM18.165 9.33301C18.165 10.022 18.1657 10.5791 18.1289 11.0293C18.0961 11.4302 18.0311 11.7914 17.8867 12.1279L17.8203 12.2705C17.5549 12.7914 17.1509 13.2272 16.6553 13.5313L16.4365 13.6533C16.0599 13.8452 15.6541 13.9245 15.1963 13.9619C14.8593 13.9895 14.4624 13.9935 13.9951 13.9951C13.9935 14.4624 13.9895 14.8593 13.9619 15.1963C13.9292 15.597 13.864 15.9576 13.7197 16.2939L13.6533 16.4365C13.3878 16.9576 12.9841 17.3941 12.4883 17.6982L12.2705 17.8203C11.8937 18.0123 11.4873 18.0915 11.0293 18.1289C10.5791 18.1657 10.022 18.165 9.33301 18.165H6.5C5.81091 18.165 5.25395 18.1657 4.80371 18.1289C4.40306 18.0962 4.04235 18.031 3.70606 17.8867L3.56348 17.8203C3.04244 17.5548 2.60585 17.151 2.30176 16.6553L2.17969 16.4365C1.98788 16.0599 1.90851 15.6541 1.87109 15.1963C1.83431 14.746 1.83496 14.1891 1.83496 13.5V10.667C1.83496 9.978 1.83432 9.42091 1.87109 8.9707C1.90851 8.5127 1.98772 8.10625 2.17969 7.72949L2.30176 7.51172C2.60586 7.0159 3.04236 6.6122 3.56348 6.34668L3.70606 6.28027C4.04237 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Amplistwistors as Local Nonlinear Operators on Cortical Fields}

The nonlinear transformations applied locally to cortical fields are formalized by a class of operators termed amplitwistors, inspired by complex analysis and geometric function theory. The concept originates from the notion of “amplitwist” transformations in the pedagogical work of Needham </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{needham1997visual}, further developed mathematically in studies of complex derivatives and rotation–dilation operators </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{soto2019amplitwist}. In the present formulation, amplitwistors generalize these operations to vector-valued fields defined on a curved manifold and serve as the infinitesimal building blocks of cortical computation.

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t, S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denote the cortical state at time </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. An amplitwistor </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> acting at a point </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is defined as a triple of maps
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is an amplitude modulation operator, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a local geometric transformation (typically a rotation, shear, or conformal distortion), and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a projection onto a lower-dimensional subspace or coordinate chart. These maps act jointly to produce a transformed value
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\prime</span></span><span>}(x) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Big</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t(x))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t(x))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Big</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

The amplitude modulation operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a nonlinear function that acts componentwise on the fields. Biophysically, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> captures gain modulation, dendritic nonlinearities, or local normalization mechanisms characteristic of cortical microcircuits </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{carandini2012normalization}. Mathematical examples include polynomial activation functions, sigmoidal compressions, or exponential scalings. Functions of this type are known to generate universal approximators when combined with affine maps, a property extensively studied in the context of neural networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{hornik1991approx, poggio1990networks}.

The geometric transformation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> acts on the vector field </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and captures the orientation- and phase-dependent modulation of signals observed in cortical columns. Empirical studies demonstrate that neuronal responses are sensitive to orientation, direction, and phase of stimuli, reflecting underlying symmetries in receptive fields </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{hubel1968receptive, miller2014canonical}. Mathematically, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> may be modeled as an element of the orthogonal group </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>SO(2)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> acting in the tangent space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>xM</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, or as a conformal map in the complex plane under suitable coordinate choices. In more general settings, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> may depend nonlinearly on the state variables, giving rise to state-dependent rotations or shears.

The projection operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> implements dimensionality reduction or selection among competing features. Projections of this sort are pervasive in cortical computation, manifesting as winner-take-all dynamics, sparse coding, or subspace selection </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{olshausen1997sparse}. Mathematically, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is modeled as an orthogonal projection or, more generally, a Lipschitz continuous map into a lower-dimensional manifold.

The composition of these three components defines a rich class of nonlinear operators. Given smoothness and boundedness conditions on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, the amplitwistor map is locally Lipschitz in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, ensuring existence and uniqueness of solutions to the evolution equation in which it is embedded. The class of all amplitwistors forms an algebra under composition, enabling the construction of complex transformations through cascades of simpler ones. This compositional property is critical for universal approximation: cascades of amplitwistors can approximate arbitrary smooth maps on compact subsets of the cortical state space, extending classical results on superpositions of sigmoidal functions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{cybenko1989superpositions} to the manifold-valued case.

Amplistwistors also admit a geometric interpretation. The action of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> can be viewed as a local rotation of directional preferences, consistent with the organization of orientation columns in visual cortex. The projection </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> incorporates the selective pooling characteristic of complex cells and higher-order cortical representations. The multiplicative combination of these actions yields a transformation whose effect resembles the composition of dilation, rotation, and projection operators studied in the context of conformal geometry and quasiconformal maps </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{ahlfors2006lectures}. The biological plausibility of such transformations is supported by empirical observations of nonlinear mixed selectivity in prefrontal and temporal cortices </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{rigotti2013mixed}.

When integrated into the iterative evolution operator described in the previous section, amplitwistors serve as the local nonlinear mechanism that reshapes the scalar, vector, and entropy fields over time. Their composition across successive operator unrollings gives rise to the temporal hierarchy observed in neural recordings, while their spatially varying structure encodes the functional specialization of cortical regions.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
