6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{The Governing Field Equations on the Cortical Manifold}

The cortical state is represented by a triplet of fields
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t, S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
defined on a compact two-dimensional Riemannian manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> embedded in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>3</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, representing the cortical sheet. The geometry of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is specified by a metric </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, with associated Laplace--Beltrami operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, covariant derivative </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, divergence </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{div}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and curl </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Diameters, curvature bounds, and injectivity radii are assumed to satisfy the regularity conditions standard in geometric analysis </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{aubin1998some, taylor2011partial}.

The temporal evolution of the fields is governed by a system of coupled semilinear partial differential equations of the general form
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{align}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>= D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> + A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:phi</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>pde}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>[4pt]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>= D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} + A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:v</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>pde}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>[4pt]
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t S </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>= D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g S + A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\label</span></span><span>{eq:S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>pde}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{align}
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v, D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S &gt; 0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> are diffusion coefficients, and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> are state-dependent stochastic terms. Each nonlinear term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> incorporates biologically motivated interactions:

1. </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> includes gain modulation, excitatory and inhibitory coupling, and cross-field terms. Motivated by work on cortical field models </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{breakspear2017dynamic, wilson1973mathematical, amari1977dynamics}, we take
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 = c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{div}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} - c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

2. </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> captures vector flow modulations and vorticity:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 = c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> + c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>4</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}) - c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>5 S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This structure is reminiscent of hydrodynamic models used in large-scale cortical dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{jones2009spatial, deco2009key}.

3. </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> represents entropy production due to divergence, nonlinear activation, and relaxation:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 = c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>6</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{div}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}| + c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>7</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 - c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>8 S.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This term aligns with classical formulations of entropy dynamics in stochastic neural field models </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{faisal2008noise}.

The stochastic components </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> are taken to be multiplicative noise processes with martingale structure, following models of neural variability </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{shadlen1998variable}:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(x,t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t(x))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(x,t),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> are spatially correlated Gaussian fields with covariance kernels of Matérn type </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{lindgren2011explicit}.

Boundary conditions reflect the topology of the cortical sheet. For surfaces with sulcal folds, no-flux (Neumann) conditions are imposed along sulcal boundaries, consistent with constrained diffusion across cortical banks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{basser1994mr}:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{n} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span> S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{n} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{n} = 0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{on } </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> M.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

The system </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eqref</span></span><span>{eq:phi</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>pde}--</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eqref</span></span><span>{eq:S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>pde} can be written compactly as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is the block-diagonal diffusion operator and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> collects all nonlinear coupling terms. This formulation prepares the ground for a rigorous analysis of the well-posedness, regularity, and long-time behavior of solutions.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
