 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Operator-Theoretic Structure and Analytical Foundations}

The evolution equation
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
defines a semilinear parabolic system on the function space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>X = H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s(M;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{d+2})</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a Sobolev space with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>s &gt; 2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> to guarantee classical differentiability of solutions under Sobolev embedding theorems </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{adams2003sobolev}. The linear operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is sectorial and generates an analytic semigroup on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, a property inherited from the ellipticity of the Laplace--Beltrami operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{pazy2012semigroups, lunardi2012analytic}. This ensures that the mild solution of the linearized system is given by
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{lin}} = e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

The nonlinear term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> consists of polynomial interactions in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and their derivatives of order at most one. Such nonlinearities satisfy local Lipschitz continuity on bounded subsets of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, a consequence of Moser-type inequalities and the algebra structure of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> for </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>s &gt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dim</span></span><span>(M)/2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{taylor2011partial}. Consequently, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a locally Lipschitz map from </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> to itself.

The full evolution problem can therefore be written in mild form as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t = e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(t-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{W}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{W}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a stochastic convolution term
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{W}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(t-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
defined in the It</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\=</span></span><span>{o} sense </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{da1992stochastic}. Under the assumption that </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is globally Lipschitz, the noise term lies in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>; C([0,T]; X))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, ensuring existence and uniqueness of global mild solutions.

To analyze long-term dynamics, we consider the dissipativity of the nonlinear operator. The divergence and vorticity couplings in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> can be shown to satisfy
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>X </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 - C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>Y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>p,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>Y</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a higher-regularity space and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>p&gt;2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The coercive term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>-C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>Y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>p</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> results from the presence of negative linear coefficients such as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>-c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>5 S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>-c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>8 S</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, which act as damping terms. This structure is typical of semilinear PDEs admitting global attractors </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{temam1997infinite}, although a full construction of the attractor is left for future work.

A crucial component of cortical modeling is the smoothing effect of the semigroup </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. For each </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>t&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, we have the estimate
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}} f</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{s+k}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> C t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-k/2}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
reflecting the analytic nature of the semigroup and aligning with the classical heat kernel bounds on manifolds </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{chavel1984eigenvalues}. This smoothing property guarantees that any initial condition </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> becomes instantly more regular for all </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>t&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, consistent with the biological requirement of spatial coherence in cortical fields.

The presence of the amplitwistor operator in the nonlinear term does not disrupt these properties. Because amplitwistors act pointwise and by smooth maps, they respect the Sobolev structure and preserve analytic semigroup well-posedness. Their composition with diffusion gives rise to a semilinear evolution equation of classical form, ensuring compatibility with established theories of parabolic PDEs on manifolds.

This analytical framework validates the use of iterative operator unfoldings to approximate the continuous dynamics. In numerical implementations, the time-discretized operator
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
converges to the semigroup evolution as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> under Trotter–Kato approximation theorems </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{kato1978trotter}. This result provides a formal mathematical bridge between the PDE formulation of cortical fields and the discrete computational dynamics described in earlier sections.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
