The coupled evolution equations governing the fields </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> constitute a semilinear parabolic system on the compact Riemannian manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. In this section a complete analytical foundation is presented for the existence, uniqueness, and regularity of solutions. The argument proceeds by analyzing the operator structure of the system, the regularity properties supplied by the Laplace--Beltrami operator, and the Lipschitz continuity of the nonlinear interactions, culminating in a rigorous well-posedness theorem in Sobolev spaces. The exposition draws on classical results from the theory of parabolic PDEs on manifolds </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{pazy2012semigroups, taylor2011partial, adams2003sobolev}, on stochastic evolution equations </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{da1992stochastic}, and on geometric analysis </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{aubin1998some, chavel1984eigenvalues}.

