</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
is well-defined in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Omega</span></span><span>; C([0,T]; X))</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and satisfies the requisite boundedness and regularity conditions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{da1992stochastic}. Pathwise uniqueness follows by standard estimates for stochastic evolution equations.

A global mild solution thus exists, is unique, and belongs almost surely to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C([0,T]; X)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for every finite </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Regularity theory ensures higher smoothness for positive times. The analytic semigroup guarantees that for each </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{s'}(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for all </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s'</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, giving </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> or even analytic regularity, depending on the smoothness of nonlinearities. Similar conclusions hold for stochastic parabolic equations under mild hypotheses </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{ferrario2003smoothness}.

These considerations establish the global well-posedness of the cortical field equations and justify the continuous dynamical framework used throughout the remainder of the analysis. They also guarantee that numerical discretizations based on Trotter splitting or finite element methods converge to the true solution, as long as stability constraints are respected. This mathematical foundation anchors the rest of the theoretical developments in a rigorous functional-analytic setting.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
