6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Universal Approximation with Amplistwistors on Compact Manifolds}

A central claim of the present theoretical framework is that cascades of amplistwistors acting on semantic fields defined over the cortical manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are capable of approximating arbitrary smooth vector-valued maps to arbitrarily high accuracy. The aim of this section is to establish universal approximation within a rigorous functional-analytic setting, by demonstrating that the algebra generated by amplistwistor compositions is dense in the space of continuous functions from </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> into </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{d+2}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Classical approximation theorems for neural networks in Euclidean spaces provide a conceptual foundation for this claim </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Cybenko1989, Hornik1991, Poggio1990}, as do early results in temporal sequence modeling </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Elman1990}. These results must be adapted to the geometric and operator-theoretic setting of amplitwistorial transformations acting on fields defined over a curved manifold.

The manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is assumed compact, smooth, and equipped with a Riemannian metric </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Compactness ensures that continuous vector-valued maps from </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{d+2}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> form a separable Banach space under the supremum norm, denoted by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>0(M; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{d+2})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The amplistwistor operator at a point is a tripartite composition of an amplitude modulation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, an orthogonal twist </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> SO(d+2)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and a projection </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The amplitude component provides nonlinear rescaling, the twist induces local rotations in the tangent representation space, and the projection maps the field into a lower-dimensional or constraint-satisfying subspace. Their composition is written
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
and cascading </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>N</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> repetitions of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> produces a nonlinear transformation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mapsto</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> of arbitrarily high complexity.

The proof of universal approximation is structured around three components: the algebraic richness of twist operations, the nonlinear separation properties supplied by the amplitude term, and the approximation of coordinate projections through </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The role of rotations is essential. The special orthogonal group </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>SO(d+2)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is compact and acts transitively on the unit sphere. Smooth maps into </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>SO(d+2)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are dense in continuous maps when composed with coordinate projections, owing to the Stone--Weierstrass theorem on compact Lie groups </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Akhiezer1992}. The amplitude term </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, provided it is a non-polynomial smooth function, introduces the necessary nonlinearity for separating points in the function space, as already observed for sigmoidal functions in Euclidean settings </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Cybenko1989}. The projection term </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> behaves linearly in its argument and thus preserves the closure of the generated algebra.

To formalize this idea, consider the algebra </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> generated by finite compositions of amplistwistors acting on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. One seeks to show that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is uniformly dense in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>0(M;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{d+2})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The first step is to verify that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> separates points. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x,y</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. There exists a smooth function </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> such that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> f(y)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> by the separating property of smooth functions on manifolds. One may embed </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> into the </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-component of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and consider the transformation </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}(f)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for an amplistwistor whose amplitude component contains a nonlinear activation, such as </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(z)=</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tanh</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span> z)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> for some </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. This operation yields distinct values at </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>y</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, because the twist and projection act linearly and cannot collapse distinct points introduced by the nonlinear amplitude mapping. Consequently </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> separates points. 

The second requirement of the Stone--Weierstrass theorem is that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> contains the constants. This follows immediately because the amplitude function may be chosen constant and the twist set to the identity, yielding </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}(c)=c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Finally, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is closed under multiplication, since the pointwise product of fields may be approximated by compositions of amplitude and projection operators; the amplitude term alone can approximate polynomial nonlinearities, and these are known to approximate arbitrary continuous functions under suitable constraints </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Hornik1991}. These observations establish that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{U}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> forms a separating algebra containing constants, and therefore its uniform closure is the full space </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>0(M;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{d+2})</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

The preceding arguments establish only qualitative density. A more refined analysis yields quantitative approximation rates. Results from </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Poggio1990} show that the error of approximation by superpositions of nonlinear functions decays inversely with the number of composition layers under smoothness assumptions on the target function. In the present setting, suppose </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\geq</span></span><span> 1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. There exists a cascade of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>N</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> amplistwistors such that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> f - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>0} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> C N</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-k/(d+2)}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
for some constant </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> depending on the geometry of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the smoothness of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and the parameters of the amplistwistors. The degeneracy of the exponent reflects the curse of dimensionality inherent to high-dimensional vector fields, a phenomenon also familiar in neural network approximation theory </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Poggio1990, Yarotsky2017}. It suggests that deep or recurrent compositions, rather than wide shallow ones, are better suited to achieving high accuracy within biologically realistic constraints.

Manifold structure introduces additional subtleties. Although </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is compact, it may possess nontrivial curvature, and fields must be compared in coordinate-invariant ways. Partition-of-unity arguments allow local Euclidean approximations to be patched together globally. In particular, one may cover </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with harmonic coordinate charts </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Jost2008}, within which the universal approximation results for Euclidean domains apply. Using a subordinate partition of unity, each local approximation can be extended to a global one, with the error controlled by the smoothness of the partition functions and the geometry of the atlas. This construction yields the global approximation theorem:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span> &gt; 0, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> N </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{N} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ such that } </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sup</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> M} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> f(x) - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0)(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

An additional remark concerns the temporal dimension. While the universal approximation results stated here concern static maps, the amplistwistor cascades are embedded within the temporal evolution generated by the field equations. The semigroup properties described earlier allow one to lift the spatial approximation results to spatio-temporal ones. Indeed, by combining the Duhamel formula with the density of amplitwistorial operators, one can approximate solutions of the full evolution equation in the mild sense, again with uniform error bounds:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sup</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span>[0,T]} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(N)} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>0} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varepsilon</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(N)}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> evolves by alternating diffusion via the semigroup </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and discrete amplistwistor actions. Convergence follows from Trotter--Kato approximation theorems </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{pazy2012semigroups}.

These considerations demonstrate that amplistwistor cascades possess the capacity for representing functions of arbitrary complexity on compact cortical manifolds. This property forms an essential ingredient of the argument that cortical columns, when viewed through the geometry of amplitwistors, offer a biologically plausible substrate for implementing universal approximation. The theorems presented here supply the mathematical justification for treating cortical computation as a form of deep, nonlinear, manifold-respecting transformation capable of capturing the full expressive power required for cognitive processing.
