6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Geometric Structure of the Cortical Manifold}

The mathematical framework developed in this work rests critically on the geometric structure of the cortical sheet, which is modeled as a two-dimensional compact Riemannian manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> embedded in three-dimensional Euclidean space. This section formalizes those geometric assumptions and develops the analytical tools required for treating the cortical fields </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as evolving over a curved surface. The presentation draws on standard references in geometric analysis </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{aubin1998some, chavel1984eigenvalues, jost2008riemannian} and integrates results from spectral theory on manifolds that have proven central in mathematical neuroscience </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Roberts2019, Breakspear2017}.

The cortex is represented as a smooth manifold of class </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with a Riemannian metric </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> inherited from its embedding in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>3</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. This metric defines the local notion of distance, area, gradient, divergence, and Laplacian. The compactness assumption is justified by the bounded extent of the cortical sheet and ensures that many of the analytically delicate issues arising on non-compact spaces do not appear here. In particular, compactness yields a discrete Laplace--Beltrami spectrum with no continuous spectrum, a property crucial for eigenmode decompositions observed in ultrafast fMRI recordings </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Cabral2023}. The absence of a boundary simplifies the analysis, although the theory can be extended to include sulcal boundaries via Neumann or mixed boundary conditions without substantive modification.

A fundamental geometric parameter is the injectivity radius, defined as the largest radius for which the exponential map at any point remains a diffeomorphism onto its image. For the human cortex this radius is strictly positive, though its magnitude varies with local curvature. The injectivity radius provides a quantitative measure of geometric regularity and allows for the construction of geodesic normal coordinates. Within these coordinates the metric approximates the Euclidean metric up to curvature corrections, and higher-order derivatives of the metric are bounded in terms of sectional curvature. This local Euclideanity underlies the use of harmonic coordinate charts, which minimize distortions and enable the application of elliptic regularity theory </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{taylor2011partial}.

Sobolev spaces on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are defined in the standard way using partitions of unity and local coordinate patches. For any integer </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\geq</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the Sobolev space </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> consists of square-integrable functions whose weak derivatives up to order </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are square-integrable in every coordinate chart, with the norm obtained by summing contributions from each chart. This definition is independent of the choice of atlas, and the collection of spaces </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{s</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\geq</span></span><span> 0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> forms a scale of Hilbert spaces central to the analysis of parabolic evolution equations. Sobolev embedding theorems apply with minor modifications to the curved setting; for instance, any function in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s&gt;1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> on a two-dimensional manifold is continuous, with Hölder exponents depending on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>s</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and the geometry of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{adams2003sobolev}. These embeddings play a significant role in establishing that the nonlinearities in the field equations map </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to itself and are locally Lipschitz.

The Laplace--Beltrami operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is defined by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g f = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{div}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and is elliptic, self-adjoint, and negative semidefinite on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with domain </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Its spectrum consists of a sequence of real eigenvalues 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
0 = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nearrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with each eigenvalue appearing with finite multiplicity. The associated eigenfunctions form an orthonormal basis for </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, allowing every square-integrable function to be expanded as a Fourier series on the manifold. Weyl's law provides the asymptotic growth of eigenvalues, stating that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> 4</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span> n / </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{Area}(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> in two dimensions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{chavel1984eigenvalues}. This asymptotic plays a crucial role in determining the temporal frequency spacing of the eigenmodes that emerge in resting-state dynamics, which appear experimentally as standing-wave patterns </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Cabral2023, Roberts2019}. The resonance frequencies of these eigenmodes lie in a hierarchy reminiscent of the organization of cortical oscillations documented in electrophysiology </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Buzsaki2006, Singer1999}.

Geodesic curvature and mean curvature enter implicitly in several of the estimates used in the analysis, particularly when bounding the distortion introduced by coordinate charts and when controlling the constants appearing in elliptic regularity theorems. On a surface embedded in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>3</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, curvature variations induce local geometric anisotropies that subtly affect the propagation and interaction of fields. Nevertheless, the boundedness of the curvature tensor ensures that the constants appearing in Sobolev embeddings, Poincaré inequalities, and heat kernel estimates remain finite and depend smoothly on geometric parameters. The heat kernel associated with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> exhibits Gaussian upper and lower bounds, a property that provides precise control over the smoothing effect of the diffusion tensor in the field equations and has been extensively applied in the analysis of biological neural fields </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Breakspear2017, Deco2009}.

The vector field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> evolves within the tangent bundle </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>TM</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and the differential operators acting upon it must respect the Levi-Civita connection. The covariant derivative </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> replaces the ordinary gradient, and the divergence </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is defined through the metric in the standard fashion. Vorticity, expressed as </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, is interpreted through the Hodge star operator and the exterior derivative acting on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-forms, allowing the curl to be represented as a scalar field on a two-dimensional manifold. The interaction terms in the evolution equations, such as </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> or </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, therefore inherit a geometric structure that remains faithful to the intrinsic curvature of the cortical sheet. Because the connection is torsion-free, these operations commute with one another in ways that simplify several analytical estimates.

The Riemannian volume form </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> defines the integration measure on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and is used extensively in energy estimates. The Poincaré inequality for </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> provides a bound on the </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> norm of any zero-mean function in terms of its gradient, with a constant depending on the first nonzero eigenvalue </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. This inequality plays an important role in establishing the dissipative property of the diffusion term, as the latter controls the norm of the gradient and therefore indirectly controls the </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> norm of the field itself. The interplay between diffusion and curvature is subtle but essential for guaranteeing that the field equations do not exhibit blow-up in finite time. 

Finally, the geometric structure of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> influences the behavior of amplistwistors. Each amplistwistor acts pointwise on the values of the field but interacts with geometry through the projection and twist operations, which may depend on spatial location via </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> SO(d+2)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> or through local geometric features. While amplistwistors do not explicitly involve the Levi-Civita connection, the interpolation of their action across </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> necessarily invokes geometric properties because global regularity is understood in the Sobolev sense. Approximation arguments using partitions of unity require that the atlas of coordinate charts satisfy specific regularity conditions related to curvature and injectivity radius. Thus, the behavior of amplistwistor cascades cannot be fully understood without reference to the underlying geometry.

These considerations establish the mathematical foundation required for the analysis of field dynamics on the cortical surface. They ensure that the field equations are well-posed in a geometrically consistent setting and that amplistwistor cascades possess the necessary degrees of freedom to operate effectively on functions defined over a curved manifold. They also provide the structural assumptions needed to justify the use of eigenmode decompositions in subsequent sections, where spectral analysis becomes central to understanding the temporal and dynamical organization of cortical activity.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
