6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Spectral Theory of the Cortical Manifold}

The spectral structure of the Laplace--Beltrami operator plays a central role in understanding the dynamics of fields defined on the cortical surface. The manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> supports a discrete orthonormal basis of eigenfunctions that encode its geometric and topological properties, and these eigenfunctions serve as the natural coordinates for expressing large-scale patterns of neural activity. The empirical relevance of such spectral decompositions has been extensively documented in studies of resting-state and task-related cortical dynamics, where intrinsic oscillatory modes emerge as principal components of observed activation patterns </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Cabral2023, Roberts2019, Breakspear2017, Deco2009}. The purpose of this section is to develop the mathematical framework for spectral analysis on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and to elucidate the relation between eigenmodes and the amplitwistorial field dynamics described in preceding sections.

The Laplace--Beltrami operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is an elliptic, self-adjoint operator on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with compact resolvent, and therefore possesses a countable collection of eigenvalues with finite multiplicity. These eigenvalues form an increasing sequence
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
0 = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
each associated with an eigenfunction </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> satisfying </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n = 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The eigenfunctions may be taken to form an orthonormal basis for </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and by elliptic regularity they are smooth </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{aubin1998some, taylor2011partial}. The leading eigenfunction </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, associated with eigenvalue </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0=0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, is constant on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> because </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is connected. Higher eigenfunctions exhibit progressively finer spatial variation corresponding to geometric features of the manifold. The completeness of the eigenfunction basis allows any square-integrable scalar field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> on </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to be expanded as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
u = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n=0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> u, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with unconditional convergence in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

The asymptotic behavior of the eigenvalues provides insight into the spatial frequency structure of the manifold. Weyl's law states that, in two dimensions,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Lambda</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\#</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>n : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Lambda</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{Area}(M)}{4</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Lambda</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
and consequently </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> 4</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span> n / </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\operatorname</span></span><span>{Area}(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> as </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{chavel1984eigenvalues}. This linear growth in the index aligns with the spectral distribution of wave modes in empirical neuroimaging, which often display linearly spaced peaks in the low-frequency bands </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Cabral2023, Roberts2019}. The eigenfunctions themselves inherit geometric information from the curvature of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, with their nodal sets and oscillation patterns exhibiting sensitivity to local distortions of the cortical sheet. This sensitivity creates a bridge between spectral geometry and functional neuroanatomy, as observed in the organization of resting-state networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Honey2010}.

The minimax principle provides a variational characterization of the eigenvalues. For </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\geq</span></span><span> 1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\substack</span></span><span>{V</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subset</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1(M) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dim</span></span><span> V = k+1}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\substack</span></span><span>{u</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> V </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span> u </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neq</span></span><span> 0}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g u|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This characterization implies that the eigenfunctions minimize the Dirichlet energy subject to orthogonality constraints. In the context of the cortical manifold, the minimax principle highlights the connection between spatial smoothness and modal amplitude. Lower modes exhibit smoother spatial structure and therefore lower energy, corresponding to the coherent large-scale activation patterns observed during resting-state and low-frequency oscillations </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Breakspear2017, Buzsaki2006}. Higher modes exhibit finer spatial detail, corresponding to more localized processes.

Eigenfunctions of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> form the basis for analyzing the dynamics of vector and tensor fields through Hodge theory. The Hodge Laplacian acting on one-forms, defined by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span> = (d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> d)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, shares the same spectral properties as the scalar Laplacian. Vector fields may be decomposed into a curl-free component, a divergence-free component, and a harmonic component. In two dimensions the harmonic component is trivial unless the manifold has nonzero genus. Because the cortex is topologically equivalent to a sphere with handles, harmonic vector fields exist and are intimately connected to the topology of the surface </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{jost2008riemannian}. This decomposition parallels the division of cortical dynamics into gradient-like, rotational, and global integrative processes.

Spectral theory provides a natural mechanism for describing the macroscopic oscillatory modes observed experimentally. Ultrafast fMRI has revealed the existence of spatiotemporal patterns consistent with standing waves at discrete frequencies </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Cabral2023}. These patterns correspond to coherent superpositions of Laplace--Beltrami eigenfunctions, modulated over time by oscillations of the form </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Although the Laplace--Beltrami operator itself does not prescribe temporal dynamics, the eigenfunctions provide the spatial templates for modes arising from second-order or damped wave equations. Models based on structural connectivity matrices, as in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Breakspear2017, Deco2009}, use the graph Laplacian of large-scale cortical networks, whose spectral properties approximate those of the continuous Laplace--Beltrami operator when the graph is sampled with sufficient density. This correspondence explains why eigenmodes derived from anatomical connectivity mirror those obtained from functional imaging </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Honey2010}.

Heat kernel estimates further illuminate the temporal evolution of fields. The heat kernel </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K(t,x,y)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> associated with </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> satisfies Gaussian upper and lower bounds on compact manifolds, with constants depending on curvature and injectivity radius. The heat kernel provides the integral representation
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g} f(x) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M K(t,x,y) f(y)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This representation reveals the smoothing effect of diffusion and plays a central role in the semigroup analysis previously discussed. The spectral expansion of the heat kernel,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
K(t,x,y) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n=0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span> e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n t} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n(y),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
links diffusion dynamics directly to the Laplacian eigenstructure. In the limit of small </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the kernel concentrates around the diagonal, reflecting local geometric properties, while for large </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> it becomes dominated by the leading eigenfunctions, reflecting global structure. These properties align well with empirical observations that early sensory processing exhibits local patterns, whereas high-level integrative processes display global coherence </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Goldstein2025}.

Spectral theory also interacts naturally with the amplitwistorial framework. Amplistwistors operate pointwise but yield global transformation properties through the harmonics of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. When a field is expanded in eigenfunctions, an amplistwistor cascade acts by mixing coefficients in nonlinear ways. The twist operator, taking values in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>SO(d+2)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, induces rotations within the coefficient space; the amplitude operator introduces nonlinear distortions; and the projection operator shapes the lower-dimensional embedding. This produces a high-dimensional nonlinear transformation in the spectral domain, analogous to deep nonlinear operators in function spaces studied in the context of neural networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Poggio1990, Hornik1991}. The interplay between eigenmodes and nonlinear transformations helps to explain how cortical dynamics can combine local nonlinear interactions with global resonance structures.

The spectral properties of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> therefore furnish a comprehensive mathematical basis for understanding the organization of cortical dynamics. They justify the use of eigenmode decompositions in theoretical and empirical analysis and provide the analytical machinery required for characterizing the evolution of complex field configurations. The interaction between spectral geometry, nonlinear dynamics, and biological architecture forms the foundation of the theoretical edifice to be developed in the subsequent sections, where spectral decompositions are used to analyze temporal evolution, resonance phenomena, and large-scale coordination across cortical regions.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
