6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Spectral Geometry and the Hierarchy of Language Processing}

The preceding sections have established the geometric and spectral properties of the cortical manifold and the algebraic structure of the nonlinear field transformations that operate on it. The purpose of the current section is to relate this mathematical framework to the hierarchical temporal organization of language processing in the human brain. Recent electrocorticographic studies have revealed a precise correspondence between the depth of representation in large language models and the timing of neural responses across cortical regions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Goldstein2025}. These findings provide empirical support for the notion that linguistic computation is implemented through a cascade of nonlinear transformations that unfold over time, with early sensory regions responding rapidly to surfaces of the linguistic signal and higher-order regions integrating information over progressively longer temporal windows. The spectral geometry of the cortical manifold provides the natural setting in which this hierarchy can be situated.

The study by Goldstein and colleagues demonstrates that deeper layers of LLMs correspond to neural responses that occur with greater latency in higher-order regions such as the inferior frontal gyrus and the temporal pole. These regions exhibit temporal receptive windows extending up to several hundred milliseconds, far exceeding the rapid onsets seen in early auditory cortex. The observed monotonic relationship between layer depth and response latency suggests that linguistic representations are constructed through successive nonlinear transformations, each introducing additional semantic complexity. In the mathematical framework developed earlier, these transformations are modeled by cascades of amplitwistorial operators acting on the evolving field </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, with the temporal evolution governed by a semilinear parabolic system that smooths and integrates the output of each transformation.

Eigenmode analysis provides the key mechanism for linking these hierarchical transformations to the spatiotemporal dynamics of the cortex. Low-frequency eigenmodes of the Laplace--Beltrami operator produce large-scale patterns with slow temporal variation, while higher modes produce finer spatial structure with faster temporal profiles. Empirical studies of ultrafast fMRI have shown that these eigenmodes manifest as intrinsic standing waves throughout the cortex </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Cabral2023, Roberts2019}. Modes with the smallest eigenvalues shape the global organization of cortical activity, and their dominance in late temporal windows accords with the increasing integration time required for high-level semantic processing. In contrast, early sensory processing corresponds to the rapid activation of higher eigenmodes driven by localized sensory input.

The hierarchical structure of linguistic processing can therefore be interpreted as a temporal progression from higher to lower eigenvalue components of the field. In the early stages of processing, the field is dominated by high-frequency modes whose spatial patterns reflect local sensory inputs and whose temporal dynamics evolve rapidly. As time progresses, diffusion via the semigroup </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> attenuates the high-frequency components and amplifies the contribution of lower modes. This produces the temporal dilation of receptive fields observed in auditory association cortex and higher-order language regions. The mathematical mechanism underlying this dilation is classical: the exponential decay </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n t}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> suppresses high-frequency components more rapidly than low-frequency ones, effectively integrating information over increasing spatial and temporal scales.

This spectral mechanism aligns with the nonlinear amplification introduced by amplitwistorial transformations. Each amplistwistor applies a nonlinear map that mixes modes in a manner analogous to the action of a deep neural network on a finite-dimensional vector. Because the twist operator acts as an element of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>SO(d+2)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, it rotates the spectral coefficients in a way that preserves their norm while redistributing energy across modes. The amplitude operator introduces nonlinear distortions that amplify or dampen particular spectral components depending on the current state of the field. The projection operator shapes the outcome by constraining the field to a lower-dimensional feature subspace. These interactions produce a complex sequence of transformations that progressively reshape the spectral structure of the field, ultimately yielding the high-level representations observed in late semantic processing.

One of the most striking findings in the LLM--ECoG study is the failure of symbolic linguistic models to reproduce the temporal hierarchy observed in the brain. Classical models based on phonemes, morphemes, or syntactic structures do not exhibit the layered temporal structure seen in biological systems. Symbolic decomposition imposes sharp boundaries between linguistic units that do not align with the continuous, graded structure of cortical representations. The spectral framework developed here provides a natural explanation: symbolic models do not operate within a function space endowed with a rich geometric and spectral structure, and therefore lack the capacity to represent the multiscale, nonlinear transformations required for temporal integration.

The parabolic evolution equations describing the dynamics of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> provide another link to experimental findings. These equations exhibit a natural hierarchy of temporal scales encoded by the eigenvalue spectrum of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. High-frequency components evolve rapidly and decay quickly; low-frequency components evolve slowly and persist longer. This hierarchy maps directly onto the functional gradient from primary auditory regions to prefrontal and temporal pole regions. The slow evolution of low-frequency components yields long integration windows in higher-order cortex, consistent with the extended temporal receptive fields observed in semantic processing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Goldstein2025}. Such slow dynamics are also predicted by models of large-scale brain activity that incorporate conduction delays, recurrent excitation, and diffusion on structural connectivity graphs </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Breakspear2017, Deco2009}. These models frequently reduce to spectral decompositions of graph Laplacians whose eigenmodes approximate the continuous eigenfunctions of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Honey2010}.

Nonlinear amplification plays an essential role in the emergence of hierarchical structure. Without the nonlinear coupling terms in the evolution equation, the field would evolve linearly under the action of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and no hierarchical, depth-dependent transformation analogous to that in deep networks would occur. Control analyses conducted in the LLM--ECoG study demonstrate precisely this phenomenon: linear interpolation models fail to reproduce the monotonic relationship between depth of representation and response latency. This observation corroborates the necessity of nonlinear transformations, which in the present framework arise from the amplitude and projection components of the amplistwistor. These components introduce nonlinearities that enable the field to represent complex semantic structures and to do so in a temporally ordered fashion.

The spectral geometry of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> therefore furnishes a coherent mathematical account of the hierarchical organization of language processing. It explains how successive transformations of the cortical field produce increasingly abstract representations over longer temporal windows; how nonlinear interactions amplify, redirect, and suppress spectral components; and how the intrinsic structure of the manifold shapes the global organization of neural activity. The correspondence between the spectral dynamics of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and the deep layer hierarchy of LLMs provides compelling evidence that the brain's language system implements a form of deep, nonlinear, spatiotemporally structured computation. The nonlinear field dynamics proposed in earlier sections supply the mathematical mechanisms for realizing this computation, while the eigenmodes of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> supply the spatial scaffolding on which it unfolds.

These considerations set the stage for the next section, which will examine how the spectral organization of the cortical manifold interacts with empirical resonant modes observed in electrophysiology and neuroimaging to produce the coherent large-scale temporal structure characteristic of cognitive processing.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
