6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Predictions and Experimental Paradigms for Testing the Amplitwistor Cascade Model}

The theoretical and empirical foundations established in the preceding sections yield a series of concrete predictions regarding the neural dynamics underlying naturalistic language processing. These predictions arise not as post hoc accommodations but as direct mathematical consequences of the spectral--geometric framework and its nonlinear amplitwistor evolution equations. A crucial feature of the model is that it links structural geometry, intrinsic oscillatory modes, and nonlinear field transformations into a unified account of cortical computation. This interdependence imposes constraints on observable neural activity, many of which have not yet been empirically examined but are within reach of current experimental technologies.

The first major prediction concerns the spectral signatures of amplitude--twist interactions during linguistic processing. The nonlinear evolution equation derived in Section~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:semilinear</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>evolution} implies that eigenmodes of the Laplace--Beltrami operator couple through the amplitude and projection components of the transformation. This coupling induces characteristic cross-frequency interactions, particularly phase--amplitude and amplitude--amplitude coupling between low-frequency eigenmodes and high-frequency components localized to specific cortical subregions. Electrophysiological studies have repeatedly observed such interactions during language comprehension, especially in tasks involving syntactic integration, semantic ambiguity, and narrative coherence tracking </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Buzsaki2006, Singer1999}. The amplitwistor model predicts that these interactions should exhibit a structured dependency on the eigenvalue spectrum: lower-frequency modes should modulate the gain of higher-frequency oscillations in proportion to the geometric overlap of their eigenfunctions. This prediction can be tested using high-density MEG or intracranial EEG through spectral decomposition and cross-frequency coupling analyses applied to continuous language stimuli.

A second prediction concerns the effect of stimulus predictability on spectral evolution. Empirical work has shown that unexpected words evoke stronger late neural responses, including enhanced activity in theta and delta bands and increased amplitude in components such as the N400 and P600 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Elman1990, Friston2005}. In the present framework, prediction error corresponds to an increase in the entropy component of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, which enhances the influence of nonlinear terms in the evolution equation. This mechanism predicts that surprising words should induce transient expansion of the spectral representation, measurable as a brief increase in the number or amplitude of active eigenmodes. Ultrafast fMRI is well suited to detect such effects, as it can capture shifts in standing-wave configurations on the timescale of hundreds of milliseconds </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Cabral2023}. Combining fMRI with simultaneous EEG or MEG would allow for direct correlation between spectral broadening and eigenmode activation.

The geometric structure of the cortical manifold also yields predictions regarding the spatial localization of semantic integration. Because low-lying eigenmodes have broad spatial support, regions with strong overlap with these modes should exhibit the longest integration windows and the strongest correlation with deep representational layers in LLMs. The findings of Goldstein and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Goldstein2025} demonstrate that the inferior frontal gyrus and the temporal pole exhibit these properties, consistent with their alignment with the lowest-frequency eigenfunctions identified in resting-state and task-based imaging </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Roberts2019}. This alignment should extend beyond language tasks, appearing whenever semantic or conceptual integration occurs. Comparative studies of narrative comprehension, logical reasoning, and conceptual metaphor processing could be used to test whether these same regions display spectral signatures consistent with low-eigenmode dominance. Network diffusion models based on structural connectomes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Honey2010} predict the same anatomical pattern, suggesting a tight coupling between geometry and computation.

The model also predicts specific dynamical responses to perturbations of neural activity. Because nonlinear coupling between eigenmodes plays a central role in amplitwistor evolution, perturbation of a single mode should alter the trajectory of the entire spectral representation. Optogenetic stimulation studies in rodents have shown that targeted activation of cortical regions produces traveling waves and global network reconfiguration consistent with eigenmode superposition </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Breakspear2017}. Transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) in humans offers an analogous method for probing the geometry of language circuits. Applying rhythmic TMS at frequencies resonant with specific eigenmodes should enhance or disrupt linguistic integration in a manner that depends on the overlap between the targeted region and the eigenfunction support. This yields a direct causal test: stimulation aligned with low-frequency eigenmodes should impair long-timescale semantic processing, whereas stimulation aligned with higher-frequency components should preferentially affect sensory or phonological processing.

A further set of predictions arises from the model's treatment of recurrence and temporal dilation. The correlation between LLM layer depth and neural latency observed in electrocorticography provides strong initial evidence that linguistic computation unfolds through a sequence of nonlinear transformations </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Goldstein2025}. The amplitwistor cascade model refines this account by predicting a quantitative relationship between the temporal derivative of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and the spectral radius of the active representation. Specifically, deeper semantic processing should correspond to increased spectral radius and reduced temporal derivative magnitude as the system approaches a low-frequency attractor. This prediction could be tested by examining the instantaneous frequency content of intracranial recordings during syntactically complex sentences or narrative shifts requiring deep semantic updates.

The spatial geometry of the cortex also suggests predictions about inter-individual variability. Because the eigenvalue spectrum of the cortical surface depends on geometric properties such as curvature, gyrification, and surface area, individuals with atypical cortical geometry should exhibit systematically shifted spectral hierarchies. This prediction is supported by findings in developmental and clinical populations, where differences in cortical folding patterns correlate with language impairments, autism spectrum traits, and dyslexia. A spectral analysis of cortical surfaces extracted from structural MRI could be paired with electrophysiology or MEG to test whether these geometric variations correspond to altered eigenmode hierarchies and changes in temporal integration windows.

Finally, the model predicts that artificial systems implementing amplitwistor-like transformations will more closely match neural data than standard feed-forward architectures. Recent work in deep learning has shown that recurrent or hybrid models capture temporal dependencies in language more effectively than purely feed-forward Transformers </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\citep</span></span><span>{Saxe2013, Vaswani2017}. Embedding amplitwistorial operations into the architecture of neural networks would allow for a more biologically plausible representation of semantic evolution. Such models should exhibit temporal receptive fields and spectral coupling patterns resembling those measured in the brain, providing an experimental pathway for in silico validation of the theory. Because the amplitwistor framework includes nonlinear, geometrically grounded transformations that mimic cortical operations, its implementation in artificial systems offers a means of testing the role of geometry in semantic computation.

Taken together, these predictions provide a comprehensive framework for empirical investigation of the amplitwistor cascade model. They encompass spatial geometry, spectral dynamics, cross-frequency coupling, perturbation responses, inter-individual variability, and artificial model alignment. They also rely on a diverse set of experimental methodologies, including intracranial ECoG, MEG, EEG, ultrafast fMRI, structural MRI, and causal perturbation techniques such as TMS. The convergence of these modalities offers an unprecedented opportunity to evaluate the viability of a unified spectral--geometric account of cortical language computation. The next section introduces a computational framework for implementing these ideas in numerical simulations, thereby providing a bridge between mathematical theory and biological data.
