6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Numerical Methods for Simulating Amplitwistor Dynamics on Cortical Manifolds}

The mathematical theory developed in earlier sections provides a rigorous foundation for the amplitwistor evolution equation, yet the complexity of the underlying operators necessitates careful numerical treatment. The present section describes the computational methods used to approximate solutions of the semilinear parabolic system defined on the cortical manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. These methods draw upon established techniques in numerical analysis of partial differential equations, spectral geometry, and finite element discretization on curved surfaces, adapted to the unique demands of nonlinear amplitwistorial transformations. Their purpose is not merely to produce numerical outputs, but to provide a constructive bridge between the geometric theory of Section~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:geometry</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>manifold} and the empirical predictions established in Sections~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:empirical</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>validation} and~</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ref</span></span><span>{sec:predictions</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>experiments}.

The representation of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is a central concern. In practice, the cortical manifold is reconstructed from high-resolution structural MRI using algorithms that extract a triangulated surface approximating the white--gray matter boundary. This triangulation forms the basis of a finite element space </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>V</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> consisting of continuous, piecewise-linear functions defined on the mesh. The Laplace--Beltrami operator is discretized using the standard cotangent formula, producing a sparse symmetric matrix </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> whose spectrum approximates the true eigenvalues of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. Convergence of the discrete spectrum to the continuous spectrum is guaranteed under mild mesh regularity conditions, and error estimates for the eigenpairs follow from classical results in geometric finite element theory. For numerical evolution, it is often advantageous to compute the first several hundred eigenfunctions of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, as these provide an efficient basis for spectral methods. The discrete eigenfunctions are orthonormalized with respect to the mass matrix to ensure numerical stability.

The amplitwistor dynamics involve a combination of linear diffusion, geometric mode coupling, and nonlinear local transformations. These components evolve on different scales, making operator splitting particularly effective. The evolution over a timestep </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is decomposed into a linear diffusion step and a nonlinear amplitwistor step. The diffusion component is advanced using an implicit Euler or Crank--Nicolson scheme, both of which provide unconditional stability for parabolic systems. If </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denotes the discrete field at time </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, the diffusion update solves the linear system
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(I - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where the star denotes an intermediate state. The sparsity and symmetry of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>I - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> t L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> permit efficient solution using conjugate gradient methods with algebraic multigrid preconditioning. Alternatively, in a spectral Galerkin implementation, the diffusion step acts diagonally on the coefficients of the eigenbasis, multiplying the </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>-th coefficient by </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exp</span></span><span>(-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.

The nonlinear amplitwistor transformation presents different challenges. Because amplitwistors act pointwise in the spatial domain, but nonlinearly on the vector-valued field, their numerical implementation must ensure that the amplification, rotation, and projection operations preserve smoothness while avoiding numerical instabilities. The amplification function </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is computed at each mesh vertex, and its derivative with respect to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is monitored to guarantee that the amplification does not exceed Lipschitz bounds necessary for stability. The rotation operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is evaluated via matrix exponentials when the transformation is small, or via polar decomposition when large twisting is required. Numerical stability demands that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> remain orthogonal up to machine precision; thus, a QR re-orthogonalization step is applied after each evaluation. The projection operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is implemented either as a soft-thresholding map or as an orthogonal projection onto a low-dimensional subspace, depending on the biological interpretation adopted for the simulation.

The nonlinear update for each vertex </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> in the mesh then takes the form
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{n+1}(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ast</span></span><span>(x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
which is evaluated independently at each vertex, enabling straightforward parallelization on multicore CPUs or GPUs. To ensure global stability of the scheme, the timestep </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is chosen according to the maximum Lipschitz constant of the nonlinear operator over the domain. For meshes of realistic cortical complexity, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> in the range of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>10</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-3}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>10</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-2}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> seconds has proven effective.

An alternative approach employs a purely spectral discretization. In this method, the field is represented as a truncated expansion
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h(t, x) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>K c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(x),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> are the discrete eigenfunctions. The diffusion component evolves according to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{c}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, yielding the closed-form update </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> t) = c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t)e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>. The nonlinear amplitwistor transformation is then applied to the reconstructed field in physical space, followed by projection back onto the span of the first </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> eigenfunctions. Spectral methods exhibit exponential accuracy when the solution is smooth, and they provide a natural way to analyze the interplay between eigenmode amplitudes. However, they are computationally intensive due to repeated forward and inverse transforms between spectral and spatial domains. Nonetheless, for theoretical explorations of eigenmode coupling, such methods are invaluable.

The numerical schemes described above must be validated to ensure fidelity to the theoretical model. Convergence studies are conducted by refining the mesh, increasing the spectral truncation order, and reducing the timestep. Empirical results confirm the expected convergence rates for parabolic semilinear equations, consistent with classical theory. Additional validation comes from reproducing empirical neural phenomena such as traveling waves, cross-frequency coupling, and eigenmode activation patterns observed in fMRI and MEG. The comparison between simulation and experiment is performed in both the spatial domain and the spectral domain, using power spectral density estimates, coherence analysis, and eigenmode decomposition of the simulated fields.

Finally, the numerical framework provides a platform for exploring parameter regimes inaccessible to direct experimentation. By varying the coefficients of the amplitwistor transformation, one can investigate transitions between stable semantic integration, oscillatory behavior, and chaotic regimes. These transitions correspond to bifurcations in the underlying dynamical system and suggest potential mechanisms for cognitive disruptions associated with neurological disorders. The ability to systematically vary parameters in silico provides an indispensable complement to empirical work, enabling a deeper understanding of the dynamical landscape underlying cortical computation.

In summary, the numerical methods presented here render the amplitwistor cascade model operational. Through a combination of finite element discretization, spectral analysis, operator splitting, and nonlinear pointwise updates, the theory becomes a computationally tractable system capable of producing detailed predictions that can be compared directly with neural data. The integration of geometric, spectral, and nonlinear components into a unified computational pipeline reflects the essential structure of the underlying theory, bringing mathematical precision to the study of cortical dynamics.
