The theoretical and numerical foundations established in the preceding sections permit the systematic exploration of amplitwistor dynamics under controlled conditions. The purpose of the simulations presented in this section is not merely illustrative but rather to provide a computational instantiation of the mathematical theory on a geometric domain approximating the cortical surface. In particular, the simulations clarify how amplitwistor cascades evolve on a curved two-dimensional manifold embedded in three-dimensional space, how eigenmodes of the Laplace--Beltrami operator mediate long-range structure, and how nonlinear interactions between amplification, twisting, and projection give rise to coherent spatiotemporal patterns that correspond to experimentally observed neural phenomena. Although the simulations are numerical, their design follows directly from the functional analytic results presented earlier, ensuring that the computational behavior arises from principled approximations of the underlying continuum dynamics.

The cortical manifold used in the simulations is obtained from a high-resolution T1-weighted MRI volume. A standard reconstruction pipeline extracts the gray--white matter boundary, producing a triangulated mesh with approximately </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>160{,}000</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> vertices. The fidelity of the simulation depends critically on the geometric accuracy of this surface, since even small perturbations of curvature influence the eigenstructure of the Laplace--Beltrami operator. After reconstruction, the mesh is smoothed using curvature-preserving algorithms to reduce numerical stiffness, yet its fine-scale gyrification is retained to ensure realistic eigenmode geometry. The first </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> eigenfunctions of the discrete Laplace--Beltrami operator, computed using the cotangent-weighted finite element method, form the spectral basis for the subsequent analysis. In all simulations presented here, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> ranges from </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>250</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> to </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>1500</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, permitting the representation of patterns spanning from coarse distributed modes to finer gyral-scale structures.

The amplitwistor field is initialized in several distinct configurations in order to probe different dynamical regimes. In the simplest case, the initial field is a smooth, broad function supported across the temporal lobe, intended to emulate the onset of a linguistic stimulus. Under amplitwistor evolution, this field contracts, twists, and reorients in ways that reflect the roles of amplification, rotation, and projection in the nonlinear operator. The diffusion term ensures that highly localized features spread over time, while the twisting and amplification terms introduce nontrivial geometric deformations that cannot be captured by linear diffusion alone. A persistent observation across all simulations is that twisting promotes alignment of the field along principal eigenmodes of the manifold, effectively channeling semantic energy into spectrally preferred directions. This behavior provides a computational analogue of resonance alignment observed in empirical neuroimaging studies such as those of Cabral, Fernandes, and Shemesh, in which oscillatory modes of the cortex exhibit strong coherence across distant regions.

In a second class of simulations, the initial field is concentrated in the superior temporal gyrus and designed to carry a rapid temporal modulation, mimicking the high-frequency components associated with early speech processing. As the amplitwistor flow evolves, these localized high-frequency components decay under diffusion while simultaneously interacting with the twisting operator. The effect is a transference of energy from high-frequency local modes to lower-frequency global modes. This transfer closely resembles the empirically observed temporal integration hierarchy reported in electrocorticographic studies, particularly those by Goldstein and collaborators, where early auditory regions exhibit narrow temporal windows, while downstream regions such as the anterior temporal lobe and inferior frontal gyrus accumulate information over much longer timescales. In the simulations, this hierarchical expansion of temporal windows emerges naturally from the nonlinear couplings between the amplitwistor operators and the spectral geometry of the manifold, without requiring any region-specific parameter tuning.

The nonlinear interaction term also produces striking spatiotemporal choreography when the twisting operator is given a biologically realistic gain. In these regimes, the field initially exhibits transient traveling-wave-like behavior, propagating along sulcal trajectories. As time progresses, the twisting operator destabilizes simple wavefronts and induces a reorganization of the field into standing-wave patterns aligned with low-eigenvalue modes. These dynamics bear resemblance to the resting-state eigenmode decomposition reported in large-scale neuroimaging studies, including those of Roberts and co-authors, where the dominant modes are spatially extended, low-frequency, and highly coherent. In the simulation, the field settles into oscillatory attractors whose frequencies correspond closely to the dominant eigenvalues of the Laplace--Beltrami spectrum. The transitions between traveling and standing waveforms are particularly sensitive to the amplification coefficient. When amplification is weak, diffusion dominates and the solution rapidly converges toward the lowest eigenmode. When amplification is strong, nonlinear twisting counteracts diffusion and maintains the field in complex, multiscale oscillatory regimes, reminiscent of the mixed spectral behavior observed in cognitive tasks requiring flexible integration across timescales.

A further set of simulations examines how perturbations analogous to sensory prediction errors modify the amplitwistor evolution. These perturbations are introduced by adding a brief high-frequency pulse to the field, localized in either frontal or temporal regions. When applied to regions corresponding to early sensory areas, the perturbation dissipates quickly and has little long-term impact. However, when applied to high-order association areas, the perturbation interacts nonlinearly with the twisting operator and produces transient deviations in the spectral distribution of the field. These deviations manifest as brief oscillatory bursts at frequencies slightly above the manifold's intrinsic low-frequency modes. This phenomenon aligns closely with time–frequency patterns observed in magnetoencephalographic and intracranial recordings during unexpected linguistic input, particularly the modulations associated with the N400 event-related potential described in the literature on semantic processing. The simulations thus suggest that amplitwistor dynamics can reproduce not only stable resting-state patterns but also transient responses to unexpected stimuli.

Several simulations investigate the effects of manipulating the twisting gain parameter. As the magnitude of the twisting operator increases, the field enters a regime characterized by complex, quasi-periodic oscillations. These oscillations are localized in gyral crowns and decay more slowly in sulcal depths, owing to curvature-driven variations in eigenmode density. In these regimes, the field exhibits behavior reminiscent of cross-frequency coupling, a phenomenon widely documented in electrophysiological studies. Although the present model does not explicitly include frequency-specific nonlinearities, the interaction between twisting and the spectral geometry of the manifold naturally produces amplitude modulations at integer multiples of the base oscillation frequency. This suggests a purely geometric origin for certain forms of cross-frequency coupling observed in neural data, complementing theoretical accounts in the dynamical systems literature.

To quantify the agreement between the simulations and experimental measurements, synthetic electrocorticographic signals are computed by sampling the amplitwistor field at mesh vertices corresponding to electrode locations. These synthetic signals are then filtered and analyzed using the same time–frequency decomposition techniques applied in empirical studies. The correspondence between simulated and experimentally observed patterns is assessed in both the time domain and the spectral domain. Preliminary analyses indicate strong coherence between the simulated oscillatory modes and those reported in fMRI and MEG studies, including the resting-state eigenmodes identified by Honey, Thivierge, and Sporns, and the dynamic coupling patterns described by Breakspear. These results provide additional evidence that the amplitwistor framework captures essential features of cortical dynamics.

Taken together, these simulation studies demonstrate that the amplitwistor system can reproduce a wide range of phenomena associated with human cortical processing, from early sensory integration to long-range semantic coherence, from transient prediction-error responses to stable resting-state rhythms, and from region-specific temporal windows to global eigenmode alignment. The coherence between simulation results and neurophysiological data is remarkable given the simplicity of the underlying assumptions: the simulations rely solely on the geometric structure of the cortical manifold and the nonlinear amplitwistor operators, without introducing ad hoc region-specific parameters. This suggests that many of the complex patterns observed in neural recordings may emerge as natural consequences of geometric and dynamical principles, a perspective that aligns closely with theoretical accounts of cortical organization proposed in diverse strands of computational neuroscience.

In the sections that follow, these computational findings are integrated with empirical evidence from neuroimaging and electrophysiology to provide a unified account of cortical dynamics grounded in both mathematical theory and biological observation.
