6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Abstract}

This monograph develops a unified theoretical framework for cortical computation in which the dynamics of neural populations are modeled as nonlinear field transformations evolving on the geometry of the cortical surface. The central construct is the amplitwistor, a mathematically precise generalization of the classical amplitwist from complex analysis, which acts locally on cortical columns to implement a nonlinear combination of amplification, rotation, and projection. When composed repeatedly across the cortical sheet, these operators form cascades capable of universal function approximation. The dynamics of these cascades are embedded within a continuous field model defined on a Riemannian manifold corresponding to the cortical surface, yielding a system of partial differential equations that integrates local nonlinear transformations with global geometric constraints.

The theoretical structure is complemented by the TARTAN recursion operator, which introduces temporally extended interactions through trajectory-dependent updates and multiscale tiling. Together, the amplitwistor and TARTAN mechanisms produce a recursive field evolution whose temporal scales naturally align with empirical observations of cortical hierarchy. The mathematical analysis demonstrates well-posedness under appropriate conditions on the nonlinear terms, establishes existence and uniqueness of solutions via semigroup theory, and proves that cascaded amplitwistor compositions are dense in the space of continuous fields on the manifold. Spectral methods are used to examine the decomposition of the evolving field into eigenmodes of the Laplace--Beltrami operator, revealing that the nonlinear twisting term drives preferential alignment with low-frequency modes whose structure reflects the geometry of the cortical surface.

A major portion of the monograph examines empirical findings from electrocorticography, magnetoencephalography, electroencephalography, and ultrafast functional MRI. Recent studies have identified a systematic correspondence between the depth of layers in large language models and the temporal latency of neural responses in high-order cortical regions. These findings resonate strongly with the recursive temporal structure generated by amplitwistor dynamics, which predict that deeper transformations unfold over longer integration windows. Additional support comes from the discovery of standing-wave eigenmodes in spontaneous cortical activity, whose spatial structure closely matches the eigenfunctions predicted by the spectral geometry of the manifold. The model provides a coherent explanation for a range of phenomena, including transient oscillatory bursts associated with prediction error, the coexistence of traveling and standing waves, the expansion of temporal receptive windows along the ventral auditory stream, and the emergence of large-scale coherence patterns.

The framework also furnishes testable predictions concerning the spectral and temporal signatures of cortical processing, the modulation of eigenmodes under anesthesia, and the geometry of evoked responses during language comprehension. By connecting nonlinear functional analysis, differential geometry, and empirical neuroscience, this work advances a principled account of cortical computation that bridges local microcircuit operations with global dynamic structure. The resulting theory suggests new directions for both cognitive neuroscience and artificial intelligence, particularly in the design of architectures that emulate the recursive geometric transformations characteristic of biological neural systems.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
