6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Introduction}

Understanding how the cortex transforms sensory input into coherent perception, language, and thought remains one of the enduring challenges of neuroscience. Decades of electrophysiology, neuroimaging, and computational modeling have shown that cortical computation is neither purely symbolic nor reducible to elementary feedforward filters. Instead, it unfolds through a complex hierarchy of nonlinear dynamics, long-range coordination, and temporally extended integration. These observations have motivated a growing body of work that seeks to characterize the cortex not as a discrete network of units but as a geometrically constrained, continuously evolving dynamical system whose structure and function are inseparable from the topology and curvature of the cortical sheet </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017,deco2009,honey2010,roberts2019}. 

Recent empirical developments have sharpened this perspective. Ultrafast functional MRI studies have demonstrated that spontaneous neural activity decomposes into a small number of stationary eigenmodes resembling low-frequency standing waves on the cortical manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}. These patterns appear to be intrinsic to the geometry of the cortex and persist across species, anesthetic depth, and behavioral state. At finer temporal scales, invasive recordings reveal a rich mixture of traveling and standing waves, oscillatory bursts associated with prediction error, and temporally layered responses that scale systematically along the auditory and language hierarchies. Notably, electrocorticographic evidence now indicates that cortical responses to natural speech display a monotonic correspondence between depth in large language models and the latency of cortical activation, with deeper model layers aligning with progressively longer integration windows in the anterior temporal lobe and inferior frontal gyrus </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}. These observations underscore the need for a mathematical framework that can unify nonlinear recursion, geometric constraints, and hierarchical temporal dynamics.

The present work proposes such a framework by introducing amplitwistors as the fundamental local operators governing cortical computation. Inspired by the amplitwist construction from complex analysis </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{needham1997}, an amplitwistor combines amplification, rotation, and projection into a single nonlinear transformation. When these operators act within the microcircuit architecture of cortical columns, they generate local dynamics that resemble the small-scale nonlinearities observed in population recordings and synaptic interactions. When cascaded across the cortical sheet, they form a class of transformations capable of universal approximation on manifolds, generalizing classical results in nonlinear function approximation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989,hornik1991,poggio1990}. The amplitwistor is therefore positioned as a biophysically interpretable, mathematically rigorous operator that links microcircuit dynamics with large-scale cortical behavior.

The amplitwistor dynamics are embedded within a continuous field-theoretic model defined on a Riemannian manifold approximating the cortical surface. This construction is informed by the extensive literature on spectral geometry and cortical eigenmodes, which has shown that the Laplace--Beltrami operator provides a natural basis for representing large-scale activity patterns </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019,honey2010}. Within this setting, the evolution of neural activity is governed by a system of nonlinear partial differential equations whose form reflects both the geometry of the cortex and the nonlinear character of local amplistwistor transformations. The resulting dynamics combine diffusion, rotation, amplification, and geometric projection, yielding a rich set of behaviors that reproduce known cortical phenomena while generating theoretically motivated predictions.

A key ingredient of the model is the TARTAN recursion operator, which introduces temporally extended dependencies through trajectory-aware updates, multiscale tiling, and annotated noise. This component provides a principled mechanism for implementing the recursive temporal structure that appears to underlie both human language processing and deep neural architectures. Unlike purely feedforward systems, the TARTAN operator endows the field with a form of history dependence that manifests in the progressive dilation of integration windows along cortical hierarchies. This property corresponds closely to empirical observations from speech comprehension studies, where high-order regions exhibit latency profiles extending hundreds of milliseconds beyond those of early auditory cortex </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{elman1990,friston</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>hierarchy}.

The overarching objective of this monograph is to integrate these strands into a coherent mathematical and neurophysiological account of cortical computation. The analysis proceeds in several stages. First, we review the neuroscientific and geometric background necessary to formulate the model, including cortical microarchitecture, spectral geometry, and the physics of standing-wave modes. We then introduce the amplitwistor operator and develop its mathematical properties as a nonlinear transformation acting on fields defined over a manifold. This machinery is used to derive a system of partial differential equations describing the evolution of neural activity, and we establish conditions for well-posedness and semigroup generation using tools from functional analysis. Building on these results, we prove that cascades of amplitwistors form universal approximators on the cortical manifold, extending classical theorems to the manifold-valued setting. 

The second half of the monograph situates the theoretical framework within the empirical landscape. We examine the correspondence between amplitwistor dynamics and recent electrocorticographic findings on hierarchical language processing, drawing explicit connections to the temporal structure of large language models. We analyze the relationship between the model and the standing-wave eigenmodes identified in ultrafast fMRI, as well as the implications for wave propagation, cross-frequency coupling, and the geometry of prediction error responses. Simulations on high-resolution cortical meshes demonstrate how the field evolves under varying parameter regimes and reproduce several known signatures of cortical activity, including mode alignment, transient oscillatory bursts, and the coexistence of traveling and stationary waveforms. Finally, we outline a set of experimentally testable predictions derived from the model, ranging from spectral and spatial signatures to perturbation responses and anesthesia effects.

By combining geometric analysis, nonlinear dynamics, and empirical neuroscience, this work aims to articulate a principled mathematical foundation for cortical computation that bridges the gap between microcircuit mechanisms and large-scale cortical organization. The amplitwistor framework offers a unified perspective on how the cortex blends local nonlinearities with global geometric constraints to generate the complex, multi-scale patterns that characterize perception, language, and cognition. The hope is that this synthesis will open new directions in both theoretical neuroscience and artificial intelligence, particularly in the development of computational architectures that emulate the recursive geometric transformations inherent in biological neural systems.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
