6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Neuroscience of Cortical Dynamics}

A comprehensive mathematical account of cortical computation must begin with an examination of the biological substrate on which these computations unfold. The mammalian cerebral cortex is neither a homogeneous sheet nor a collection of isolated processing units. Rather, it is a hierarchically organized, intricately folded, and richly interconnected geometric surface whose dynamical properties reflect both its microcircuit structure and its large-scale architectural constraints. This section reviews several core principles of cortical organization that motivate the amplitwistor framework formulated in subsequent chapters.

At the smallest relevant scale, cortical processing is mediated by columnar microcircuits whose structure is highly conserved across species. Each column comprises a vertically oriented ensemble of excitatory and inhibitory neurons whose synaptic connections generate nonlinear transformations of incoming signals. These microcircuits exhibit recurrent excitation, layered inhibition, and modulatory gain control, yielding rich local dynamics that include amplification, normalization, and phase-sensitive integration. Although there has been debate regarding the universality of columns as a canonical computational unit, converging evidence from electrophysiology and modeling suggests that they support a common repertoire of transformations across sensory and association cortices. These local microcircuits provide the biological substrate for the amplitwistor operators introduced later in the monograph.

Beyond the microcircuit level, cortical computation is shaped profoundly by oscillatory dynamics. Neural populations exhibit rhythmic activity across a wide range of frequencies, from the slow fluctuations of the delta band to the fast oscillations of the gamma band. The functional significance of these rhythms has been explored extensively in the literature on neural synchrony, most notably in the work of Singer, who argued that oscillatory phase alignment supports the binding of distributed neural representations </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999}. Complementary perspectives have emerged from the dynamical systems literature, which emphasizes the role of oscillations in structuring communication across large-scale networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017}. Oscillatory coherence, phase-amplitude coupling, and cross-frequency interactions are now widely recognized as key mechanisms in temporal prediction, hierarchical inference, and information gating.

One of the most striking and robust findings across modalities is the presence of traveling and standing waves in cortical activity. Electrocorticographic recordings reveal waves that propagate along specific sulcal and gyral trajectories, often aligned with gradients in anatomical connectivity. At the same time, large-scale imaging modalities, particularly ultrafast functional MRI, show that spontaneous cortical activity can be decomposed into stationary eigenmodes that behave as standing waves on the cortical manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}. These modes have spatial patterns that correspond closely to eigenfunctions of the Laplace--Beltrami operator on the cortical surface, suggesting that the geometry of the cortex constrains its intrinsic dynamics in a manner analogous to the vibrational modes of physical membranes. Analyses by Roberts and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} and by Honey, Thivierge, and Sporns </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{honey2010} demonstrate that these eigenmodes can account for a significant portion of resting-state functional connectivity, reinforcing the view that cortical dynamics are shaped not only by synaptic networks but also by geometric and physical principles.

In addition to these geometric influences, cortical dynamics exhibit pronounced hierarchical structure, particularly in the domain of auditory and linguistic processing. Early auditory cortex responds rapidly to acoustic features with integration windows on the order of tens of milliseconds, while higher-order temporal and frontal regions integrate information over progressively longer timescales extending to several hundred milliseconds. This temporal dilation has been documented in classical electrophysiological work and is reflected prominently in contemporary studies of narrative comprehension. Most recently, electrocorticographic recordings analyzed by Goldstein and collaborators revealed a systematic correspondence between the depth of layers in large language models such as GPT-2 and LLaMA and the latency of neural responses in high-order regions including the inferior frontal gyrus and the temporal pole </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}. The alignment between model depth and cortical temporal hierarchy suggests that recursive, layer-wise transformations play a fundamental role in human language processing.

The phenomenon of hierarchical temporal integration is not limited to language. Studies in vision, audition, and multisensory integration consistently demonstrate that cortical areas farther along processing pathways respond with longer latencies, integrate over broader temporal windows, and exhibit greater sensitivity to contextual and predictive information. These empirical observations resonate with theoretical frameworks such as hierarchical predictive coding, which posit that higher-order regions generate predictions that constrain early sensory processing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>hierarchy}. Although predictive coding formulations differ from the amplitwistor model in several respects, they share the core idea that cortical computation involves recursive transformations that unfold over multiple timescales.

A final component of cortical dynamics essential for the present work is the relationship between noise, stochastic fluctuations, and large-scale coherence. Neural systems operate in environments characterized by substantial stochasticity arising from synaptic variability, channel noise, and spontaneous population activity. Paradoxically, these fluctuations often enhance, rather than impair, cortical function. Deco, Jirsa, and collaborators have shown that noise-driven dynamics contribute to resting-state fluctuations and facilitate transitions between metastable states </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009}. These findings underscore the importance of incorporating noise into any realistic model of cortical computation. In the amplitwistor framework, noise enters through the TARTAN recursion operator, which modulates the temporal evolution of the field and contributes to the emergence of complex, multiscale activity patterns.

Taken together, the empirical literature paints a picture of the cortex as a nonlinear, oscillatory, geometrically constrained dynamical system endowed with hierarchical temporal structure. Local microcircuits implement nonlinear transformations that amplify, rotate, and normalize signals, while the geometry of the cortical surface shapes global patterns of activity through its eigenmodes. Oscillations synchronize distributed populations, traveling and standing waves emerge from the interplay between geometry and dynamics, and stochastic fluctuations enrich the repertoire of possible states. Any comprehensive mathematical theory of cortical computation must therefore integrate nonlinear local operators, geometric constraints, temporal recursion, and stochastic dynamics. The amplitwistor framework introduced in subsequent sections is designed precisely to meet this requirement.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
