6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Spectral Geometry of the Cortical Surface}

The cerebral cortex can be modeled mathematically as a two-dimensional Riemannian manifold embedded in three-dimensional space. Although biological tissue deviates from mathematical idealization at microscopic scales, the Kolmogorov-scale irregularities of cortical lamination do not significantly perturb the global geometric structure relevant for macroscopic neural dynamics. Consequently, the cortical sheet may be treated as a compact, oriented manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> (M, g) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> with bounded curvature, positive injectivity radius, and finite volume. These geometric properties play a central role in shaping the oscillatory, propagative, and resonant phenomena observed across neuroimaging modalities.

A natural mathematical tool for characterizing dynamics on such a surface is the Laplace--Beltrami operator, defined for smooth functions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> f : M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> by
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g f = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{div}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g f),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{div}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> are the gradient and divergence operators induced by the metric </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The Laplace--Beltrami operator generalizes the classical Laplacian to curved surfaces and captures the intrinsic geometry of the manifold independently of its embedding. Solutions to diffusion, wave, and Helmholtz equations on the cortex all depend fundamentally on the spectral properties of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.

The eigenvalue problem
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with Dirichlet, Neumann, or mixed boundary conditions depending on the treatment of cortical folds, yields a countable sequence of nonnegative eigenvalues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and corresponding eigenfunctions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span> u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. For a compact manifold, the spectrum is discrete, unbounded, and satisfies the Weyl asymptotic law,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{2/2},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> C </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> depends on the volume and geometry of the surface. In the two-dimensional case relevant for cortex, Weyl's law predicts that the number of eigenvalues less than </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Lambda</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> grows asymptotically as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Area}(M)}{4</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Lambda</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. This asymptotic distribution has been studied extensively in geometric analysis and underlies many results concerning heat kernel decay and wave propagation on manifolds.

Eigenfunctions of the Laplace--Beltrami operator form an orthonormal basis for </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, providing a natural coordinate system in which cortical activity may be decomposed into spatial modes. This decomposition has deep biochemical and biophysical significance. Empirical studies using ultrafast functional MRI have demonstrated that resting-state activity in rodent cortex can be represented accurately as a superposition of stationary eigenmodes whose temporal evolution reflects slow oscillatory dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}. These modes exhibit spatial profiles that align with cortical folding patterns, intrinsic connectivity gradients, and laminar boundaries, indicating that the geometry of the cortical surface constrains the large-scale structure of spontaneous neural activity.

Analyses by Roberts and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} further show that eigenmodes derived from the Laplace--Beltrami spectrum explain a substantial proportion of variance in human resting-state functional connectivity. Their work demonstrates that large-scale patterns of cortical activation, often interpreted in terms of network communities or modular interactions, can instead be understood as low-frequency, geometry-induced resonances. Honey, Thivierge, and Sporns </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{honey2010} corroborate this interpretation with independent evidence that anatomical connectivity and geometric constraints jointly sculpt functional interactions. Together, these findings support the view that cortical dynamics are strongly determined by the spectral properties of the underlying manifold.

From a mathematical standpoint, the eigenmodes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> provide a basis in which solutions to neural field equations, diffusion models, and wave-like propagation can be analyzed with precision. Linear operators defined on the cortex often act diagonally in this basis, simplifying stability analysis, perturbation expansions, and spectral regularity arguments. Moreover, the smoothness of eigenfunctions improves with increasing eigenvalue, reflecting classical elliptic theory: low-frequency modes vary gradually across the surface, while high-frequency modes exhibit fine-grained structure. This hierarchy mirrors the multiscale nature of cortical dynamics, where slow, global fluctuations coexist with fast, local interactions.

The geometry of cortical folds introduces additional structure. Gyri and sulci generate curvature gradients that create localized distortions in eigenmode morphology. These geometric irregularities influence the coupling between modes and can shape the propagation of traveling waves, phase gradients, and resonance patterns. For example, experimental observations of traveling waves in electrocorticographic recordings often reveal preferred directions that align with geodesic curvature lines. Such phenomena are consistent with theoretical predictions from spectral geometry, where curvature acts as a potential that modifies local wave speed and pattern formation.

The interplay between geometry and dynamics has long been recognized in the study of physical systems, but only recently has it been applied systematically to neuroscience. The success of eigenmode decompositions in modeling resting-state activity, combined with the strong alignment between cortical geometry and functional gradients, provides a compelling case for incorporating spectral geometry directly into theories of cortical computation. The amplitwistor framework developed in later sections leverages this structure by using eigenmodes as the natural global coordinates for field dynamics. Local nonlinear transformations, represented by amplitwistor operators, act primarily on the eigenmode coefficients, while the global evolution is shaped by the geometric properties of the manifold through the Laplace--Beltrami spectrum.

The spectral approach thus offers a unified mathematical language for describing both the structural and functional organization of the cortex. It captures the geometry of the cortical sheet, the multiscale structure of neural activity, and the constraints imposed by curvature and topology. As a consequence, spectral geometry serves not simply as a mathematical convenience but as an empirically grounded framework for understanding the computational architecture of the brain. The remaining sections build on this foundation to develop a formal account of cortical computation grounded in nonlinear operators, global oscillatory fields, and multidimensional geometric constraints.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
