The formulation of cortical computation developed in this work is grounded in a field-theoretic approach in which neural activity is represented by the coupled evolution of a scalar potential field, a vector flux field, and an entropy or uncertainty field. Although the specific framework adopted here is theoretical in nature, its structure is motivated by decades of empirical and computational research on neural population dynamics, including neural mass models, neural field equations, and large-scale dynamical systems analysis of cortex. Classical models of large-scale activity such as those of Wilson--Cowan, Jansen--Rit, and later extensions in mean-field and dynamic causal modeling provide essential precedents for treating cortical computation as an evolution on continuous fields, but the more recent lines of work in computational neurodynamics have revealed that neural activity cannot be fully captured by a single scalar field alone. Instead, contemporary research emphasizes the need to account for directional interactions, propagating wavefronts, structured noise, and entropic or uncertainty-based modulation of functional coupling </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017, deco2009, friston</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>hierarchy, honey2010}.

The geometric and spectral properties described in the preceding section motivate the adoption of a coupled-field system in which the cortex is treated as a compact Riemannian manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> (M, g) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and the neural state at each point of the manifold is encoded by a triplet of fields
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t, x) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bigl</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(t, x), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}(t, x), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> S(t, x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bigr</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
consisting of a scalar potential </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, a tangential vector field </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Gamma</span></span><span>(TM) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and an entropy field </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The scalar potential is intended to represent a graded measure of local activation or semantic density, analogous to coarse-grained membrane potential or local field potential amplitude in neural field models. The vector field captures directed flow or current-like propagation along the cortical surface, incorporating both axodendritic biases and anisotropic conductivities imposed by white-matter projections and laminar orientation. The entropy field represents uncertainty or dispersion in local activity, reflecting variability introduced by noise, prediction error, or unresolved competition between neural assemblies.

The evolution of these fields is governed by a system of partial differential equations that generalizes classical neural field dynamics. A representative formulation takes the form
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{align}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> t}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>= D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>
        + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span>
        - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>
        + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>3
        + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> t}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>= D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>
        + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}
        - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}
        - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}
        + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> S}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span> t}
    </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">&amp;</span></span><span>= D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g S
        + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}|
        + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2
        - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 S
        + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{align}

Each term in this system carries a clear neurobiological interpretation. The diffusion terms </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> encode short-range coupling mediated by horizontal fibers and dendritic arborization, consistent with both empirical and computational studies showing that fast synaptic spread is well-approximated by a diffusion-like process on the cortical manifold. The presence of the advection term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> reflects the well-documented phenomenon of traveling waves and propagating phase gradients observed in electrocorticography and voltage-sensitive dye imaging. Such waves have been shown to coordinate neural assemblies over centimeters of cortex, often aligning with anatomical pathways and geodesic curvature lines. The vector-curl term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> captures rotational components of flow that arise naturally from laminar anisotropy and synaptic asymmetry, while the nonlinear damping </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> ensures that excessive flux does not diverge.

The coupling between the scalar potential and entropy fields represents the influence of uncertainty on neural gain and the modulation of activation by noise or prediction error. Contemporary theories of cortical computation, most prominently hierarchical predictive coding </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>hierarchy}, emphasize that neural activity is regulated by the balance between precision and prediction error. In the present formulation, the term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> ensures that elevated uncertainty suppresses activation, while the source term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\beta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> in the entropy equation reflects the increase in uncertainty when competing activations are unresolved. The damping term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> models the decay of uncertainty as evidence accumulates.

Noise enters the system through the terms </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>v </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>S </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Unlike classical models where noise is treated as perturbative, large-scale empirical studies have documented that cortical noise possesses structured correlations and contributes functionally to the emergence of spontaneous dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009}. This structured noise supports the generation of metastable states, spontaneous transitions, and low-frequency oscillations, all of which are preserved in the field equations when noise is modeled as a Gaussian process with nontrivial covariance kernel adapted to the geometry of the cortical surface.

The vector field equation contains both gradient and curl components, reflecting the fact that cortical interactions contain both push–pull dynamics and rotational components. The inclusion of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> as a source term rests on empirical findings that gradients of activation drive waves of recruitment, while the curl term reflects asymmetries introduced by laminar architecture and anisotropic conduction. In combination, these features produce wave-like activity with spatial patterns that resemble eigenmodes of the Laplace–Beltrami operator, consistent with the observations of Cabral et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} and Roberts et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}, who found that intrinsic fluctuations can be understood as combinations of stationary modes of the cortical geometry.

The full system defines a nonlinear, coupled evolution equation on a Banach space of fields over </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The mathematical treatment of well-posedness, semigroup generation, and regularity will be developed in subsequent sections, but the essential analytic structure is governed by the interplay between linear elliptic operators derived from the geometry and nonlinear terms derived from local neural interactions. The presence of diffusion guarantees smoothing, whereas the nonlinear terms ensure rich dynamical behavior including oscillations, propagating fronts, and multistable regimes. These properties mirror those found in large-scale biophysical models of cortical networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017}, and provide the foundation on which the amplitwistor framework later builds a universal function approximation scheme.

The field equations introduced here serve as the dynamical substrate for the amplitwistor cascades developed in subsequent sections. They provide a geometrically grounded, biologically informed, and mathematically tractable system for modeling large-scale cortical computation and will be used to derive the nonlinear operators governing local transformations and global resonances.
