6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Amplistwistors as Local Nonlinear Operators}

The analysis of large-scale cortical dynamics requires a mathematical formalism capable of describing how local neural populations implement the nonlinear transformations that underlie hierarchical representation. While the preceding section formulated the cortex as a coupled field system evolving on a Riemannian manifold, the present section develops the local transformation theory required to understand how such fields can approximate complex semantic functions and support universal computation at the population level. The central construct introduced here, the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplistwistor}, provides a geometrically explicit operator that generalizes the classical concept of an ``amplitwist'' from complex analysis </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{needham1997} to the setting of nonlinear transformations on field-valued neural states.

In classical complex analysis, a differentiable map </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> f : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{C} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{C} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> acts locally as an infinitesimal combination of an amplitude scaling and a rotation. The derivative </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> f'(z) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> at a point </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> z </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> encodes the infinitesimal map </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mapsto</span></span><span> f'(z)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\xi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, which Needham describes as an ``amplitwist'': the linear action that uniformly stretches and rotates the infinitesimal neighborhood around </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> z </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. This perspective provides a powerful geometric intuition for analytic mappings and motivates an analogous approach to local transformations in high-dimensional neural state spaces. In the cortical setting, however, the neural state at a point of the manifold is represented not by a scalar complex number but by the triplet </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t,x) = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}, S) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> defined previously. Moreover, the transformations applied by local populations are neither strictly linear nor uniform across directions, and therefore require a more general operator class.

The </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplistwistor} developed here is defined as a triplet of operators acting on the local field state,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bigl</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bigr</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> represents local amplitude modulation, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> represents a nonlinear twisting or rotational transformation acting on the vector components, and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> represents a projection or normalization operator. The combined action of these three components is given by the update rule
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{t+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span>}(x)
    = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(
        </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>)
        </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdot</span></span><span>
        </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>)
        </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t(x)
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
which serves as the infinitesimal generator of a nonlinear flow acting on the field space. The operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> modulates local gain, amplifying or attenuating the influence of activation; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> introduces a geometrically meaningful deformation such as rotation, shear, or torsion; and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> restores normalization or sparsity constraints characteristic of neural population codes.

The biological basis of these operators can be traced to three well-studied physiological phenomena. First, amplitude modulation arises naturally from gain-control mechanisms mediated by attentional signals, neuromodulators, and dendritic nonlinearities. Second, twisting and rotational effects reflect the influence of the local circuit geometry, particularly anisotropic dendritic arborization and the directionality imposed by laminar structure. Third, projection effects reflect the competition among neurons and populations, such as winner-take-all dynamics, divisive normalization, and sparsity-inducing inhibitory feedback. Together, these mechanisms produce transformations that are inherently nonlinear and cannot be captured by simple linear filtering models. This observation aligns with empirical findings showing that linear models are unable to account for the temporal hierarchy of language processing revealed by electrocorticography </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}.

The amplistwistor construction gains additional significance from its relationship to classical results on universal approximation. Foundational theorems by Cybenko </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989}, Hornik </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hornik1991}, and Poggio and Girosi </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{poggio1990} demonstrate that compositions of nonlinear transformations suffice to approximate arbitrarily complex functions on compact subsets of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. These theorems have had profound impact on the development of artificial neural networks and deep learning, yet their geometric content is rarely expressed explicitly. The present framework reveals that cortical columns may implement analogous universal approximation capabilities, not through discrete layers but through a continuous cascade of amplistwistors acting along the cortical surface.

To formalize this relationship, consider the iterated composition
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)
    = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\underbrace</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>N</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> times}}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
If the operators </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> satisfy mild regularity and nondegeneracy conditions, then the family of maps generated by finite compositions of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is dense in the space of continuous maps from a compact subset of the field space to itself. In biological terms, this implies that finite cascades of cortical microcircuits can approximate arbitrarily complex semantic transformations, provided the underlying nonlinearities exhibit sufficient richness. The presence of rotational components in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, akin to the infinitesimal rotations of analytic maps, ensures the ability of the system to explore diverse directions in field space, avoiding degeneracy. Projection operators ensure that the output remains normalized and biologically plausible. Amplitude modulation controls gain and sensitivity, permitting fine-grained adjustments of representational precision.

The geometrical nature of the amplistwistor also aligns with experimental findings on cortical synchrony and oscillatory coupling. Singer's extensive work on neuronal synchrony </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999} and the large body of research on oscillatory coordination </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006} emphasize that coherent transformations across distributed populations rely on phase-locked, rhythmically structured updates. The amplistwistor encapsulates the geometry of such phase-dependent modulation by allowing the twisting operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> to depend on local phase structure, thereby producing transformations whose effect depends on the timing of oscillatory activity. This integration of local geometry, nonlinear transformation, and oscillatory phase structure anticipates the later sections, where global eigenmodes provide the temporal scaffolding for distributed coordination.

Amplistwistors therefore occupy a dual position in this theoretical framework. On one hand, they provide the microscopic, biologically grounded mechanism by which local populations compute nonlinear transformations of field states. On the other hand, they serve as the mathematical building blocks for understanding how cascades of such transformations can implement the function-approximation capabilities necessary for high-level cognition, including the multi-step semantic transformations implicated in natural language processing. Their structure unifies classical complex-analytic intuition, contemporary universal-approximation theory, and the physiological reality of dendritic and laminar nonlinearities. In the broader development of this manuscript, amplistwistors will furnish the local computational machinery whose interplay with global eigenmodes produces the large-scale, temporally layered structure of cortical computation demonstrated empirically in recent electrocorticographic studies.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
