6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Eigenmodes, Standing Waves, and Large-Scale Cortical Structure}

The preceding section developed the local computational geometry of amplistwistors. We now turn to the complementary phenomenon governing global cortical organization: the emergence of large-scale eigenmodes and standing-wave patterns across the cortical surface. These modes, empirically documented across modalities including fMRI, MEG, ECoG, and electrophysiology, provide the temporal and spatial scaffolding through which local nonlinear operations become coordinated into coherent cognitive dynamics. Their mathematical presence is most naturally expressed through the spectral theory of Laplace-type operators defined on Riemannian manifolds, combined with the anatomical connectivity structure of the cortex.

Large-scale cortical eigenmodes have received increasingly precise empirical support over the past two decades. Early work using diffusion models of cortical connectivity demonstrated that slow resting-state fluctuations could be recovered as the principal eigenvectors of connectivity matrices derived from DTI tractography, revealing a small number of dominant spatial patterns structured by long-range axonal pathways </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{honey2010}. Subsequent advances in biophysical neural mass models showed that the dynamical interplay of coupling strength, conduction delays, and intrinsic noise produces macroscopic activity patterns whose geometry is determined primarily by the eigenmodes of the underlying anatomical Laplacian </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009,breakspear2017}. The interpretation of these modes as resonant or standing-wave patterns gained decisive support from ultrafast fMRI experiments in rodents </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}, where the cortical signal was reconstructed as a superposition of a small number of spatially extended stationary modes oscillating at characteristic frequencies.

Mathematically, these findings can be formulated in terms of the spectral decomposition of a Laplace–Beltrami operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> defined on the cortical surface </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, possibly supplemented by a structural connectivity operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{C} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> encoding long-range coupling. The simplest formulation proceeds by considering the eigenvalue problem
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> form an orthonormal basis of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> are the corresponding non-negative eigenvalues. When long-range connectivity is included, one replaces </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> with a graph Laplacian or continuous operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{C} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\gamma</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> controlling coupling strength. In either case, the eigenfunctions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> provide a coordinate system for describing large-scale cortical activity, and empirical results demonstrate that only the lowest few dozen modes play a significant role in shaping observed neural signals </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}.

The temporal evolution of cortical activity can then be expressed as a modal decomposition,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t,x) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>} a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t) u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(x),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where the coefficients </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> satisfy dynamical equations whose structure depends on local nonlinearities, sensory input, and interactions among modes. In the resting state, the dominant modes correspond to slowly varying patterns capturing the gross organization of sensory, associative, and motor systems, consistent with the well-known organization of resting-state networks. Under cognitive load, task-relevant modes modulate the spatial distribution of activity, producing transient patterns whose spatial geometry remains constrained by the eigenmode basis.

The observation that the cortex supports standing-wave patterns is consistent with theoretical expectations for excitable media embedded in approximately two-dimensional manifolds. As shown in classical models of neural synchrony </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999} and in extensive research on oscillatory coordination across cortical regions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006}, distributed neural populations achieve coherence by aligning their phases through both synaptic interactions and shared oscillatory drives. When expressed in the eigenmode basis, these phase relationships take on a geometrically structured form: lower-order modes correspond to large-scale synchronous oscillations, while higher-order modes encode finer spatial structure and faster temporal dynamics.

Empirical evidence for this modal structure is particularly compelling in the ultrafast fMRI study by Cabral, Fernandes, and Shemesh </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}. By achieving a temporal resolution orders of magnitude higher than typical fMRI protocols, the authors directly observed propagating waves and stationary resonant modes whose superposition reproduces the full complexity of resting-state dynamics. The ability to reconstruct the entire cortical activity pattern from a small number of modes confirms that large-scale cortical dynamics are low-dimensional in a spectral sense, even though local microcircuit dynamics remain high-dimensional and nonlinear.

The role of eigenmodes extends beyond resting-state structure. Recent work by Roberts and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} demonstrated that eigenmode decompositions yield accurate predictions of activity patterns across diverse tasks and conditions. Similarly, studies integrating dynamical systems modeling with anatomical connectivity have shown that large-scale neural mass models require only a small number of modes to capture the temporal structure of MEG signals </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017}. This convergence across methodologies suggests that the cortex operates in a regime where global geometry strongly constrains the space of possible patterns, while local nonlinearities—such as those implemented by amplistwistors—provide the flexibility needed for high-level computation.

From a mathematical perspective, the eigenfunctions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> of the Laplace–Beltrami operator form a complete orthonormal basis for square-integrable functions on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, ensuring that any sufficiently regular field </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t,x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> can be expressed as a convergent series. The eigenvalues satisfy Weyl’s asymptotic law, with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> C m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{2/d} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> for </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> d = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dim</span></span><span> M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, implying that the number of eigenmodes below a given frequency grows polynomially. This structure provides natural smoothness constraints on cortical fields: high-frequency modes contribute fine spatial detail but are strongly penalized by the Laplacian, a fact reflected biologically in the predominance of low-frequency oscillations across modalities.

Eigenmodes thus supply the global framework within which local amplistwistor dynamics unfold. While amplistwistors determine the nonlinear transformation of field values at each point in time, eigenmodes determine the geometry along which these transformations become coordinated, enabling the formation of coherent, large-scale patterns observed in empirical studies. The interaction of these two components—the microscopic nonlinear operators and the macroscopic modal geometry—forms the foundation for the temporally layered, spatially structured architecture of cortical computation articulated throughout this manuscript.

</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
