6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Cortical Columns and Microcircuit Geometry}

The macroscopic eigenmode structure described in the preceding section provides a global geometric framework for cortical dynamics, but cognition is ultimately implemented through the microscopic circuitry of cortical columns. These vertically organized microcircuits, spanning layers II through VI and connected through recurrent excitatory and inhibitory loops, constitute the basic computational units of the neocortex. Their local geometry, synaptic coupling, and nonlinear response properties determine the form of the transformations that may be realized at each point of the cortical sheet. In the present formulation, these operations are expressed through amplistwistors, which generalize the complex-analytic notion of an amplitwist </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{needham1997} to the high-dimensional, nonlinear transformations required for biological computation.

The justification for treating cortical columns as local nonlinear operators rests on a convergence of empirical and theoretical findings. Anatomical studies have long indicated that minicolumns and columns form repeating canonical microcircuits whose internal wiring is preserved across sensory, associative, and motor cortices. This canonical pattern, involving feedforward excitation from layer IV to layers II/III, widespread horizontal connectivity within layers II/III, and strong feedback from layers V/VI, is well captured by the family of biologically grounded neural field models developed over the past decades. Neural mass and neural field formulations, such as those analyzed in the dynamical-systems treatments of large-scale brain activity </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017}, show that cortical columns exhibit rich nonlinear dynamics capable of amplification, filtering, oscillation, and gain control. These findings provide an empirical basis for modeling columns as nonlinear operators acting on the local field value.

The analytic structure of these nonlinear transformations becomes clearer when considered against the background of universal approximation theorems in neural network theory. Results due to Cybenko </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989} and Hornik </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hornik1991} demonstrate that sufficiently large families of nonlinear functions composed in multilayer architectures can approximate arbitrary continuous maps on compact subsets of Euclidean space. Poggio and Girosi </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{poggio1990} further extended these results to networks implementing radial basis functions, while Saxe, McClelland, and Ganguli </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{saxe2013} showed that deep networks learn hierarchical structure by gradually linearizing manifolds in representation space. Although derived in artificial systems, the structural parallels with cortical microcircuitry are striking. Cortical columns possess layered recurrent architectures with diverse nonlinearities, including saturating synaptic dynamics, short-term plasticity, and dendritic nonlinearities. The presence of varied interneuron subtypes, each modulating activity with distinct time constants, further enhances the expressive power of these units. When cast in an operator-theoretic form, these microcircuits realize local maps whose compositions across time and space can approximate highly complex transformations, in accordance with the universal approximation results.

It is within this theoretical landscape that the notion of an amplistwistor becomes valuable. In complex analysis, Needham’s amplitwist describes the local effect of a complex derivative as a combination of amplification and rotation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{needham1997}. Generalizing this idea to cortical microcircuits, an amplistwistor is defined as a local high-dimensional operator that combines amplitude modulation, rotational or shear-like transformations in representation space, and projection onto task- or context-relevant subspaces. The dynamical action of a cortical column can be expressed by such an operator, and repeated application over short temporal windows yields a local cascade that implements nonlinear semantic refinement. The interplay of amplification, twisting, and projection is biologically grounded in mechanisms such as synaptic gain modulation, phase-dependent firing, and inhibitory gating, each of which contributes to the shaping of activity patterns in the column.

Experimental studies provide converging evidence that cortical columns operate as context-sensitive, nonlinear processors, rather than simply as feature detectors. Work based on laminar electrophysiology, including studies of predictive coding and hierarchical inference </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>hierarchy}, reveals that deep and superficial layers carry distinct error and prediction signals that interact through recurrent circuits. These recurrent interactions generate transformations whose effects resemble the amplitude–rotation–projection triplets encoded in the amplistwistor formalism. Meanwhile, synchrony-based theories of cortical function, such as the relational coding hypothesis of Singer </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999} and the oscillatory coordination principles outlined by Buzsáki </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006}, demonstrate that local microcircuits shape the phase structure of neural oscillations. These phase relationships appear as rotational components in the amplistwistor interpretation, mapping neatly onto Needham’s decomposition of complex derivatives.

When cortical columns are situated within the broader geometric context of the cortical sheet, their functional role becomes even clearer. Each column is embedded in a manifold whose global modes are determined by the eigenstructure described previously. The local amplistwistor acts on the field value at a specific point, but the effect of this transformation propagates globally through the eigenmode basis. Because the eigenfunctions form an orthonormal basis of the function space over the cortical surface </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}, any localized transformation can be expressed as a modification of the spectral coefficients </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Thus the nonlinear action of columns and the linear propagation of modes jointly produce the macroscopic dynamics observed in empirical studies, including the large-scale patterns reconstructed from fMRI data </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} and the temporally layered structure of language processing demonstrated through ECoG in higher-order regions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}.

This linking of local geometry and global dynamics is central to understanding the computational power of the cortex. Cortical columns generate nonlinear transformations that allow for expressive mappings and semantic refinement, while eigenmodes determine the directions along which these refinements propagate and combine. The former introduces flexibility and nonlinearity, while the latter imposes geometric coherence. Cognitive function arises from their continuous interplay, with the amplistwistor formalism offering a mathematically precise account of the local operations and the spectral framework grounding their global consequences.

In the sections that follow, we formalize this interaction through a system of coupled partial differential equations defined on the cortical manifold. These equations provide a unified mathematical representation of the combined local–global architecture, yielding a rigorous description of how nonlinear microcircuit transformations shape, and are shaped by, the geometry of the cortical sheet and its eigenmodes.

