6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{PDE Formulation of Field Dynamics}

The interplay between local nonlinear transformations and global geometric propagation can be formalized within a continuous field-theoretic framework defined on the cortical manifold. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denote a compact, orientable two-dimensional Riemannian manifold without boundary, representing the cortical sheet after flattening along the gyral–sulcal structure. The metric tensor </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> g</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ij}(x) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> encodes local curvature and anisotropy, and its associated Laplace–Beltrami operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> provides the canonical second-order differential operator intrinsic to the geometry of the surface. Function spaces over </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> are thus naturally taken as Sobolev spaces </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s(M) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, whose properties follow directly from the spectral theory of elliptic operators on compact manifolds </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}.

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x,t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denote the cortical field at location </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and time </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. This field aggregates the relevant neural variables representing local information content, population firing rates, and the internal states of microcircuits. The local dynamics induced by a cortical column at </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> are modeled through an amplistwistor operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> indexed by a parameter vector </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> reflecting synaptic strengths and short-term plasticity. Formally, the local transformation is given by
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)(x,t)
  = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>(x,t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>( </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>(x,t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>(x,t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x,t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> implements a nonlinear amplitude modulation, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> SO(d) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> a rotation or shear in representation space, and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> a projection onto a lower-dimensional subspace associated with momentary functional demands. These components reflect the nonlinearities that arise biologically from dendritic integration, synaptic gating, inhibitory interneuron modulation, and laminar feedback </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017}.

The evolution of the field is influenced not only by these local processes but also by long-range propagation constrained by the geometry of the cortical surface. Following classical neural field theories and the modern dynamical systems approaches to large-scale cortical activity </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009, honey2010}, we introduce a diffusive–propagative term governed by the Laplace–Beltrami operator. This component ensures that the effect of local transformations spreads across the cortical sheet in a manner consistent with axonal distances and geometric embeddings. The simplest form of this geometric propagation can be written as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
D</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x,t),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> D &gt; 0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a diffusion-like coefficient reflecting a mixture of passive dendritic spread, horizontal connectivity, and wave-like propagation along the cortical mantle.

To incorporate the dynamics observed experimentally in both resting-state and stimulus-driven activity, a forcing term is added to represent extrinsic inputs and intrinsic oscillations. The empirical finding that cortical activity spontaneously decomposes into low-dimensional eigenmodes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023, roberts2019} motivates decomposing the external forcing into contributions from resonant modes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(x) e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m t} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. These modes arise naturally from the spectral decomposition of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and their temporal evolution approximates the standing-wave and quasi-standing-wave patterns detected in ultrafast fMRI. Accordingly, the forcing term is expressed as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
F(x,t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>M c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(x)e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\omega</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m t},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where the coefficients </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> modulate mode amplitudes in response to cognitive demands, arousal state, and prediction error.

Finally, stochasticity is introduced through an additive noise process </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span>(x,t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, representing microscopic synaptic fluctuations, channel noise, and uncertainty in external stimuli. Following the stochastic formulations of large-scale cortical dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017}, the noise is modeled as a spatially correlated Gaussian process with covariance kernel </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> K(x,y) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, ensuring biologically plausible smoothness.

Combining these elements yields the full partial differential equation governing cortical field evolution:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x,t)
 = D </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x,t)
 + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)(x,t)
 + F(x,t)
 + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span>(x,t),
 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> M,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This system synthesizes diffusion on the cortical manifold, nonlinear local operators modeled by amplistwistors, global resonance modes derived from spectral geometry, and stochastic fluctuations intrinsic to neural circuits. Its structure parallels classical neural field equations yet extends them by incorporating the geometric and spectral constraints identified in empirical studies of functional connectivity </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} and by grounding the nonlinearities in the mathematical apparatus of amplistwistors.

The PDE provides a rigorous formal environment in which to analyze stability, bifurcations, and emergent large-scale patterns. Moreover, its reliance on geometric operators ensures coherence with the empirical eigenmode structure of cortical dynamics. In the next section, we examine the analytic properties of this equation, demonstrating existence, uniqueness, and regularity of solutions through semigroup theory applied to nonlinear operators on Sobolev spaces.

