6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Semigroup Theory and Well-Posedness of the Cortical Field Equation}

The field equation derived in the preceding section represents a nonlinear, stochastically forced evolution on a compact Riemannian manifold. To ensure that this model constitutes a mathematically legitimate description of cortical dynamics, we must demonstrate that the associated initial value problem is well posed. This requires establishing existence, uniqueness, and continuous dependence on initial conditions for solutions in appropriate function spaces. The natural setting for this analysis is provided by the theory of strongly continuous semigroups on Banach and Hilbert spaces, which offers a framework for studying evolution equations of both linear and nonlinear type. 

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H = L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denote the Hilbert space of square-integrable vector fields on the cortical manifold, and let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denote the realization of the Laplace--Beltrami operator acting componentwise. Classical elliptic theory on compact manifolds ensures that </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is self-adjoint, densely defined, and has a discrete spectrum </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> satisfying </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>0 = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The associated eigenfunctions form a complete orthonormal basis of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, providing the spectral decomposition essential for the geometric analysis of cortical dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}. Since </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is nonpositive and generates a strongly continuous semigroup </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tA}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, classical results in semigroup theory guarantee that the linear diffusion equation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> = A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> admits a unique mild solution for every initial condition in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.

The full cortical field equation incorporates nonlinear terms and forcing, yielding the evolution
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t) = A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t) + N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t)) + F(t) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span>(t),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denotes the amplistwistor-induced nonlinearity. To apply the standard theory of semilinear evolution equations, we must ascertain that </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>N</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is locally Lipschitz on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> or on a Sobolev space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>s &gt; n/2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>n=2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is the dimension of the cortical surface. Since the amplistwistor operator is constructed from smooth functions of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, including amplitude modulation, orthogonal transformations in representation space, and projections onto fixed subspaces, its components are smooth maps from </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> to </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. When lifted pointwise to a mapping </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>N: H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s(M) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, smoothness in finite dimensions together with Sobolev embedding theorems implies local Lipschitz continuity for any </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>s &gt; n/2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Because the cortical manifold is two-dimensional, the condition reduces to </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>s &gt; 1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, ensuring that </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> embeds continuously into </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>0(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and providing the regularity needed to evaluate nonlinearities pointwise. The existence of such embeddings on a compact manifold follows from standard results in geometric analysis.

With the Lipschitz property established, the existence and uniqueness of local mild solutions follows from the classical semilinear evolution equation theory developed by Henry and others. The mild solution is expressed in integral form as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t) = e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tA}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(t-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)A}N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>
         + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(t-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)A}F(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>
         + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(t-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>)A}dW(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where the last term denotes the stochastic convolution with a cylindrical Wiener process </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>W(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> having covariance operator determined by the spatial correlation kernel </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>K(x,y)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The theory of stochastic evolution equations on Hilbert spaces, as developed in the works of Da Prato and Zabczyk, guarantees the mathematical validity of this expression under mild integrability and regularity assumptions on the noise. In particular, if </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is trace class on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, the stochastic convolution defines an </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>-valued process with continuous sample paths.

The smoothing properties of the semigroup </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tA}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> play a crucial role in ensuring global well posedness for sufficiently small nonlinearities. The heat semigroup generated by </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is analytic, yielding estimates of the form
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tA} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{s+2}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> C t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
for all </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>s </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>t&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. These bounds, derived from elliptic regularity on compact manifolds, imply that the diffusive component of the evolution exerts a smoothing effect which counteracts potential blow-up from nonlinear amplification. Indeed, many biological neural field equations exhibit global existence due to the natural balance between smoothing by diffusion and saturation in the nonlinear terms </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017}. The amplistwistor nonlinearity is structured in such a way that the amplitude modulation saturates for large inputs, while the orthogonal transformation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> preserves norms. These mechanisms ensure that the energy functional
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
E(t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{1}{2}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{H}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
cannot diverge in finite time under biologically realistic parameters, thus guaranteeing global solutions.

Continuous dependence on initial data follows from standard Grönwall estimates applied to differences of mild solutions. This property is essential for interpreting the equation as a meaningful model of cortical dynamics, since small perturbations in initial neural states should not produce disproportionately large divergences in macroscopic patterns. The same estimates establish uniqueness.

Taken together, these results ensure that the cortical field equation possesses a unique global mild solution for every initial condition in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>s(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>s &gt; 1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, that the solution depends continuously on the initial state, and that the smoothing properties of the semigroup guarantee regularity for positive times. The analytic foundations of the model are therefore secure, allowing us to proceed to more detailed investigations of the expressive capacity of the nonlinear operators and their relationship to empirical cortical dynamics. 

In the next section, we examine the approximation properties of amplitwistor cascades, showing that they can approximate arbitrary continuous maps on the cortical manifold, thereby providing a functional analogue to universal representation theorems in neural network theory.
