6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Universal Approximation and Expressivity of Amplistwistor Cascades}

The mathematical legitimacy of treating cortical columns as local nonlinear operators depends not only on the well-posedness of the evolution equation but also on the expressive capacity of the nonlinear transformations they implement. If columns are to support the wide range of perceptual, cognitive, and inferential operations observed in the cortex, then the family of mappings generated by amplistwistors and their compositions must be sufficiently rich to approximate the functional transformations required by neural computation. This section develops the theoretical foundations of this claim by establishing that cascades of amplistwistors possess the universal approximation property. 

The argument begins with the classical universal approximation theorems for neural networks. In a seminal result, Cybenko showed that finite superpositions of sigmoidal functions can uniformly approximate any continuous mapping on a compact subset of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989}. Hornik extended this theorem by demonstrating that multilayer feedforward neural networks with a wide class of activation functions can approximate arbitrary Borel measurable functions and their derivatives </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hornik1991}. Poggio and Girosi constructed a similar framework for radial basis function networks, emphasizing the geometric structure of approximation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{poggio1990}, while later work by Saxe, McClelland, and Ganguli </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{saxe2013} showed that deep networks learn hierarchical structure through progressive linearization and curvature reduction in representational space. These results collectively demonstrate that biologically plausible classes of nonlinear transformations are capable of approximating any function consistent with the smoothness and continuity properties of neural computations.

The amplitwistor operator inherits this expressive capacity because it is constructed from smooth scalar and vector-valued nonlinearities applied pointwise to the cortical field. If the functions defining </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> are chosen from any of the classes appearing in the universal approximation literature, then any single amplistwistor corresponds to a nonlinear transformation lying within the closure of a universal family. Since compositions of universal approximators remain universal, finite cascades of amplistwistors can approximate arbitrary continuous functions from </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> to </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, provided the nonlinearities satisfy the standard conditions of discriminatory power and boundedness. 

To extend these results to the cortical manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, we view the field </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> as an element of the Banach space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>0(M; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Because </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is compact, the Stone--Weierstrass theorem implies that the algebra of continuous functions defined by finite compositions of smooth local operators is dense in the space of continuous functions on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The amplistwistor family is closed under composition and contains nonconstant functions capable of separating points on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>; the orthogonal transformation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> alone suffices for separation in the representation space. Consequently, the conditions for Stone--Weierstrass are met, and cascades of amplistwistors approximate any continuous local mapping </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>F: C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>0(M; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>0(M; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> arbitrarily well in the uniform norm. 

A more refined argument considers the spectral decomposition of the field. Any function </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> can be expressed as a convergent series
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(x) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span> a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(x),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> are the Laplace--Beltrami eigenfunctions forming an orthonormal basis of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}. The space of admissible spectral coefficient sequences </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span> a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is dense in the appropriate Sobolev spaces, and any finite truncation of the series yields a smooth approximation of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Because amplistwistors operate pointwise in physical space but induce linear transformations in spectral space, they implicitly provide nonlinear maps from finite-dimensional spectral coefficient vectors to new coefficient vectors. The universal approximation results for finite-dimensional nonlinear maps then imply that amplistwistor cascades can approximate arbitrary transformations of spectral coefficients as well. Thus both physical-space and spectral-space approximations fall under the same mathematical principle: amplistwistors form a functionally complete class of local operators.

From a biological perspective, this expressivity is consistent with the rich nonlinear dynamics observed in cortical microcircuits. Neurons exhibit saturating nonlinear responses, dendritic trees implement local polynomial-like expansions of synaptic inputs, inhibitory interneurons sculpt the geometry of local representation, and recurrent laminar loops provide depth through iterative application of nonlinearities. These mechanisms mirror the nonlinear layers of artificial neural networks and provide a plausible physiological substrate for the mathematical operations in the universal approximation theorems. 

The functional completeness of amplistwistor cascades implies that the cortex possesses the representational capacity necessary for complex information processing, provided that the nonlinearities are organized temporally in a manner consistent with the dynamics of the cortical field equation. The subsequent sections demonstrate that this temporal structure is not arbitrary but shaped by the global geometry of the cortical manifold, the spectral structure of its Laplacian, and the experimentally observed hierarchical organization of cortical computation.

