6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Alignment With Hierarchical Structure in Language and Cognition}

The universal approximation properties of amplistwistor cascades acquire their full neuroscientific significance only when examined in the context of the hierarchical temporal structure of cortical computation. Language processing, in particular, offers an empirical domain in which the layered transformations predicted by the mathematical model can be compared directly with high-temporal-resolution recordings from the human brain. The cortex exhibits a progressive expansion of integration windows from early auditory areas to higher-order associative regions, reflecting the cumulative effect of nonlinear transformations applied over increasingly extended temporal intervals. This phenomenon resonates profoundly with the layered structures discovered in artificial language models and has become increasingly accessible to measurement through electrocorticography and high-frequency neural recordings.

Recent work by Goldstein and colleagues provides the most direct empirical demonstration of this hierarchical temporal architecture </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}. In their study, deep language models such as GPT-2 XL and LLaMA were probed for the contextual embeddings generated at each intermediate layer during the processing of spoken narratives. These embeddings were then aligned with intracranial electrocorticography recordings from patients listening to the same auditory stimuli. A striking correspondence emerged: the deeper the layer in the artificial model, the later in time the cortical response peaked in high-order language areas, particularly in the inferior frontal gyrus and temporal pole. This result establishes a monotonic relationship between model-layer depth and cortical-latency depth, with correlation coefficients exceeding 0.8 in key regions implicated in semantic integration.

Such findings are difficult to reconcile with classical symbolic approaches to linguistic processing, which typically posit separate stages for phonology, syntax, and semantics. These stages do not exhibit the graded, continuously expanding temporal windows observed experimentally. Instead, the empirical data indicate a cascade of nonlinear transformations unfolding over several hundred milliseconds, a structure that closely aligns with the recursive dynamics inherent in the field evolution equation developed earlier. In the context of the PDE model, each amplistwistor application corresponds to a local nonlinear transformation, while the diffusive term and the eigenmode decomposition govern the propagation and integration of these transformations across space and time. The deeper the effective cascade produced by unfolding these operators, the broader the temporal window over which information is accumulated and refined.

The spectral geometry of the cortical manifold plays a decisive role in determining the temporal profile of this accumulation. Low-frequency eigenmodes of the Laplace–Beltrami operator dominate large-scale connectivity patterns </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}, and their slow temporal dynamics govern the emergence of high-order semantic representations. Studies using ultrafast functional MRI have demonstrated that resting-state cortical activity can be reconstructed as a superposition of a small number of such eigenmodes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}. These stationary waves oscillate at frequencies consistent with the slow, integrative processes observed in higher cortical areas, providing a global scaffold upon which local nonlinear transformations accumulate. As information ascends the cortical hierarchy, the field becomes increasingly dominated by low-frequency modes, producing the progressive temporal dilation documented in electrophysiological studies of language comprehension.

The convergence of these mathematical and empirical observations suggests that the hierarchical structure of linguistic processing is not a special-purpose architectural feature but an emergent property of the interaction between local nonlinear transformations and the global spectral geometry of the cortex. This perspective is consistent with the long-standing hypothesis that cortical hierarchies reflect intrinsic timescales arising from variations in recurrent connectivity, synaptic dynamics, and dendritic integration </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>hierarchy}. The present model extends this view by showing that these intrinsic timescales can be derived from the interplay between amplistwistor nonlinearities and eigenmode-mediated propagation, without appealing to ad hoc modular stages of linguistic processing.

Experimental findings on neuronal synchrony provide an additional source of support for this interpretation. Singer's landmark work demonstrated that synchrony and phase locking constitute fundamental mechanisms for binding distributed neural representations </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999}. These mechanisms are naturally incorporated into the amplistwistor framework through the rotational component of the operator, which modulates the phase of local activity and aligns it with global oscillatory patterns. Similarly, the oscillatory regimes described by Buzsáki </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006} emerge from interactions among inhibitory interneurons and recurrent pyramidal networks, mechanisms that are mathematically encoded in the amplitude modulation and projection components of the amplistwistor operator.

The alignment with artificial neural networks is equally striking. Deep language models exhibit a hierarchical structure in which successive layers perform increasingly abstract transformations, a phenomenon well documented in representation-learning research </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{saxe2013}. The local nonlinearities of amplistwistors, combined with repeated compositions across time, naturally produce a similar hierarchy of transformations. The difference is one of medium rather than principle: in biological systems, the hierarchy unfolds in time through the dynamics of the cortical field, whereas in artificial systems, it is implemented spatially across stacked layers. The empirical correspondence between layer depth in artificial models and temporal depth in cortical dynamics suggests that both systems instantiate a shared computational principle grounded in the iterative refinement of representational geometry.

Thus, the hierarchical structure of language and cognition can be understood as the macroscopic manifestation of the nonlinear partial differential equation governing the evolution of the cortical field. The temporal expansion observed experimentally, the layered structure of deep language models, and the spectral decomposition of cortical activity converge on a single theoretical explanation. In the following section, we examine the empirical evidence supporting this alignment in greater detail, drawing upon multimodal neuroimaging studies and high-density electrophysiology.
