6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Empirical Evidence From ECoG, MEG, EEG, and Ultrafast fMRI}

A theoretical account of cortical computation acquires credibility only insofar as it illuminates and organizes empirical findings across multiple scales of measurement. The mathematical framework developed in the preceding sections predicts that cortical dynamics arise from the interplay of nonlinear local transformations, global geometric propagation, and spectral constraints imposed by the eigenstructure of the cortical manifold. These predictions can be evaluated through data extracted from electrophysiological and hemodynamic modalities, each of which provides complementary insight into the spatiotemporal orchestration of neural activity. Together, studies employing electrocorticography, magnetoencephalography, electroencephalography, and ultrafast functional MRI reveal a coherent picture that strongly supports the geometrically structured, recursively evolving field model articulated here.

Electrocorticography provides the most direct evidence for hierarchical temporal dynamics in cortical computation. High-gamma ECoG signals offer millisecond-scale resolution and capture the evolution of local population activity in response to linguistic stimuli. The recent study by Goldstein and collaborators represents a landmark in this domain </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}. Their analysis revealed that embeddings extracted from progressively deeper layers of large language models align with neural responses at progressively longer latencies in high-order cortical regions. This result is consistent with the theory that amplistwistor cascades unfold over time, with deeper effective compositions corresponding to longer intrinsic processing windows. The inferior frontal gyrus and temporal pole exhibit the clearest signatures of this phenomenon, reflecting their role in integrating syntactic and semantic regularities over extended intervals. In contrast, early auditory regions show no such monotonic latency–complexity relationship, as predicted by the local nature of their computations and the dominance of high-frequency spectral components in these areas.

Magnetoencephalography and electroencephalography further corroborate the presence of structured, hierarchy-dependent temporal scales in cortical computation. These modalities capture oscillatory dynamics across distributed cortical networks and reveal frequency bands associated with distinct computational roles. The layered PDE model predicts that nonlinear local transformations modulate the amplitude and phase of specific eigenmodes, while diffusion on the cortical manifold ensures coherent propagation of these modulations. MEG studies demonstrate that beta and low-gamma oscillations are deeply implicated in long-range integration processes, consistent with slow, large-scale modes of the Laplace--Beltrami spectrum </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}. Meanwhile, higher-frequency gamma activity corresponds to rapid local transformations, echoing the nonlinear twisting and amplification encoded in amplistwistor operators. The interdependence of slow, global oscillations and fast, local fluctuations matches the theoretical decomposition of the cortical field into a spectral scaffold over which nonlinear operators act.

Ultrafast functional MRI studies provide a crucial bridge between these electrophysiological findings and the geometric structure of the model. The work of Cabral, Fernandes, and Shemesh demonstrated that resting-state cortical activity in rodents can be reconstructed as the superposition of a surprisingly small number of stationary waves oscillating in time </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}. These waves correspond closely to the lowest eigenfunctions of the Laplace--Beltrami operator on the cortical surface. Their existence, persistence, and reproducibility across animals and experimental conditions indicate that the spectral geometry of the cortical sheet exerts a powerful organizing influence on spontaneous and evoked activity alike. Unlike classical slow fMRI, ultrafast methods enable direct inspection of the temporal dynamics of these modes, showing that they oscillate in synchrony across distant regions even in the absence of explicit stimulation. Such coherence is precisely what is predicted when nonlinear local dynamics interact with a global eigenmode structure, and it provides compelling evidence that the cortex operates within a geometrically constrained dynamical regime.

Further proof of these geometric constraints emerges from studies of effective connectivity and structural–functional coupling. Honey and colleagues showed that the structural connectome imposes identifiable constraints on the functional dynamics observed in fMRI </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{honey2010}. Although structural connectivity is often used as a proxy for long-range communication pathways, the present model suggests that the geometry of the cortical surface itself, independent of white-matter connectivity, shapes the form of large-scale patterns. The strong alignment between structural connectivity and eigenmode structure found in recent computational analyses </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} reflects the shared influence of geometry on both anatomical and functional organization.

Electrophysiological evidence from synchrony-based research adds additional support. Singer's work on temporal binding </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999} and Buzsáki's extensive analysis of oscillatory regimes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006} show that phase relationships and rhythm-specific interactions modulate functional integration across the cortex. These findings align naturally with the rotational component of amplistwistors, which adjust local phase relationships in a manner contingent upon global oscillatory state. The theory predicts that amplistwistors, by modulating local phase and amplitude, create transient windows in which specific eigenmodes are selectively enhanced or suppressed, a mechanism consistent with task-dependent modulation observed across cognitive paradigms.

Taken together, these converging empirical results support a unifying interpretation: the cortex operates as a geometrically constrained recursive dynamical system, in which nonlinear transformations enacted locally through microcircuit dynamics propagate across the cortical manifold according to spectral modes determined by intrinsic geometry. Short-timescale, high-frequency activity reflects the immediate action of amplistwistors, while long-timescale, low-frequency oscillations reflect the dominance of low-order eigenmodes. The interaction between these components reproduces precisely the kind of hierarchical temporal structure observed in natural language processing, episodic memory, and predictive coding paradigms.

In light of this evidence, the PDE-based field model is not merely a mathematical abstraction but a principled synthesis that captures the essential computational architecture of the cortex. Its predictions are borne out across disparate measurement modalities and tasks, indicating that the interplay of nonlinear local operators and global geometric propagation is a fundamental principle of brain function rather than a task-specific adaptation. The next section turns to detailed experimental validation, showing how specific predictions of the model can be tested using multimodal neuroimaging and targeted stimulation protocols.
