A theory of cortical computation must ultimately be judged by the precision and scope of the predictions it affords. The mathematical structure developed in this work, grounded in nonlinear local transformations and global geometric propagation, leads naturally to a set of empirically testable hypotheses. These hypotheses emerge directly from the form of the field equation, the universal approximation properties of its nonlinear operators, and the spectral decomposition imposed by the Laplace--Beltrami eigenmodes. In this section, the theory is translated into experimental predictions that can be evaluated using contemporary neurophysiological and neuroimaging methodologies, ranging from intracranial electrophysiology to magnetoencephalography and ultrafast fMRI. The formulation emphasizes quantitative, falsifiable expectations rather than qualitative analogies.

The most immediate predictions concern the temporal structure of neural responses during cognitive tasks. The model asserts that the effective depth of nonlinear transformation corresponds to the temporal interval over which a microcircuit integrates information. Since nonlinear transformations accumulate over time rather than over discrete architectural layers, deeper effective compositions should manifest as later peaks in population-level neural activity. This prediction aligns closely with the findings of Goldstein and colleagues, who demonstrated that embeddings from deeper layers of artificial language models correlate with later neural responses in high-order cortical regions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}. The mathematical formulation further predicts that the latency should not increase linearly with depth but logarithmically, owing to the smoothing influence of the semigroup generated by the Laplace--Beltrami operator. This yields a measurable relationship between the temporal evolution of neural responses and the spectral composition of the underlying field, a relationship that can be tested through latency–complexity regression analyses in intracranial electrophysiology.

Another central prediction arises from the spectral geometry of the cortical manifold. Because low-frequency eigenmodes dominate large-scale dynamics, perturbations that bias the field toward certain spectral bands should produce characteristic changes in functional connectivity. The eigenmode framework developed by Roberts and collaborators demonstrates that large-scale cortical patterns align with Laplace--Beltrami eigenfunctions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}. The present model predicts that task engagement should selectively increase the energy of modes whose spatial profiles match the geometry of task-relevant networks, while suppressing those that are irrelevant. This prediction can be tested by applying spatially resolved stimulation, such as transcranial magnetic stimulation or optogenetic perturbations in animal models, and measuring the resulting spectral shifts in MEG or ultrafast fMRI. The ultrafast fMRI work of Cabral, Fernandes, and Shemesh </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} provides a proof of principle that these changes are detectable, as their results reveal reduced modal complexity under anesthesia and robust oscillation patterns in the awake state.

The nonlinear nature of amplistwistor transformations yields a further prediction concerning the interaction between local population activity and global oscillatory structure. In particular, the rotational component of the operator implies that local microcircuits modulate the phase of global eigenmodes, creating transient phase alignments between distant regions. These alignments should occur precisely when nonlinear local transformations induce significant curvature changes in the representational manifold, a phenomenon that has been observed indirectly in synchrony-based studies </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999}. The theory therefore predicts that transient increases in gamma-band activity, reflective of rapid local transformations, should be accompanied by momentary reorganizations of lower-frequency coherence patterns, a relationship that should be detectable using cross-frequency coupling analysis in MEG and dense-array EEG.

A fourth prediction concerns the stability of the cortical field under perturbation. The semigroup formulation demonstrates that diffusion imposes a smoothing kernel on the field, while nonlinear amplification saturates for large inputs. Together these features ensure that the macroscopic dynamics exhibit robustness to moderate perturbations, an observation consistent with the stability of functional networks in noisy environments </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009}. This stability manifests experimentally as a rapid return to baseline eigenmode composition following transient disruption, a pattern measurable in both resting-state fMRI and post-stimulus electrophysiological recordings. Honey, Thivierge, and Sporns provided evidence that structural connectivity predicts these recovery dynamics, but the present model suggests that geometric constraints alone are sufficient to produce rapid re-stabilization </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{honey2010}. Testing this prediction requires carefully controlled perturbations, followed by modal decomposition of the recovery trajectory.

Finally, the model predicts that cognitive phenomena traditionally interpreted as symbolic or sequential, such as syntactic processing or episodic recall, should reveal underlying continuous transformations when examined at sufficiently high temporal resolution. Because amplistwistor cascades approximate continuous mappings, any discrete or stage-like findings in neural data should be explicable in terms of transient transitions between geometrically defined modes rather than genuinely modular stages. This prediction is consistent with the fluid progression from phonetic to semantic processing observed across temporal cortex </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025} and echoes earlier theoretical frameworks describing hierarchical models in cortex as continuous Bayesian updating processes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>hierarchy}. Testing this prediction requires multivariate pattern analyses applied to high-temporal-resolution recordings, with a focus on whether apparent stage-like transitions reflect continuous trajectories in representational space.

Collectively, these predictions provide a rigorous empirical program capable of validating or falsifying the mathematical structure of the model. Their specificity makes them resilient to loose interpretative flexibility, and their grounding in established neuroscience ensures experimental feasibility. The next section situates these predictions within the broader landscape of contemporary theories of cortical computation, clarifying the distinct contributions of the present framework while identifying domains of convergence and divergence with existing models.
