6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Theoretical Context and Relation to Contemporary Neuroscience Models}

A comprehensive account of cortical computation must not only accommodate empirical data and provide mathematically coherent predictions but must also be situated among the major theoretical traditions that have shaped contemporary neuroscience. The present framework intersects with, and departs from, a number of influential models, including predictive coding, dynamical systems theories of brain function, hierarchical Bayesian inference, neural field formulations, and connectome-based approaches to large-scale organization. Understanding these relations is crucial for appreciating both the novelty and the continuity of the amplitwistor–eigenmode field formulation.

Dynamical systems perspectives have long emphasized the importance of nonlinear population dynamics and recurrent architecture in shaping cortical activity. The extensive review by Breakspear </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017} consolidates decades of evidence showing that large-scale cortical networks exhibit both oscillatory and multistable regimes governed by nonlinear interactions among neuronal populations. The field equation developed here inherits much from these traditions, especially the central role of the Laplace operator and the importance of nonlinearities in shaping attractor structure. However, the present account extends earlier formulations by embedding these dynamics within the geometric and spectral structure of the cortical manifold, thereby providing a principled explanation for the spatial regularities observed in empirical data. Earlier neural field theories often posited lateral kernels or ad hoc coupling matrices, whereas the eigenmode-based propagation introduced in this work arises naturally from the geometry of the cortical surface and is supported by ultrafast imaging results </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}.

Predictive coding frameworks, originating from the works of Rao and Ballard and later formalized within the free-energy principle </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>hierarchy}, propose that cortical hierarchies implement iterative Bayesian updates in which superficial layers encode prediction errors and deep layers encode predictions. While predictive coding has enjoyed wide empirical support, particularly in laminar electrophysiology, it traditionally lacks a geometric account of large-scale propagation. The present model does not explicitly implement Bayesian inference but parallels predictive coding in its emphasis on recursive refinement through nonlinear transformations. The amplistwistor provides a local mathematical structure through which prediction-like and error-like signals may be unified, and the eigenmode-driven propagation furnishes the long-range communication mechanism that is often left unspecified in predictive coding models. Rather than relying on specialized circuits for error correction, the PDE formulation implies that discrepancies between expected and observed patterns naturally induce perturbations in the spectral coefficients, producing dynamics that resemble error propagation across a hierarchical system.

Connectome-based models occupy a different conceptual territory. The work of Honey, Thivierge, and Sporns </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{honey2010} demonstrated that structural connectivity imposes strong constraints on functional dynamics, motivating approaches that model cortical activity as a diffusion or wave process on the graph of long-range white-matter tracts. Such models have achieved significant explanatory power but often struggle to explain the continuity of functional patterns across regions with weak anatomical connections. The spectral theory developed by Roberts and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} offers a partial resolution by showing that many functional connectivity patterns align with intrinsic eigenmodes of the cortical surface itself. The present framework explicitly synthesizes this geometric view with local nonlinearities, suggesting that the surface geometry may exert a stronger organizing influence on cortical dynamics than previously appreciated. Long-range connectivity remains relevant, but its influence is mediated through the geometry of the manifold and the spectral structure of the Laplacian.

Oscillatory theories of cognition, particularly those described by Buzsáki </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006} and by Singer's seminal work on temporal binding </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999}, provide another crucial point of comparison. These models emphasize that rhythmic coordination and phase synchrony serve as flexible mechanisms for communication across distributed networks. The rotational component of the amplistwistor formalism maps naturally onto phase modulation and provides a mathematically explicit account of how local nonlinear transformations adjust the phase relationships of global oscillatory patterns. Unlike some oscillatory theories that posit distinct rhythms serving distinct cognitive roles, the PDE model treats rhythms as emergent properties of the interaction between nonlinear operators and spectral geometry. This leads to a unified view in which local computations and global oscillations are inseparable components of a single mathematical process.

The relationship to artificial neural networks and deep learning offers additional perspective. The demonstration by Goldstein and colleagues that deep language model layers align temporally with cortical processing dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025} provides strong empirical grounding for the present theory. The amplistwistor cascades constructed here act analogously to layers of a deep neural network, but with the crucial difference that layer depth in the biological system is implemented temporally rather than spatially. This distinction yields significant theoretical implications: artificial networks achieve expressive power by stacking layers, whereas biological networks achieve the same effect through time-evolving field dynamics governed by a semigroup of operators. The present model interprets the brain's computational capacity as arising from the continuous unfolding of nonlinear transformations constrained by geometry, rather than the explicit modular hierarchy familiar in deep learning.

Finally, the theory intersects with information-integration models, particularly those inspired by Tononi’s information integration theory </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{tononi2004}. Although IIT is concerned primarily with consciousness rather than general computation, the emphasis on global integration and the interplay between local differentiation and global unity resonates strongly with the eigenmode structure of the present model. The PDE formalism provides a mechanism through which integration emerges from the combination of local nonlinearities and global geometric coherence, supplying a dynamical substrate for phenomena that IIT treats at a more abstract level.

Taken together, these comparisons demonstrate that the amplitwistor–eigenmode framework does not stand apart from the main currents of theoretical neuroscience but synthesizes them into a geometric and dynamical formulation with greater mathematical precision. It preserves the recursive and integrative features of predictive coding, the nonlinear richness of neural field theories, the oscillatory coordination emphasized by synchrony-based models, the structural constraints highlighted by connectomics, and the hierarchical refinement characteristic of deep learning. At the same time, it introduces new principles grounded in spectral geometry and operator theory that allow these components to be unified within a single mathematical structure.

The next section draws together the theoretical and empirical threads to articulate the broader implications of this approach for understanding cortical computation, cognition, and the design of biologically informed artificial systems.
