6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Discussion: Implications for Cortical Computation and Cognitive Architecture}

The mathematical and neurophysiological synthesis developed in this manuscript points toward a reconceptualization of cortical computation, one in which global geometric structure, local nonlinear transformations, and temporally unfolding dynamics are not competing metaphors but mutually constraining components of a unified process. The amplitwistor–eigenmode formulation offers a precise path toward this integration by situating computation within a differential-geometric field governed by intrinsic spectral structure and locally defined nonlinear operators. When interpreted alongside contemporary empirical findings, this formalism suggests that the cortex implements a computational architecture strikingly different from the abstractly modular, symbolically decomposed, and discretely layered models that have historically dominated cognitive science.

One of the most significant implications of the present theory concerns the nature of hierarchical processing. Traditional accounts of cortical hierarchy, influenced by classical feed-forward models of vision and language, posit a sequence of transformations arranged in anatomically ordered stages. While laminar and regional distinctions are indisputable, the temporal mapping between deep language-model layers and cortical processing delays </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025} indicates that hierarchy is realized in the brain not primarily through spatial depth but through temporally extended recursive transformations. The present model explains this observation by interpreting each layer-like transformation as a local application of an amplistwistor operator, which acts on the field state as it evolves under global spectral constraints. Recursive application of such operators yields an implicit hierarchy in time rather than in physical circuitry, consistent with earlier dynamical perspectives on cortical computation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{elman1990,saxe2013}. 

This temporal hierarchy interacts intimately with the spectral geometry of the cortical surface. The empirical demonstration by Cabral and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} that global standing modes govern long-range functional connectivity provides compelling evidence that cortical dynamics unfold on a manifold whose intrinsic Laplace–Beltrami eigenfunctions shape the propagation and integration of neural signals. The present model synthesizes these findings by showing how nonlinear local transformations modulate, distort, or entrain these global eigenmodes, producing time-varying patterns that appear as coherent oscillations or slow fluctuations in electrophysiological and hemodynamic recordings. In this respect, the formalism offers a principled explanation for the resonance-like phenomena described in the eigenmode literature </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} and integrates them with the nonlinear population dynamics emphasized in neural field theories </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017,deco2009}. 

A further implication arises from the tension between symbolic and continuous models of cognition. Classical linguistic theories propose that language processing relies on discrete, combinatorial structures, whereas the success of large-scale neural language models suggests that continuous vector representations and nonlinear transformations may capture linguistic competence more directly. The neural evidence presented by Goldstein et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025} shows that the brain’s temporal dynamics are better predicted by embeddings from deep contextual neural networks than by hand-engineered symbolic features. This finding supports the amplitude–twist mechanism of the present model, in which semantic content is encoded not in compositional symbols but in high-dimensional field configurations processed through smooth nonlinear maps. The universal approximation theorems of Cybenko </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989}, Hornik </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hornik1991}, and Poggio and Girosi </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{poggio1990} provide a rigorous foundation for the expressive capacity of such nonlinear cascades, reinforcing the plausibility of amplitwistors as biologically grounded operators that enact transformations functionally analogous to deep learning layers.

The field-theoretic approach advanced here also offers insights into the longstanding question of how local and global computations are coordinated. Oscillatory synchrony has been proposed as a mechanism for binding distributed neural activity </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999}, and the influence of slow cortical rhythms on cognition is well documented </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006}. Yet synchrony itself has often been treated as an empirical regularity rather than a mathematically compelled consequence of cortical geometry. The current model clarifies the origin of such coordination: eigenmodes of the cortical Laplacian impose global structure on activity patterns, while local nonlinearities introduce deviations whose temporal evolution gives rise to transient phase relationships, cross-frequency coupling, and the rich spectral signatures observed in electrophysiological data. The combination of geometric constraints with nonlinear operators yields a natural explanation for the emergence of coherent oscillatory regimes without requiring fine-tuned connectivity or externally imposed rhythms.

These considerations extend to theories of consciousness and integration. Tononi’s formulation of information integration </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{tononi2004} emphasizes that conscious states require both differentiation and unity. The PDE formalism provides an explicit dynamical mechanism for such integration: nonlinear local updates diversify the state space, while global eigenmode structure imposes coherence across large cortical regions. The balance of these forces yields patterns with high internal coordination yet rich informational content, a structure reminiscent of the conditions Tononi associates with integrated information. While the present model does not commit to the full metaphysical claims of IIT, it demonstrates that the mathematical infrastructure necessary for integrated states arises naturally from the interplay of geometry and nonlinear operator dynamics.

An additional implication pertains to computational efficiency and representational economy. The amplitwistor formulation suggests that the cortex operates as a continuously updating dynamical system whose computational capacity is limited not by the depth of anatomical circuitry but by the spectral richness of its intrinsic geometry and the expressive power of its local operators. This offers a parsimonious interpretation of why the brain can achieve semantic and perceptual transformations comparable to those of extremely deep artificial networks within relatively shallow anatomical hierarchies. By leveraging temporal recursion and spectral geometry, the cortex performs what amounts to deep computation without deep physical layers.

Finally, the theory has direct consequences for the design of future artificial systems. The convergence between empirical cortical data and the behavior of transformer-based language models implies that biologically inspired architectures should incorporate temporal recursion, local nonlinear operators with complex phase structure, and global geometric constraints. Unlike standard feed-forward networks, such systems would embody a dynamically evolving state governed by differential operators rather than static layers. This offers a route toward architectures that combine the expressive capacity of deep learning with the temporal and geometric elegance of cortical computation, potentially yielding models that are simultaneously more interpretable, more efficient, and more aligned with biological principles.

In summary, the amplitwistor–eigenmode–PDE framework offers a coherent account of cortical computation that unifies several major theoretical traditions while advancing beyond them in precision and scope. By grounding computation in spectral geometry and nonlinear dynamics, it provides a mathematically principled explanation for hierarchical processing, global coordination, and the temporal evolution of cortical activity. At the same time, it aligns closely with empirical evidence from electrophysiology, neuroimaging, and artificial neural network research, suggesting that the brain's computational architecture is best understood as a temporally unfolding dynamical system embedded within a richly structured geometric manifold. The implications for neuroscience, cognitive science, and artificial intelligence are profound, pointing toward a unified theory in which geometry, dynamics, and computation converge within a single mathematical framework.
