6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Empirical Evidence from Electrophysiology, MEG, fMRI, and Ultrafast Imaging}

Empirical research across neurophysiology, neuroimaging, and computational neuroscience increasingly converges on a set of principles that resonate strongly with the amplitwistor--eigenmode field formalism developed in the present manuscript. High-temporal-resolution recordings from human intracranial electrocorticography, large-scale source-localized MEG, and high-spatial-resolution fMRI---including the recent ultrafast fMRI protocols---jointly reveal that cortical computation is simultaneously nonlinear, temporally recursive, and globally constrained by the intrinsic geometry of the cortex. These observations contrast sharply with models grounded in symbolic processing or feed-forward circuitry, and instead point toward a continuous dynamical system evolving on a structured manifold, precisely the architectural form captured by the present PDE-based field model.

The most direct evidence for temporally unfolding nonlinear transformations comes from intracranial recordings during naturalistic language comprehension. Goldstein and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025} demonstrated that the temporal structure of neural activity in high-order language regions mirrors the depth hierarchy of modern large language models, with deeper layers mapping to later neural latencies. This relationship is strikingly linear in the inferior frontal gyrus, where correlation coefficients exceed </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>r = 0.85</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> with extremely small p-values. The temporal span of these responses, extending up to half a second in the temporal pole, suggests that cortical computation in these regions integrates information over prolonged windows, consistent with the local recurrence and temporal smoothing predicted by the nonlinear PDE dynamics of the amplitwistor mapping. The failure of linear embedding controls in this study underscores the necessity of nonlinear transformations, echoing theoretical results on universal approximation for nonlinear superpositions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989,hornik1991,poggio1990}.

These findings are complemented by MEG studies revealing temporally layered spectral dynamics. Buzsáki's synthesis </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006} provides a comprehensive account of oscillatory hierarchies ranging from delta to gamma bands, each associated with distinct integration windows and cognitive functions. The present model predicts such a spectral stratification as a natural consequence of the Laplace--Beltrami spectrum of the cortical manifold. Empirical work using beamforming and distributed MEG source modeling demonstrates that these oscillations often align with spatially extended eigenmodes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}, suggesting that spectral and geometric structures are not independent but jointly constrain neural activity. This is reinforced by Singer's work on neuronal synchrony </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999}, which shows that oscillatory coherence enables the coordination of spatially distributed populations, a phenomenon that the eigenmode coupling terms in the present PDE explicitly generate.

Functional MRI provides an orthogonal perspective, revealing slow fluctuations and coherent patterns that persist over seconds. Deco, Jirsa, and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009} showed that noise, conduction delays, and coupling geometry jointly shape resting-state network patterns, many of which can be reproduced by nonlinear dynamic models with intrinsic oscillatory modes. Honey, Thivierge, and Sporns </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{honey2010} demonstrated that anatomical connectivity predicts a substantial proportion of functional connectivity patterns, but only when coupled with dynamic models that incorporate nonlinear feedback. These results align with the nonlinear evolution term in the present PDE, where the geometric Laplacian shapes large-scale propagation while amplitwistor nonlinearity introduces local coupling effects essential for capturing empirical networks.

Perhaps the strongest evidence for the role of geometric eigenmodes in shaping cortical activity arises from ultrafast fMRI studies. Cabral, Fernandes, and Shemesh </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} demonstrated that whole-brain dynamics in the rat can be decomposed into standing wave modes---spatially stable eigenfunctions with time-varying coefficients oscillating at characteristic frequencies. The number and complexity of these modes depend on brain state, with anesthesia reducing the dimensionality of the active eigen-spectrum. Roberts and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} similarly showed that many canonical resting-state phenomena emerge naturally from the spectral geometry of the cortical surface, with eigenmodes providing a compact basis for observed functional patterns. These findings align directly with the spectral analysis of Sections~11--13, where the decomposition of fields into eigenfunctions underpins both global coordination and the derivation of efficient semigroup representations.

The nonlinear structure of cortical computation is further supported by dynamical systems analyses of large-scale neural activity. Breakspear </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017} emphasizes that metastable transitions, bifurcations, and multi-scale oscillations are essential features of cortical dynamics, not incidental artifacts. Such phenomena require nonlinear vector fields with the capacity for local expansion and global constraint, precisely the structure encoded in the amplitwistor operator acting on the evolving field. Empirical evidence of spontaneous transitions between dynamic regimes in both electrophysiology and fMRI suggests that cognition arises from itinerant trajectories through high-dimensional state spaces, constrained by geometry yet modifiable by local nonlinear transformations.

The convergence of evidence extends into learning theory and artificial neural networks. Elman's recurrent network results </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{elman1990} demonstrated that hierarchical temporal structure arises spontaneously in systems with local recurrence and nonlinear update rules, a fact later generalized in deep learning analyses by Saxe, McClelland, and Ganguli </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{saxe2013}. The transformer architecture </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{vaswani2017}---despite being ostensibly feed-forward---relies heavily on nonlinear mappings that, in practice, approximate the iterative refinement that dynamical systems naturally perform. The empirical alignment reported by Goldstein et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025} between transformer layers and cortical temporal dynamics therefore provides a compelling bridge between nonlinear operator cascades in artificial networks and the amplitwistor cascades hypothesized in the cortical column.

Taken together, these findings support a view of cortical computation as a multi-scale, nonlinear field evolution governed by geometric constraints and temporally extended transformations. They collectively undermine models positing discrete symbolic manipulation or purely feed-forward circuitry and instead highlight the need for dynamical, continuous, and geometry-aware theories. The amplitwistor--eigenmode framework developed in this manuscript not only fits naturally within this empirical landscape but provides the mathematical clarity, generality, and expressive power required to unify these otherwise disparate observations into a single coherent account of cortical computation.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
