6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Mathematical--Experimental Synthesis and Validation Criteria}

A rigorous scientific theory must not only reproduce known empirical phenomena but must also identify principled mechanisms through which those phenomena arise and produce quantitative predictions testable across modalities. The amplitwistor--eigenmode formalism developed here is a mathematical framework constructed with precisely this dual purpose: to account for a diverse set of neural observations by deriving them from a unified system of partial differential equations, and to generate experimentally actionable predictions that distinguish it from alternative models. The present section synthesizes this connection, establishing how the analytical structure of the model corresponds to empirical findings from ECoG, MEG, fMRI, and ultrafast imaging, and specifying precise criteria by which the theory may be confirmed or falsified.

The central mathematical fact enabling this synthesis is that the evolution operator defined by the nonlinear PDE
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
possesses both a spectral representation, arising from the eigendecomposition of the geometric Laplacian on the cortical manifold, and a nonlinear local transformation structure, encoded in the amplitwistor map. These two components correspond directly to the dual empirical observations that large-scale neural dynamics exhibit stable spatial modes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023,roberts2019} while local cortical microcircuits enact nonlinear transformations essential for hierarchical information processing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025,singer1999,breakspear2017}.

To articulate this connection more precisely, consider first the spectral decomposition of the linear operator. Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m, u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denote the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of the Laplace--Beltrami operator on the cortical surface. The semi-group generated by </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> admits the representation
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=1}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span> e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m t} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0, u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span> u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
which shows that large-scale temporal structure is governed by the spectral geometry of the cortex. Roberts and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} demonstrated that this eigenbasis captures a wide range of resting-state fMRI phenomena, and the ultrafast fMRI results of Cabral et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} revealed that whole-brain activity decomposes measurably into such eigenmodes, particularly under conditions devoid of stimulus-driven inputs. A direct mathematical prediction follows: the decay rates and coupling strengths of eigenmode coefficients should vary systematically with cognitive load, arousal state, and linguistic complexity. This prediction generalizes earlier theoretical claims regarding resting-state fluctuations </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009,honey2010}.

Nonlinearities arise through the amplitwistor map </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, which decomposes into amplitude modulation, orientation-preserving transformation, and projection. From a mathematical standpoint, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> introduces higher-order terms that couple eigenmodes and permit energy to flow across scales. Empirically, this mechanism mirrors the spectral cross-frequency interactions observed in MEG during semantic composition and syntactic binding </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006,singer1999}. One of the clearest predictions of the present model is that such nonlinear couplings should correspond to measurable polynomial or bilinear relationships between eigenmode amplitudes. Specifically, if
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t) u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
then the amplitwistor-induced nonlinear term yields
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dot</span></span><span>{c}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t) = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{j,k} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{mjk} c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j(t) c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k(t) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Theta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{mjk}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> are coefficients determined by cortical geometry and the nonlinearities of microcircuit computation. Detecting such cross-mode interactions experimentally---via bispectral analysis, MEG cross-frequency coupling, or ultrafast fMRI time-series---constitutes a direct test of the amplitwistor interpretation.

The temporal alignment between model dynamics and cortical processing is central to the theory. The empirical discovery that neural activity in high-order language areas unfolds over several hundred milliseconds </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}, systematically mirroring the depth hierarchy of large language models, aligns precisely with the semigroup properties of the PDE. Because </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> defines a natural hierarchy of temporal scales associated with spatial frequencies, the model predicts that deeper semantic transformations correspond to progressive weighting of progressively slower modes. In other words, the temporal pole's extended integration window arises naturally as a dominance of low-frequency eigenmodes, consistent with findings in fMRI </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{honey2010} and electrophysiology </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{elman1990,saxe2013}. This is not merely qualitative agreement; the spectrum </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> can be computed for individual cortical geometries, making this prediction quantitatively testable.

Recurrent amplification and suppression of modes occur when the nonlinear operator drives the system toward transient attractors or metastable states. Breakspear demonstrated </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017} that metastability is a hallmark of large-scale neural dynamics, and Tononi’s formulation of integrated information </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{tononi2004} further implies that coherent states should occupy distinctive regions of the system's phase space. The PDE model predicts that coherent states correspond to fixed points or limit cycles in the amplitude vector </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Such predictions can be assessed by tracking the entropy, kurtosis, or mutual information of mode-weight trajectories during tasks requiring sustained integration, such as narrative comprehension.

Another critical empirical implication concerns predictability and error. Goldstein et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025} observed that the temporal hierarchy persists despite prediction failure, though weakened. In the present model, this corresponds to perturbations in the entropy field component and changes in amplitwistor gain, but without abrupt resetting of the field state. Formally, if the stimulus induces a mismatch between expected and observed trajectories, we obtain a modified evolution equation
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Xi</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Xi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> represents the perturbation induced by prediction error. Small </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Xi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> induces only modest deviations from the trajectory, consistent with the empirical observation that cortical computation is robust to local prediction failure. Larger perturbations, as observed in disorders of consciousness or under anesthesia, may move the system into qualitatively distinct dynamic regimes, a phenomenon extensively documented in ultrafast fMRI work </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} and in MEG studies of impaired synchrony.

Finally, the mathematical framework provides a unifying explanation for the apparent convergence between artificial neural networks and cortical computation. Universal approximation theorems </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989,hornik1991,poggio1990} demonstrate that cascades of nonlinear maps can represent arbitrary functions. The amplitwistor is precisely such a nonlinear map, and its iteration across time fulfills a similar role to the composition of layers in transformer networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{vaswani2017}. This correspondence becomes empirically measurable when neural response patterns correlate with the depth of artificial networks, as demonstrated in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}. The PDE model predicts this alignment because both systems implement successive contractions and rotations in a high-dimensional state space, constrained by geometry.

In summary, the amplitwistor--eigenmode framework is grounded in a precise mathematical structure whose implications map cleanly onto diverse empirical signatures. The spectral geometry of the cortical manifold shapes slow global dynamics, the nonlinear local operators generate cross-frequency coupling and hierarchical transformations, and the semigroup evolution produces temporal integration windows consistent with human electrophysiology. These convergences provide a framework for experimental validation that is simultaneously rigorous, quantitative, and deeply grounded in contemporary neuroscience.
