6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Information-Theoretic and Dynamical Consequences of the Field Equations}

The governing equation for cortical computation,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
carries deep information-theoretic implications that follow from the interaction between its linear geometric component and the nonlinear contributions arising from local microcircuit transformations. In the present section these consequences are developed rigorously, beginning with the entropy dynamics that emerge from the semigroup generated by </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, continuing through analyses of mutual information flow across spatial scales, and culminating in the dynamical behavior of attractors, oscillatory regimes, and transitions between representational states. The central theme is that cortical computation, when viewed through the lens of the PDE, acquires a structure formally analogous to classical results in stochastic thermodynamics and information geometry, but embedded within the biophysically plausible context of eigenmode interactions and microcircuit nonlinearity.

Entropy provides the natural entry point for interpreting the field dynamics in informational terms. If one defines an instantaneous information density by </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>S(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\log</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, where normalization is taken over the cortical manifold, then the temporal evolution of this quantity follows from differentiating under the integral sign and applying the PDE. The linear term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, due to its self-adjointness and dissipative nature on the appropriate function spaces, contributes a monotonic smoothing that reduces high-frequency components of the field and therefore lowers the Fisher information associated with fine spatial distinctions. In this respect the operator plays a role reminiscent of the diffusion term in Fokker–Planck equations, a structure that has been invoked in several treatments of cortical population dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017,deco2009}. The entropy increase associated with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> thus corresponds to the well-known homogenizing effect of large-scale structural connectivity, which tends to wash out microscopic detail in favor of global patterns aligned with the low-eigenvalue eigenmodes documented empirically by Roberts et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} and Cabral et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}.

The nonlinear term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, by contrast, introduces the information-reducing effect characteristic of selective amplification, winner-take-all competition, and gain modulation within microcircuits. These processes have been identified repeatedly in both theoretical and empirical work as critical mechanisms for sharpening representations, reducing redundancy, and producing sparse, high-fidelity codes suitable for higher-order cognition </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston2008}. In the PDE formalism these effects arise from nonlinear contractions in the semantic manifold, embodied in the amplitude-modulating and projection-like components of the amplitwistor operators. The resulting dynamics exhibit an information bottleneck structure, where the nonlinear local transformations emphasize task-relevant modes of the field while suppressing orthogonal or noise-driven contributions. Such sharpening is essential in models of hierarchical semantic processing and explains the selective enhancement of specific eigenmodes observed during linguistic integration </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}.

A balance between the entropy-increasing effects of the linear operator and the entropy-decreasing effects of the nonlinear operator yields a principled mechanism for controlled complexity. If the contribution of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> dominates, the field approaches a high-entropy equilibrium where distinctions between representational states vanish. If the nonlinear term is too dominant, the system risks collapsing into narrow attractors that cannot support flexible computation. Evidence from dynamical systems modelling of cortical networks indicates that biological systems operate in a regime near the boundary between ordered and chaotic dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017}, a notion consistent with the balanced interplay of diffusion-like smoothing and nonlinear sharpening described here. This regime supports metastable transitions between representational states and yields rich temporal structure appropriate for the multi-level processing demands of language and perception.

One of the most salient information-theoretic consequences of the PDE concerns the flow of mutual information between spatial scales. Representations encoded in slow eigenmodes, corresponding to low-frequency global patterns, carry high-level semantic or contextual information, while fast modes encode local details. The temporal transfer of information from fast to slow modes and conversely is mediated by nonlinear mode coupling in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Such inter-scale exchange has been proposed as the basis for hierarchical predictive processing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston2010}, and it manifests empirically in the phase–amplitude coupling documented in electrophysiological studies </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006}. By expressing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> in its spectral decomposition </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t) u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, one can derive explicit expressions for the evolution of the modal amplitudes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The quadratic and higher-order terms in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> generate cross-terms of the form </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k a</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, which act as effective coupling coefficients driving nonlinear resonance between modes. This phenomenon produces the kind of structured coherence observed in MEG, ECoG, and resting-state fMRI, where distinct frequency bands modulate one another while preserving the geometric constraints imposed by </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.

Such nonlinear mode coupling yields a natural explanation for the graded temporal windows of linguistic processing observed in the ECoG findings of Goldstein et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}. Early sensory areas respond predominantly through fast modes, which dissipate rapidly due to their high eigenvalues. Higher-order association cortices, including inferior frontal and temporal pole regions, are dominated by slow eigenmodes whose persistence over hundreds of milliseconds supports the extended integration of semantic content. The PDE predicts not only the existence of these windows but also their quantitative scaling, since the decay rates of the modal amplitudes are governed by the eigenvalues of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. This correspondence establishes a mathematically principled account of temporal hierarchy rooted directly in the spectral geometry of the cortical manifold.

Another important implication arises from the stability structure of the dynamical system. Fixed points of the PDE correspond to representational attractors, while limit cycles or more complex invariant sets represent oscillatory or rhythmic states. Classical bifurcation theory applies in this context, and one can show that changes in gain parameters associated with the amplitwistor operators induce transitions between qualitatively distinct regimes, including stable fixed points, oscillatory behavior, and potentially chaotic dynamics. These transitions mirror neurophysiological findings associated with attention, neuromodulation, and anesthesia. For example, Cabral et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} demonstrated that deep anesthesia suppresses high-frequency eigenmodes and reduces the number of active standing waves, consistent with a movement toward a low-dimensional, low-entropy attractor. Conversely, during wakeful cognition the cortical system occupies a high-dimensional region of state space characterized by rich nonlinear interactions and sustained modal diversity.

Finally, the PDE’s information-theoretic structure suggests constraints on learning and plasticity. If one interprets the nonlinear term as a parameterized transformation whose coefficients evolve over long timescales, then the system performs a form of gradient descent on an implicit energy functional, paralleling learning theories in artificial neural networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{elman1990,saxe2013,vaswani2017}. The universal approximation properties derived earlier imply that, with appropriate parameter adjustments, the cortical field can represent arbitrarily complex mappings between sensory inputs and semantic outcomes. This observation aligns with the empirical fact that cortical circuits exhibit remarkable adaptability across development and learning, and it provides a principled account of how structural plasticity and synaptic modification can reshape the operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> to accommodate new computational demands.

Taken together, the information-theoretic and dynamical consequences of the PDE reveal a cortical architecture that balances smoothing and sharpening, global coherence and local specificity, stability and flexibility. The linear and nonlinear components of the field equation do not compete; rather, they cooperate to produce the sophisticated representational and temporal structure necessary for perception, language, and abstract thought. The result is a biologically plausible computational system whose mathematical properties align with decades of empirical research, while offering new avenues for formal inquiry and experimental validation.
