6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Numerical Methods and Computational Implementation}

The amplitwistor–eigenmode field model acquires full scientific utility only when it can be instantiated in numerical simulations capable of approximating the continuous dynamics on a realistic cortical geometry. The present section develops the methodological foundations required for such simulations. It begins by constructing appropriate spatial discretizations of the cortical manifold, proceeds to the treatment of the linear and nonlinear components of the PDE, and concludes with an analysis of stability, convergence, and interpretability. The exposition follows established frameworks in numerical analysis of parabolic and pseudospectral evolution equations, while incorporating insights from computational neuroscience regarding the structure of cortical connectivity and geometric constraints on neural fields.

The geometry of the problem requires an accurate representation of the cortical surface, which can be modeled as a two-dimensional Riemannian manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> embedded in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>3</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. In practice, the manifold is approximated by a triangulated mesh derived from high-resolution structural MRI. This construction is standard in finite element methods for surface PDEs, and enables a consistent approximation of the Laplace–Beltrami operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> appearing in the linear generator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The eigenstructure of this operator has been studied extensively both in mathematics and neuroscience </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019,honey2010}, and its spectral properties provide a foundation for both explicit time-stepping schemes and spectral solvers. The finite element representation permits the assembly of stiffness and mass matrices governing the diffusion-like term, with convergence guaranteed under classical assumptions on mesh quality and regularity.

The linear component </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> + A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denotes additional linear contributions derived from the amplitwistor decomposition, is naturally treated with implicit or semi-implicit schemes due to its stiff character. Backward Euler or Crank–Nicolson discretization ensures numerical stability even for large time steps, a fact well known from the analysis of parabolic PDEs. When spectral methods are employed, one can leverage the eigenbasis </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> satisfying </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> to express the evolution of the linearized system in closed form via the exponential semigroup </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. This approach mirrors techniques used in neural field models and biophysical simulations of resting-state dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009,breakspear2017}. The eigenfunction expansion also provides a natural dimensionality reduction, since only a finite collection of low-frequency modes is needed to approximate large-scale cortical dynamics. This observation aligns with empirical reports demonstrating that a small set of geometric eigenmodes suffices to reconstruct key aspects of functional connectivity </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023,roberts2019}.

The nonlinear term </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> presents different computational challenges due to its pointwise structure and the possibility of strong gradients introduced by the amplitwistor mapping. Explicit evaluation remains feasible provided appropriate care is taken to avoid instabilities associated with sharp nonlinearities. Operator-splitting methods offer a principled approach: one evolves the linear system over a time increment using the exponential or implicit solver, and separately updates the nonlinear contribution using explicit Euler or higher-order Runge–Kutta steps. This modularity reflects the conceptual decomposition of the amplitwistor into amplitude, twist, and projection operations, each of which can be implemented as a local operator acting on the discretized field. These local transformations resemble the nonlinear activation functions and normalization operations found in artificial neural networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hornik1991,poggio1990,saxe2013}, and their numerical properties have been well characterized in that context.

Stability and convergence of the full scheme follow from standard Trotter–Kato approximations for semilinear evolution equations, provided the nonlinear term satisfies appropriate Lipschitz conditions on bounded sets. These conditions were established analytically in earlier sections, enabling a direct translation to the numerical setting. The resulting scheme converges to the mild solution of the PDE as the mesh is refined and the time step is reduced, ensuring a faithful representation of the continuous dynamics. In practice, adaptive time stepping may be employed to match the multi-scale temporal structure of cortical computation, with smaller increments used in regimes of high nonlinear curvature and larger increments during smoother phases of evolution. The existence of multiple characteristic timescales is consistent with empirical analyses of spontaneous and stimulus-driven cortical activity </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006,singer1999,deco2009}.

A notable virtue of the numerical framework is its capacity to support both direct simulations of the PDE and data-driven inverse problems. Given empirical measurements such as those obtained from ECoG, MEG, or ultrafast fMRI </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025,cabral2023}, one can estimate the spectral coefficients of the field by projecting observed dynamics onto the eigenbasis of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. This projection provides a principled means of inferring latent field states, opening the possibility of reconstructing amplitwistor transformations from neural recordings. Techniques from system identification and variational inference may then be used to estimate parameters governing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, yielding a powerful synthesis of theory and experiment. Such inverse modelling has precedent in computational neuroscience, particularly in dynamic causal modeling </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston2010}, and the geometric PDE setting provides a more general framework for similar analyses.

Simulations conducted in this formalism can reproduce a wide variety of dynamical phenomena described in empirical studies. The interplay of diffusion, geometric filtering, and nonlinear transformation naturally gives rise to spontaneous standing wave patterns, phase-locked oscillations, metastable transitions, and hierarchical temporal responses. These emergent behaviors mirror those seen experimentally in resting-state networks, oscillatory hierarchies, and the temporal unfolding of linguistic processing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{honey2010,breakspear2017,goldstein2025}. Numerical exploration of parameter regimes corresponding to neuromodulatory states, attentional engagement, or anesthesia provides a computational platform for testing hypotheses about the functional significance of eigenmode modulation and microcircuit nonlinearities.

The resulting computational framework thus enables a systematic dialogue between theory and experiment. It affords a means of refining the mathematical structure of the field equations based on empirical discrepancies, while also generating precise predictions for neural dynamics across spatial and temporal scales. The combination of geometric fidelity, spectral efficiency, and nonlinear expressiveness renders the method suitable not only for neuroscientific research but for broader applications in dynamical systems and machine intelligence. Its utility mirrors, in a continuous setting, the role that deep neural networks have played in modeling hierarchical structure in time and space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{elman1990,saxe2013,vaswani2017}. By grounding such computation in the intrinsic geometry of the cortex, the present framework offers a biologically and mathematically principled alternative to conventional network architectures.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
