6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Simulations and Computational Experiments}

The theoretical and numerical structures developed in preceding sections acquire their scientific salience most clearly in simulated experiments that reproduce and predict empirical neural phenomena. The amplitwistor–eigenmode field model lends itself naturally to such simulations. Its semilinear nature permits efficient numerical integration, while its geometric formulation ensures that simulated patterns remain faithful to the spatial constraints of the cortex. In this section the relationship between theoretical predictions, numerical realizations, and empirical data is examined in detail, with emphasis on phenomena documented in electrophysiology, MEG, and ultrafast fMRI.

Simulations begin with an initialization reflecting the empirical distribution of cortical activity in a given cognitive state. For resting-state conditions this initialization often consists of a broadband combination of low-amplitude eigenmodes, consistent with empirical observations that spontaneous cortical activity reflects large-scale geometric constraints </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023,roberts2019,honey2010}. When the PDE is evolved forward in time, the linear operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> immediately imposes a geometric structure on the evolving field by damping high-frequency components and accentuating slow modes. This behavior parallels resting-state fMRI dynamics reported by Deco, Jirsa, and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009}, where noise-driven fluctuations evolve under the anatomical connectome to produce coherent low-frequency patterns. In the simulations, these patterns typically manifest as spatially extended standing waves whose morphology is determined by the eigenfunctions of the cortical Laplacian. Their persistence across time mirrors the ultrafast fMRI results of Cabral and Shemesh </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}, where stationary modes were found to underlie long-range functional connectivity even in the absence of explicit task demands.

When nonlinear amplitwistor transformations are added to the evolution, the simulated dynamics acquire a richness absent from linear models. The amplitude terms heighten local contrast, the rotational components introduce directional modulations, and the projection operations selectively suppress or enhance features along preferred axes of the field. Together these actions generate a repertoire of activity patterns that resemble empirical reports of oscillatory hierarchies, traveling and standing waves, and metastable transitions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{buzsaki2006,singer1999,breakspear2017}. Indeed, by adjusting amplitwistor parameters to mimic shifts in neuromodulatory tone or attentional engagement, simulations reproduce characteristic changes in spectral composition and coherence documented in electrophysiological data. Slow eigenmodes become more prominent during simulated drowsiness, matching observations of reduced modal diversity under anesthesia </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}, while increased nonlinear coupling induces faster oscillatory cycles associated with alert cognitive states.

Language processing provides an especially revealing illustration of the model’s explanatory power. When linguistic inputs are introduced as time-varying boundary or forcing terms, the simulated field evolves in a temporally layered fashion reminiscent of empirical findings from ECoG </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}. Early in the simulation, transient high-frequency perturbations rapidly dissipate, mimicking the brief, localized activity in primary auditory areas. As the field progresses, nonlinear interactions between eigenmodes generate increasingly long temporal integration windows. This gradual deepening of temporal structure closely parallels the layer-by-layer temporal progression observed in inferior frontal and temporal pole regions, where neural responses follow a hierarchy extending up to several hundred milliseconds </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}. In simulations, this hierarchy arises naturally from the interaction of diffusively constrained linear dynamics and recursively applied nonlinear transformations, a structure strongly reminiscent of hierarchical processing in recurrent neural networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{elman1990} and deep transformer models </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{vaswani2017}.

The model also reproduces key aspects of predictive processing. When simulations incorporate external inputs representing predicted future stimuli, mismatch between predicted and actual inputs manifests as transient increases in nonlinear energy, producing sharp fluctuations in modal amplitudes. These fluctuations resemble the electrophysiological signatures of prediction error described in hierarchical Bayesian models of cortical computation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston2010}. The ability of the PDE to sustain stable trajectories in the presence of prediction errors, without collapsing to noise-dominated regimes, accounts for the empirical finding that the temporal hierarchy of linguistic responses persists even when predicted words fail to occur </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}. The nonlinear term contracts deviations into the semantic manifold, ensuring stable yet flexible computation.

Simulations further reveal the interplay between geometry and computation. When the cortical manifold is artificially flattened or distorted, the spectrum of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> alters dramatically, leading to qualitatively different temporal and spatial dynamics. These distortions provide insight into neuropathological conditions where cortical geometry or connectivity is disrupted. For example, reducing long-range connectivity in the discretized manifold diminishes the influence of low-frequency eigenmodes, yielding fragmented activity reminiscent of disrupted synchrony in schizophrenia </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999} or disorders of consciousness </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009}. Conversely, enhancing nonlinear gain simulates epileptiform bursts, again demonstrating the model’s capacity to replicate empirically documented pathological states.

A powerful feature of the simulation framework is its ability to generate synthetic datasets suitable for comparison with experimental measurements. By mapping the field </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(t,r)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> to hypothetical sensor or voxel space using empirically derived forward models, one can produce synthetic ECoG, MEG, or fMRI time series. These synthetic signals exhibit temporal autocorrelations, power spectra, and spatial coherence patterns that match many features of empirical recordings </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017,buzsaki2006}. In the case of ultrafast fMRI, simulated BOLD responses filtered through hemodynamic models yield standing-wave patterns analogous to those observed by Cabral et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}. These comparisons establish quantitative benchmarks for validating the PDE’s dynamical assumptions and provide avenues for refining parameters to improve correspondence with empirical data.

In sum, simulations derived from the amplitwistor–eigenmode PDE demonstrate that the model captures an impressive breadth of empirically observed cortical phenomena. From resting-state coherence to hierarchical linguistic processing, from oscillatory hierarchies to pathological disruptions, the computational experiments corroborate the theoretical claim that cortical computation emerges from the interaction of geometric constraints and nonlinear local transformations. The resulting synthetic datasets bridge the gap between mathematical theory and biological measurement, establishing the model as a robust tool for neuroscience and providing a foundation for predictive, experimentally testable hypotheses.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
