6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Relation to Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning}

The mathematical structure embodied in the amplitwistor–eigenmode PDE has profound connections with modern artificial intelligence, particularly with the architectures that currently dominate machine learning: deep feed-forward networks, recurrent neural networks, and transformer-based models. These connections are not merely analogical, nor do they rest upon superficial similarities between biological and artificial systems. Instead, they arise from deep structural correspondences between nonlinear operator cascades in functional spaces, hierarchical temporal integration, and the spectral geometry of information flow, all of which have been articulated in the theoretical and empirical sections of this manuscript.

One of the most direct points of correspondence lies in the theory of universal approximation. Classical results by Cybenko </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989}, Hornik </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hornik1991}, and Poggio and Girosi </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{poggio1990} established that sufficiently wide or deep networks implementing nonlinear transformations can approximate any continuous function on compact subsets of Euclidean space. While these results were originally framed in finite-dimensional settings, the amplitwistor operators introduced earlier generalize the functional form of such approximators to Riemannian manifolds. Each amplitwistor transformation applies a nonlinear combination of amplification, rotation, and projection to the field </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and repeated compositions generate a cascade of operators that mirror the layered structure of deep networks. The fact that these operations act pointwise or locally in the spatial domain reflects the locality of microcircuit transformations, yet their composition across time reproduces the hierarchical depth characteristic of deep learning architectures. The theoretical results in Section 10 extend classical approximation theorems by showing that amplitwistor compositions densely span a broad class of nonlinear operators on manifold-valued fields, providing a principled bridge between infinite-dimensional PDE dynamics and finite-dimensional neural networks.

Temporal processing introduces an additional layer of correspondence. Elman’s pioneering work </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{elman1990} demonstrated that recurrent neural networks acquire hierarchical temporal structure when exposed to sequential data. Later studies elaborated these dynamics in the context of deep networks, where the eigenvalue structure of linear components and the saturation of nonlinear activations give rise to slow modes that process long-range dependencies </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{saxe2013}. The amplitwistor–eigenmode PDE provides a continuous formulation of this mechanism. The linear operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> generates low-frequency geometric eigenmodes with long temporal persistence, exactly replicating the slow temporal structure needed for hierarchical processing. The nonlinear operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> introduces rapid, context-sensitive refinements, analogous to the local nonlinearities applied within each layer of a deep network. Thus, the PDE model provides a mathematically principled analogue of deep recurrent computation, where depth in artificial architectures corresponds not to discrete layers but to evolving trajectories within an operator-generated semigroup.

The relationship with transformer architectures </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{vaswani2017} is more subtle but equally significant. Transformers rely on multi-head self-attention mechanisms to perform content-sensitive integration across tokens, and the resulting representations evolve through a stack of nonlinear transformations that refine semantic structure. Empirical results by Goldstein et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025} showed that the temporal hierarchy of human cortical responses during language processing mirrors the depth hierarchy of transformer models, with deeper layers corresponding to later neural latencies and broader temporal receptive windows. In the PDE framework, these temporal receptive windows emerge from the modal decomposition induced by the cortical geometry. Shallow eigenmodes dissipate quickly, providing early-stage processing analogous to shallow transformer layers, while deeper eigenmodes integrate information over longer periods, corresponding to later layers. The nonlinear amplitwistor mapping further refines these modes in a context-dependent manner, reproducing the essential computational features of self-attention without requiring explicit pairwise token interactions. From this perspective, attention-like behavior arises not from discrete architectural motifs but from continuous geometric and nonlinear transformations.

Spectral analysis provides yet another link between the PDE formalism and artificial architectures. Several lines of machine learning research have recognized that neural networks implicitly exploit eigenmode structure. For instance, convolutional networks approximate localized filters corresponding to low-frequency eigenfunctions on Euclidean grids, while graph neural networks generalize these principles to irregular domains by using graph Laplacian eigenvectors as a basis for message passing. The amplitwistor–eigenmode PDE extends these insights to the smooth cortical surface, where the Laplace–Beltrami spectrum provides a biologically grounded coordinate system that shapes information flow. The PDE formulation thereby reveals that neural networks and cortical computation share a deep mathematical substrate: both rely on geometric operators whose spectra encode the degrees of freedom available for information processing.

These correspondences acquire additional interpretive power when considered in the context of learning. Numerical experiments described earlier demonstrate that adjusting the parameters of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> permits the PDE system to approximate diverse dynamical behaviors. In machine learning, such parameter adjustment corresponds to gradient-based optimization. Though the biological substrate lacks the backpropagation mechanism used in artificial networks, the PDE formulation suggests alternative learning principles that achieve comparable functional outcomes. For example, one may interpret the dynamics of neural adaptation as a slow-timescale evolution of the operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, guided by error signals or mismatches between predicted and observed activity, in line with theories of predictive processing </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston2010}. The amplitwistor PDE thus provides a mathematical framework in which biological learning and artificial gradient descent appear not as fundamentally distinct processes but as different instantiations of operator evolution in functional space.

The comparison extends to the interpretability of internal representations. Studies of transformer models have shown that intermediate layers encode linguistic or semantic structure in ways that mirror human intuitions about syntax and meaning. In the PDE framework, similar structure emerges through the interaction of geometric eigenmodes and nonlinear amplitwistors. Lower-order eigenmodes encode global, slowly varying semantic contours, while higher-order modes contribute local context and rapid corrections. The combination produces internal representations whose structure can be probed through spectral decomposition, offering a principled method for analyzing the interpretability of cortical computation. This stands in contrast to the opaque internal structure of deep networks, and suggests that insights from geometry and PDEs may inform the development of more interpretable artificial systems.

Finally, the PDE model offers avenues for developing new architectures inspired directly by cortical computation. Because amplitwistor transformations act as pointwise nonlinear operators and eigenmode dynamics provide domain-wide coherence, one can construct artificial systems in which layers correspond to time steps of a PDE evolution, and attention-like refinement emerges naturally from the nonlinear structure of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Such architectures would differ from transformers in form but not in computational capability, and might achieve greater stability, interpretability, or biological plausibility. They would also inherit universal approximation capabilities </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989,hornik1991} and hierarchical representation learning properties </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{saxe2013}, thereby unifying computational and biological principles within a single operator-theoretic design.

Taken together, these correspondences demonstrate that the amplitwistor–eigenmode PDE is not merely a formal model of neural computation but a mathematical bridge linking biological and artificial intelligence. Its geometric, dynamical, and information-theoretic structure encompasses the essential computational features of deep learning while providing a principled biological grounding. This synthesis not only enriches our understanding of cortical computation but also suggests new directions for the design of artificial architectures capable of matching or surpassing the efficiency, flexibility, and interpretability of biological systems.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
