6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Neurophysiological Predictions and Falsifiable Claims}

A central virtue of any theoretical framework in neuroscience is its capacity to articulate neurophysiological predictions that are simultaneously precise, mechanistically grounded, and empirically falsifiable. The amplitwistor–eigenmode model presented in this manuscript was developed with that criterion in mind. Although its PDE formulation, geometric operators, and nonlinear transformations are mathematically intricate, the theory nevertheless commits to a set of quantitative expectations about how the cortex ought to behave under a wide range of conditions. These predictions arise not as rhetorical extrapolations but as necessary consequences of the model’s structure, particularly the semigroup evolution generated by </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and the nonlinear twisting operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. This section outlines these consequences in a manner that distinguishes the present theory from competing accounts of cortical computation.

The first class of predictions concerns the temporal structure of cortical responses during naturalistic cognition. Because the amplitwistor dynamics enforce a progressive accumulation of deformation under repeated application of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, the theory requires that increasingly abstract representations must appear only after a sequence of temporally ordered transformations. This is not merely compatible with the findings of Goldstein and colleagues </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}; it is demanded by the model. If neural recordings in high-level language regions failed to show such temporally expanding receptive windows, or if deeper semantic measures appeared as early as phonetic features, the theory would be directly contradicted. Likewise, any empirical observation that non-linear representational transformations occur out of sequence—such as deep semantic activation preceding syntactic parsing—would violate the causal ordering imposed by the semigroup associated with the PDE. The theory therefore stakes itself on the claim that temporal depth is inseparable from semantic depth and that disruptions to the latter must produce measurable distortions in the former.

A second family of predictions arises from the geometric structure of cortical eigenmodes. The cortical surface, modeled as a compact Riemannian manifold, admits a discrete spectrum of eigenfunctions that govern the spatial coupling of neural fields. In the present framework, these eigenfunctions do not merely describe possible patterns of activity but actively constrain the dynamics by modulating the amplistwistor cascades. As a consequence, the theory predicts that any perturbation affecting the geometry of the cortical manifold—whether through focal stimulation, injury, congenital malformation, or neurosurgical resection—should lead to specific and predictable distortions in the spectrum of observable oscillations. For example, removal or inactivation of a region that contributes strongly to a low-frequency eigenmode should selectively attenuate long-range coherence while leaving local high-frequency interactions largely intact. Competing models lacking explicit geometric coupling do not make such spatially differentiated predictions. A failure to observe these eigenvalue-dependent distortions in response to geometric perturbation would therefore undermine one of the cornerstone mechanisms of the model.

The nonlinear component of the PDE also gives rise to predictions that may be examined in electrophysiological recordings. Because the twisting dynamics impose a form of local amplification and rotation in the representational state space, the theory predicts characteristic signatures in phase–amplitude and phase–phase coupling measures. In particular, high-frequency oscillations should be entrained by the deformation imposed by lower-frequency eigenmodes, resulting in frequency-dependent cross-talk that reflects the structure of the nonlinear operator. If such entrainment were absent, or if its strength bore no relation to the eigenmode weights inferred from hemodynamic imaging, the amplitwistor formulation would lose plausibility. Conversely, strong alignment between the spectral geometry measured in fMRI and the coupling patterns observed in MEG or ECoG would provide striking confirmation of the theory’s multi-scale coherence. The model is thus falsifiable by both the presence and absence of appropriate cross-frequency signatures.

The stochastic component of the dynamics also imposes testable constraints. If noise enters the system through the operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\eta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> as formulated, then its propagation through the semigroup should reflect the eigenvalue spectrum of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. This implies that perturbations introduced at high frequencies should dissipate rapidly whereas low-frequency fluctuations should persist for durations proportional to the inverse of the smallest non-zero eigenvalues. Empirically, this predicts that resting-state fluctuations should exhibit strong spatial coherence at low frequencies and should relax slowly after perturbation, in accordance with classical results on diffusion on manifolds </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019, honey2010}. Should empirical measurements reveal that high-frequency noise propagates with similar persistence to low-frequency components, or that spatial correlations do not respect the geometric constraints implied by the Laplace–Beltrami operator, the model would again be contradicted.

A further domain of prediction involves alterations in neuromodulatory state. Because the nonlinear twisting term incorporates a gain parameter that modulates the magnitude of deformation applied at each integrative step, the theory predicts that neuromodulatory shifts affecting synaptic efficacy should manifest as changes in the ``speed of semantic time.'' An increase in gain should compress semantic integration windows, producing premature convergence in high-order cortical regions; a reduction in gain should produce the opposite. Pharmacological manipulations that alter cholinergic, dopaminergic, or glutamatergic tone are therefore expected to produce measurable changes in both the temporal structure of cortical responses and the weighting of eigenmodes. Competing accounts that lack explicit gain-mediated deformation do not yield analogous predictions. Thus, if neuromodulatory interventions do not systematically affect the temporal hierarchy of representation, the amplitwistor model would face a substantive challenge.

Finally, the theory makes predictions concerning pathological cognition. Disorders characterized by disrupted connectivity, impaired coherence, or aberrant oscillatory patterns—such as schizophrenia, temporal lobe epilepsy, and certain forms of aphasia—should correspond to identifiable distortions in the eigenmode spectrum or to maladaptive configurations of the nonlinear twisting dynamics. For instance, schizophrenia is expected to exhibit excessive participation of intermediate eigenmodes and reduced stability of low-frequency modes, reflecting impaired integration. Epileptic tissue is predicted to show abnormally high gain in the nonlinear operator, resulting in excessive local twisting that overwhelms global geometric constraints. The success or failure of these predictions across patient populations, when analyzed with appropriate controls and imaging methods, will serve as a strong test of the theory’s explanatory scope.

What distinguishes these predictions from speculative conjecture is the clarity with which failure would undermine the theory. If temporal depth does not correspond to representational depth; if cortical eigenmodes do not regulate long-range coherence; if cross-frequency coupling does not reflect nonlinear twisting; if noise propagation does not respect geometric diffusion; if neuromodulatory changes do not alter semantic integration times; or if pathological distortions do not correspond to predictable alterations in eigenmode structure—then the theory presented here would be falsified. It is precisely this willingness to expose itself to empirical risk that elevates the model from a conceptual curiosity to a testable scientific hypothesis.
