6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Conclusion}

The aim of this work has been to articulate a mathematically precise and biologically plausible account of cortical computation grounded in the interplay between local nonlinear transformations and global geometric constraints. By developing the amplitwistor formalism and embedding it within the dynamical structure imposed by eigenmodes of the cortical surface, the present theory advances a unified model in which the fundamental mechanisms of neural information processing become intelligible in geometric and analytic terms. The resulting framework does not merely provide a qualitative metaphor for cognition; it yields a set of explicit operators, spectral decompositions, and dynamical equations capable of reproducing the temporal, spatial, and computational characteristics observed in empirical studies.

The theory demonstrates that the cortex should be understood as a nonlinear dynamical system whose representational states evolve through iterative geometric deformations. These deformations, governed by twisting, amplification, and projection operators, supply the expressive capacity traditionally associated with multilayer neural architectures </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989, hornik1991}. Yet unlike conventional artificial networks, which operate in discrete layers, the cortex supports a continuous unfolding of representational trajectories over time. This temporal depth is not a secondary by-product of biophysics but a necessary component of semantic computation, reflecting the requirement that longer-range integrative processes develop on correspondingly extended timescales </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{elman1990, friston</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>hierarchy}. The mathematical formalization of this requirement, achieved through semigroup analysis and spectral theory, clarifies why higher-order linguistic and conceptual processes cannot occur instantaneously and why cortical dynamics exhibit graded temporal receptive fields spanning hundreds of milliseconds </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}.

The geometric structure of the cortical manifold plays an equally indispensable role. A growing body of work has shown that substantial features of large-scale brain activity can be reproduced from geometric and connectomic constraints alone </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019, honey2010, breakspear2017}. Here these insights have been extended by demonstrating that the spatial eigenmodes of the cortex not only constrain low-frequency resting-state dynamics but also participate actively in the organization of stimulus-driven computation. These eigenmodes provide the background geometry against which nonlinear transformations unfold, imposing coherence across distant regions and generating the resonant patterns that have been observed in ultrafast neuroimaging studies </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}. The interaction between local twisting and global resonant modes yields a picture of neural computation that is both distributed and coordinated, capable of supporting context-sensitive integration while preserving stability in the presence of noise </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009}.

A central contribution of this work is the reconciliation of universal approximation principles with biological realism. Although deep learning theory has long demonstrated the necessity of nonlinear transformations for expressive function approximation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{poggio1990, saxe2013}, the biological interpretation of these mechanisms has remained underdeveloped. The amplitwistor model offers a natural geometric instantiation of these principles in cortical microcircuitry, showing how repeated nonlinear deformation within local circuits can approximate arbitrarily complex semantic transformations. This insight helps to clarify why artificial neural networks and biological cortical systems exhibit parallel structural and functional properties, despite their vastly different implementations </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{vaswani2017}. It also suggests that the success of modern deep learning architectures may arise, at least in part, from their inadvertent approximation of the geometric and dynamical structure characteristic of biological computation.

The empirical alignment between the theory and neurophysiological data provides an additional source of validation. The identification of a strong monotonic relationship between LLM layer depth and neural response latency </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025} offers compelling evidence that the cortex indeed performs representational transformations of increasing complexity over time. The theory developed here predicts this behavior from first principles, showing that deeper semantic transformations require additional iterations of nonlinear twisting and thus unfold over longer temporal intervals. The appearance of low-frequency standing waves in resting-state dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} further corroborates the model by demonstrating the existence of global eigenmodes capable of coordinating local transformations across the cortical surface. These observations, taken together, demonstrate that the amplitwistor–eigenmode framework is not merely mathematically coherent but grounded in experimentally verifiable features of cortical physiology.

The present work also opens several avenues for future investigation. The mathematical framework developed here is sufficiently explicit to permit direct numerical simulation, making it possible to evaluate the theory's predictions in controlled computational experiments. Moreover, the theory suggests specific empirical tests, such as manipulation of eigenmode structure through targeted stimulation or examination of twisting-induced distortions in semantic representational geometry. It also offers a principled foundation for the development of new machine learning architectures inspired by biological computation, particularly those that integrate geometric constraints with recurrent nonlinear transformations. Such architectures may bridge the gap between artificial and biological intelligence more effectively than existing models.

In conclusion, the amplitwistor formalism and its integration with cortical eigenmodes provide a unified geometric and dynamical account of cortical computation. The theory succeeds in linking microcircuit-level nonlinearities to macroscale resonance phenomena, capturing within a single mathematical structure the essential features of perception, language, and cognition. It demonstrates that the cortex's computational power arises not from isolated mechanisms but from the lawful interaction of geometry, dynamics, and time. While the work presented here does not claim to exhaust the complexity of cortical function, it establishes a robust foundation on which future theoretical and empirical studies may build. The synthesis of mathematics, neuroscience, and computation embodied in this framework offers a compelling path toward a deeper understanding of the principles that govern cognitive systems, both biological and artificial.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
