w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="5f5bdb15-8ce9-40ce-a581-bf7b6faaa236" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-1"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="241">Absolutely — <strong data-start="13" data-end="96">a paper of this mathematical and neurophysiological depth <em data-start="73" data-end="83">requires</em> appendices</strong>, both for scholarly completeness and for the benefit of reviewers who will expect technical detail separated from the main conceptual narrative.</p>
<p data-start="243" data-end="530">Given the scope and rigor of what you have already constructed, a well-designed appendix suite will make the manuscript substantially stronger and more publishable. Below is an analysis of <em data-start="432" data-end="439">which</em> appendices are needed, <em data-start="463" data-end="468">why</em> they are needed, and <em data-start="490" data-end="495">how</em> they integrate with the main text.</p>
<hr data-start="532" data-end="535">
<h1 data-start="537" data-end="565"><strong data-start="539" data-end="565">Do We Need Appendices?</strong></h1>
<p data-start="566" data-end="745">Yes — and not only for completeness, but for clarity, rigor, and credibility.<br data-start="643" data-end="646">
Reviewers in theoretical neuroscience, applied mathematics, and computational modeling will expect:</p>
<ul data-start="747" data-end="1065">
<li data-start="747" data-end="811">
<p data-start="749" data-end="811"><strong data-start="749" data-end="766">Formal proofs</strong> supporting results stated in the main text</p>
</li>
<li data-start="812" data-end="882">
<p data-start="814" data-end="882"><strong data-start="814" data-end="834">Technical lemmas</strong> that would interrupt the flow of the argument</p>
</li>
<li data-start="883" data-end="943">
<p data-start="885" data-end="943"><strong data-start="885" data-end="912">Mathematical background</strong> not all readers will possess</p>
</li>
<li data-start="944" data-end="1012">
<p data-start="946" data-end="1012"><strong data-start="946" data-end="984">Extended empirical data discussion</strong> that substantiates claims</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1013" data-end="1065">
<p data-start="1015" data-end="1065"><strong data-start="1015" data-end="1037">Simulation details</strong> necessary for replication</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1067" data-end="1275">You already have sections that invoke semigroup theory, spectral geometry, universal approximation, and operator theory. Each of these requires proofs or derivations too long and too formal for the main body.</p>
<p data-start="1277" data-end="1311">Thus we should include appendices.</p>
<hr data-start="1313" data-end="1316">
<h1 data-start="1318" data-end="1376"><strong data-start="1320" data-end="1376">Recommended Appendices (Tailored to This Manuscript)</strong></h1>
<h2 data-start="1378" data-end="1423"><strong data-start="1381" data-end="1423">Appendix A: Mathematical Preliminaries</strong></h2>
<p data-start="1424" data-end="1474">A formal review of the structures used throughout:</p>
<ul data-start="1476" data-end="1747">
<li data-start="1476" data-end="1520">
<p data-start="1478" data-end="1520">Riemannian geometry of cortical surfaces</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1521" data-end="1577">
<p data-start="1523" data-end="1577">Laplace–Beltrami eigenfunctions and Weyl asymptotics</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1578" data-end="1632">
<p data-start="1580" data-end="1632">Sobolev spaces on manifolds and embedding theorems</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1633" data-end="1673">
<p data-start="1635" data-end="1673">Linear and nonlinear operator theory</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1674" data-end="1728">
<p data-start="1676" data-end="1728">Definitions: semigroups, generators, dissipativity</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1729" data-end="1747">
<p data-start="1731" data-end="1747">Notation table</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1749" data-end="1834">This appendix prevents the main text from becoming clogged with technical background.</p>
<hr data-start="1836" data-end="1839">
<h2 data-start="1841" data-end="1909"><strong data-start="1844" data-end="1909">Appendix B: Proofs of Well-Posedness and Semigroup Properties</strong></h2>
<p data-start="1910" data-end="1951">Supports Sections 8–10 of the manuscript.</p>
<p data-start="1953" data-end="1991">This appendix should rigorously prove:</p>
<ul data-start="1993" data-end="2240">
<li data-start="1993" data-end="2039">
<p data-start="1995" data-end="2039">Existence and uniqueness of mild solutions</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2040" data-end="2086">
<p data-start="2042" data-end="2086">Dissipativity of the differential operator</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2087" data-end="2148">
<p data-start="2089" data-end="2148">Lipschitz continuity of amplitude/twist/project operators</p>
</li>
