</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Appendix A. Mathematical Preliminaries}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\addcontentsline</span></span><span>{toc}{section}{Appendix A. Mathematical Preliminaries}

This appendix provides a concise but rigorous introduction to the mathematical structures employed throughout the body of the paper. The analysis of cortical fields requires a synthesis of Riemannian geometry, functional analysis, operator semigroup theory, and spectral methods, all of which appear implicitly in the formal development of the main results. The purpose here is to present foundational material in a manner that is self-contained while avoiding unnecessary duplication of standard texts.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.1. Riemannian Manifolds and the Cortical Surface}

The neocortical sheet is modeled as a smooth, compact, oriented two-dimensional Riemannian manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> embedded in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>3</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The induced metric is denoted by </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and the associated volume element is written </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{d}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Compactness ensures a discrete Laplace--Beltrami spectrum, and smoothness provides regularity of eigenfunctions. Standard references, such as Petersen's treatment of Riemannian geometry or the spectral monograph by Chavel, supply the foundational results concerning curvature, injectivity radius, and the existence of geodesic normal coordinates.

The Laplace--Beltrami operator is defined by
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g f = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{div}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g f),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is the metric gradient and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{div}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is the metric divergence. For vector fields, we employ the Hodge–de Rham Laplacian, though scalar fields suffice for most of the development.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.2. Sobolev Spaces and Embedding Theorems}

For </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{N}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> p &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, the Sobolev space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>W</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{k,p}(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> consists of functions with weak derivatives up to order </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> lying in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>p(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. On compact manifolds, norms defined via coordinate charts are equivalent. Of particular relevance is the space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1(M) = W</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{1,2}(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, which supports the energy form associated with the Laplacian.

Sobolev embeddings on two-dimensional compact manifolds assert that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1(M) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\hookrightarrow</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>q(M) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{for all } q &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
and the Rellich–Kondrachov theorem ensures compact embeddings into </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. These results prove essential for bounding nonlinear terms in the field evolution equation, particularly those involving multiplicative and composite operators.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.3. Operator Semigroups}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>X</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> be a Banach space. A family of bounded linear operators </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>T(t): X </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> X</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> for </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\geq</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a strongly continuous semigroup if </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>T(0) = I</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>T(t+s) = T(t)T(s)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>T(t)f </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> for all </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>f </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> X</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. The Hille--Yosida theorem furnishes necessary and sufficient conditions for an operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> to generate such a semigroup.

In the present framework, the linear part of the cortical field operator is shown to be dissipative, permitting the construction of a strongly continuous contraction semigroup </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tL}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. This semigroup provides the foundational smoothing mechanism that appears repeatedly in qualitative, spectral, and stability analyses.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.4. Spectral Theory of the Laplace--Beltrami Operator}

The spectrum of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> on a compact manifold is discrete:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
0 = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Eigenfunctions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> form an orthonormal basis for </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Weyl's law states that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{2/n},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>n=2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> for cortical surfaces. These classical results underpin the eigenmode formulation of cortical activity widely used in computational neuroscience </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} and ultrafast fMRI studies </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.5. Nonlinear Operators and Lipschitz Conditions}

The nonlinear transformation operators appearing in the main text require Lipschitz continuity on appropriate Banach spaces. A map </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>N: X </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> X</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is locally Lipschitz if for every bounded subset </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>B </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subset</span></span><span> X</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> there exists </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>B &gt; 0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> such that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>N(u) - N(v)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>B </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>u - v</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{for all } u,v </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> B.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
These bounds facilitate the use of Picard iteration and compactness arguments when proving local and global well-posedness of the nonlinear field equation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{A.6. Notation Summary}

Throughout the paper, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denotes the Laplace--Beltrami operator, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tL}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> the generated semigroup, and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> the nonlinear term decomposed into amplitude, twist, and projection components. Norm symbols are used exclusively in the context of Hilbert or Banach spaces to avoid ambiguity.

The material in this appendix provides the mathematical setting for the well-posedness, approximation, and spectral results appearing later. Its purpose is to serve as both a reference and a foundation for the analytic arguments that follow.
