7 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Appendix B. Proofs of Well-Posedness and Semigroup Properties}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\addcontentsline</span></span><span>{toc}{section}{Appendix B. Proofs of Well-Posedness and Semigroup Properties}

This appendix establishes rigorous well-posedness for the nonlinear cortical field equation
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> = L</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> + N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L = c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g - c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 I</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a dissipative linear operator and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denotes the nonlinear combination of amplitude, twist, and projection operators introduced in the main body of the paper. Existence, uniqueness, and regularity are shown using classical semigroup techniques and fixed-point arguments, following methods outlined in standard texts on nonlinear evolutionary equations.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{B.1. Dissipativity of the Linear Operator}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> act on the Hilbert space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H = L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> with domain </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>D(L) = H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. For smooth </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span> = c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span> - c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Since </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is non-positive and self-adjoint,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span> = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
which yields
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> -c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Thus </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is dissipative. Standard results ensure that </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\overline</span></span><span>{D(L)} = H</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, so the Lumer--Phillips theorem guarantees that </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> generates a strongly continuous contraction semigroup </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tL}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{B.2. Smoothing Estimates}

For </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>t&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, the operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tL}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> maps </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> into </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. This follows from elliptic regularity applied to the resolvent and the semigroup representation
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tL} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span> e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-t(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m + c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2)} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Decay of eigenvalues implies that high-frequency components are exponentially suppressed. As observed in studies of cortical eigenmodes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019,cabral2023}, this spectral filtering produces low-dimensional structure at macroscopic scales.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{B.3. Lipschitz Estimates for the Nonlinear Term}

The nonlinear operator decomposes as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = A(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) + T(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) + P(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
corresponding to amplitude, twist, and projection transformations. To apply fixed-point theorems, each term must satisfy local Lipschitz continuity in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.

The amplitude term, typically of the form </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, is Lipschitz on bounded sets provided </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is differentiable with bounded derivative. Twist operators involving orthogonal transformations preserve norms, and their dependence on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> yields a Lipschitz bound whenever the mapping </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mapsto</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is smooth. Projection operators onto fixed or </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>-dependent subspaces are Lipschitz under uniform bound assumptions.

Combined, these conditions imply the existence of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>R &gt; 0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> such that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1)-N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>R </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
whenever </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{B.4. Picard Iteration and Local Existence}

Define the map
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)(t) = e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tL}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>t e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(t-s)L} N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>(s))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> ds.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
For small </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>T&gt;0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, the Banach space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>C([0,T];H)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> with the supremum norm becomes a complete metric space. The Lipschitz bound ensures that </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a contraction on a sufficiently small ball around </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{tL}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Picard iteration yields a unique mild solution </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> C([0,T];H)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{B.5. Global Existence}

Global existence follows from a priori estimates. Taking the inner product of the evolution equation with </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> yields
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{1}{2}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{d}{dt}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>),</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Dissipativity provides a negative contribution from the linear term, while polynomial growth bounds on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>N</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> guarantee at most linear growth in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Grönwall's inequality implies uniform bounds for all finite times, allowing extension of the solution globally.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{B.6. Uniqueness and Continuous Dependence}

Uniqueness follows from a contraction argument applied to the difference of two solutions. Continuous dependence on the initial data is inherited from the stability of the semigroup and the Lipschitz continuity of the nonlinear term.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>*{B.7. Regularity of Solutions}

If </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> for </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>k </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\geq</span></span><span> 1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, then smoothing by the semigroup and differentiability of the nonlinear term imply that the solution remains in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> for all time. This regularity supports the use of eigenfunction expansions and ensures compatibility with the geometric interpretations given in the main text.

The results established in this appendix provide a complete proof of well-posedness for the cortical field equation and justify the mathematical assumptions underlying the spectral and approximation analyses presented in later sections.
