6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Appendix C. Universal Approximation via Amplistwistor Cascades}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\addcontentsline</span></span><span>{toc}{section}{Appendix C. Universal Approximation via Amplistwistor Cascades}

This appendix provides a rigorous foundation for the universal approximation results invoked in the main text. The nonlinear architecture described therein depends fundamentally on the expressive capacity of local transformation operators, which combine amplitude modulation, rotational or shearing twists, and projection maps. These operators, collectively termed amplistwistors, form a structured class of nonlinear mappings acting on Banach-valued function spaces. The purpose of this appendix is to establish that finite compositions of such maps are dense in the space of continuous functions defined on compact subsets of appropriate Banach spaces, thereby ensuring that the nonlinear component of the field equation is capable of representing arbitrarily complex transformations.

The argument begins with a compact subset </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>K </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subset</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> representing the admissible range of field configurations at a given spatial point. Consider the space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>C(K;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> equipped with the uniform norm. A classical result by Cybenko </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989} establishes the density of finite superpositions of sigmoidal functions in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>C(K)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Hornik's generalization </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{hornik1991} extends this principle to multilayer feedforward networks, demonstrating that universal approximation is a consequence not of specific nonlinearities but of mild regularity and non-polynomiality conditions. Poggio and Girosi further developed connections between approximation theory and regularization perspectives, unifying functional and geometric viewpoints </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{poggiogi}. The present setting requires a vector-valued analogue of these theorems, adapted to operators arising from geometric actions rather than scalar activation functions.

Let an amplistwistor be defined as a map of the form
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span>: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>+</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is an amplitude function, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span>: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> SO(d)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a smooth rotation field, and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span>: </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a smooth projection onto a lower-dimensional or otherwise constrained subspace. Smoothness assumptions ensure local Lipschitz continuity, while non-degeneracy of the twist operator guarantees a rich set of local deformations.

The algebra generated by finite compositions of such maps,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
is shown to be dense in </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>C(K;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> by verifying two classical conditions of the Stone--Weierstrass theorem. First, the algebra separates points. For any distinct </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>x,y </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> K</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, one may choose an amplistwistor whose amplitude factor produces distinct scalar values at </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>y</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, or whose twist component induces distinct orientations, or whose projection emphasizes a direction in which the two points differ. Smoothness ensures that this construction can be achieved perturbatively if necessary. Second, the algebra contains the constant functions, since a degenerate amplistwistor with trivial twist and projection reduces to multiplication by a constant amplitude.

Having satisfied the hypotheses of the Stone--Weierstrass theorem, it follows that the closure of the amplistwistor algebra is the full space of continuous vector-valued functions. The densities of such nonlinear operators imply that any continuous mapping from </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> to </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> can be approximated arbitrarily well in the uniform norm by finite compositions of amplistwistors. Although universal approximation theorems typically rely on scalar activation functions, the geometric structure of the present operators renders them even more expressive, since they simultaneously apply multiplicative, rotational, and contractive transformations.

Beyond existence, one may inquire into the number of compositions required for a given level of approximation. The classical bounds of Saxe, McClelland, and Ganguli </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{saxemc} for deep linear networks suggest that depth can provide considerable expressive efficiency. Although comparable bounds have not been fully derived for the nonlinear geometric operators considered here, arguments based on Lipschitz constants and curvature of the representational manifold imply that the number of compositions grows at most polynomially in the inverse of the approximation tolerance.

The implications for the cortical field model are substantial. Because amplistwistor cascades can approximate any continuous local operator, the nonlinear component of the field equation can in principle encode arbitrary local interactions modulated by the geometry of the manifold. This establishes a fundamental expressive equivalence between the proposed cortical operator and the class of deep neural architectures studied in machine learning, although the geometric interpretation differs markedly. The universal approximation property ensures that the nonlinear term can represent arbitrarily subtle deformations of the field, thereby justifying its role as the primary mechanism of local computation within the broader dynamical system.
