</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\addcontentsline</span></span><span>{toc}{section}{Appendix D. Spectral Geometry and Eigenmode Analysis}

The analysis of large-scale cortical dynamics relies heavily on the spectral properties of the Laplace--Beltrami operator defined on the cortical manifold. This appendix develops the necessary elements of spectral geometry, beginning with foundational results concerning the structure of eigenfunctions and proceeding to asymptotic properties, completeness, regularity, and orthogonality. These principles underlie the modal decompositions used throughout the main text and allow for rigorous interpretation of empirical findings from ultrafast fMRI and electrophysiology.

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>M</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> be a smooth, compact Riemannian manifold without boundary. The Laplace--Beltrami operator </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is self-adjoint and non-positive on </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Its spectrum consists solely of eigenvalues of finite multiplicity,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
0 = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0 &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span> , </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with corresponding eigenfunctions </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> forming an orthonormal basis of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. This decomposition is the analytic foundation of modal analyses in computational neuroscience </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} and in ultrafast fMRI investigations revealing intrinsic oscillatory modes </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}.

A central theorem establishing the completeness of this eigenbasis follows from the compactness of the resolvent </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span> I)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-1}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Compactness ensures that every function </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>f </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> can be expanded as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
f = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{m=0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> f, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
with convergence in the </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> norm. If </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>f</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> possesses additional smoothness, such as belonging to a Sobolev space </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k(M)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, the Fourier coefficients decay rapidly with respect to the eigenvalues, and the series converges in stronger norms. This property explains the empirical observation that only a small number of modes suffice to reconstruct large-scale patterns of brain activity recorded with fMRI or MEG, particularly those associated with resting-state networks or long-range oscillations.

The asymptotic behavior of the eigenvalues is described by Weyl's law, which in two dimensions asserts that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sim</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{4</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span> m}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{vol}(M)}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Although the cortical surface exhibits significant curvature and topological complexity, the leading-order term of this asymptotic relation remains valid, and deviations encode geometric irregularities. Numerical studies confirm that cortical eigenmodes display partition-like structures reminiscent of those seen in membranes with complex boundaries, and these structures align with empirically measured functional networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}.

Regularity theory implies that eigenfunctions are smooth, and elliptic estimates yield
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>k} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> C</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{k/2},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
for any non-negative integer </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>k</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. These estimates have practical significance when interpreting electrophysiological signals: higher-frequency eigenmodes are more spatially oscillatory, and their presence in measured data corresponds to fine-scale cortical dynamics. Studies of intrinsic modes in ultrafast fMRI reveal oscillatory structures consistent with low- to mid-frequency eigenmodes of the cortical manifold </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023}. The spectral gap between low-order modes also influences how quickly cortical fields relax toward coarse-scale patterns, a phenomenon relevant to theories of functional integration </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{breakspear2017,deco2009}.

The Courant--Fischer minimax principle provides a variational characterization of eigenvalues:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\min</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\substack</span></span><span>{V </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subset</span></span><span> H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1(M) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\dim</span></span><span> V = m+1}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\max</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> V </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\setminus</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span>0</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span>} 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M |</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\nabla</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span>|</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{d}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g}{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\int</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>M </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\varphi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{d}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mu</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>g}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This principle elucidates how geometric features of the cortex---such as gyrification patterns or variations in cortical thickness---influence the spectral distribution. The eigenfunctions minimize energy under orthogonality constraints, yielding modes that naturally align with the large-scale organization of functional networks. Honey, Thivierge, and Sporns </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{honey2010} demonstrated empirically that structural connectivity constrains functional responses, while the present analysis suggests that intrinsic geometry alone provides a substantial contribution to observed large-scale patterns.

Orthogonality of eigenfunctions is both a mathematical necessity and an empirical signature. If two oscillatory patterns detected in fMRI or MEG correspond to distinct eigenmodes, their inner product vanishes up to measurement noise. This phenomenon has been reported in decompositions of spontaneous activity </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} and supports the idea that cortical dynamics may be understood as superpositions of standing waves shaped by the geometry of the manifold rather than solely by synaptic interactions.

The results in this appendix justify the spectral decompositions employed in the main text and establish a rigorous connection between geometric structure and large-scale electrophysiological and hemodynamic signals. They further reinforce the applicability of eigenmode-based analyses in interpreting cortical dynamics at both fine and coarse scales.
