</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\addcontentsline</span></span><span>{toc}{section}{Appendix E. Neurophysiological Data Analysis Methods}

The empirical interpretations advanced in the main text draw upon established methodologies in electrocorticography, magnetoencephalography, and functional magnetic resonance imaging. This appendix provides a detailed exposition of the analytical procedures that connect the mathematical framework to empirical datasets, with particular emphasis on the decoding and encoding models, spectral decompositions, and statistical validation techniques that underlie contemporary systems neuroscience.

Neural recordings with sufficient temporal resolution to resolve the dynamical signatures predicted by the model are typically obtained using intracranial electrophysiological methods. Electrocorticography affords millisecond-level precision and is therefore well suited to studies of temporal hierarchies in language comprehension and sensory integration. Data are commonly preprocessed by applying high-pass filters to remove slow drifts, line noise removal via notch filtering, and referencing procedures such as common-average or bipolar referencing. Time–frequency decompositions such as wavelet transforms or short-time Fourier methods yield measures of oscillatory power and phase across canonical bands. Studies of hierarchical language processing, including the recent work demonstrating temporal alignment between transformer layers and cortical responses </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025}, rely on such decompositions to quantify the onset and latency of neural responses to linguistic input.

The spatial resolution required for large-scale eigenmode analysis arises from functional MRI, particularly ultrafast variants capable of sampling at tens of frames per second. The methodological advances of Cabral, Fernandes, and Shemesh </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} demonstrate that ultrafast fMRI reveals intrinsic oscillatory modes consistent with the standing-wave structure predicted by spectral geometry. Preprocessing pipelines generally include motion correction, spatial smoothing, temporal filtering, and normalization procedures that preserve spectral content while suppressing measurement noise. The resulting time series permit voxelwise or regionwise decomposition into eigenmodes derived from the cortical geometry, allowing direct comparison with theoretical predictions concerning modal energy distributions and their modulation under experimental conditions.

Magnetoencephalography provides an intermediate modality, capturing large-scale oscillatory coherence at high temporal resolution. Beamforming and minimum-norm estimation techniques convert sensor-level recordings into estimates of cortical activity, albeit with inherent spatial uncertainty. Nevertheless, MEG remains a powerful tool for assessing phase coherence, cross-frequency coupling, and transient synchrony between distant cortical areas. Earlier work on neuronal synchrony </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999} demonstrated that informational relations between neural assemblies may be reflected in precise phase relationships. The present framework extends this principle by predicting that phase relationships should reflect transitions between eigenmodes of the underlying field, particularly in the presence of nonlinear local deformations.

The statistical relationship between neural signals and computational models is often established using encoding models. A typical setup constructs a linear or nonlinear mapping from features extracted from a stimulus, such as word embeddings or model-derived hidden states, to observed neural responses. Ridge regression, canonical correlation analysis, or generalized linear models quantify the degree to which particular computational features predict neural activity. The work of Goldstein et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025} exemplifies this approach, demonstrating positive correlations between the depths of transformer layers and the latencies at which they best predict intracranial responses. When combined with temporal receptive field analyses, such models permit reconstruction of the evolving representation from neural data, thereby enabling direct evaluation of the temporal dilation predicted by the semigroup formulation of cortical fields.

Conversely, decoding models attempt to predict features of the stimulus or internal task variables from neural recordings. The success of such models is often cited as evidence for representational equivalence between computational architectures and cortical dynamics. Machine learning methods such as support vector machines, recurrent neural networks, and convolutional networks supplement classical statistical approaches and are now standard tools in the analysis of MEG and fMRI data.

Permutation testing, cross-validation, and bootstrap methods provide safeguards against overfitting and ensure statistical robustness. Encoding and decoding correlations are typically compared against null distributions generated by temporal shuffling or phase randomization, which preserve low-level structure while destroying meaningful signal–feature correspondences. The statistical rigor of these procedures is crucial when interpreting subtle associations between spectral patterns and computational models.

The methodologies summarized here underpin the empirical validations discussed in the main text and establish the feasibility of testing the model’s predictions using existing neurophysiological techniques. They also provide a template for future experimental paradigms designed to probe the geometric and dynamical features central to the theoretical framework.
