7 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Appendix F. Computational Methods and Numerical Discretization}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\addcontentsline</span></span><span>{toc}{section}{Appendix F. Computational Methods and Numerical Discretization}

The field equations developed in the main text possess a rich geometric structure that complicates numerical approximation. Their analysis requires careful discretization of both the cortical manifold and the nonlinear operators acting upon it. This appendix provides a detailed discussion of the numerical schemes suitable for simulating the evolution equation and computing the associated spectral decompositions, with attention to stability, convergence, and computational efficiency.

The discretization of the cortical manifold is commonly performed using triangulated surface meshes derived from anatomical MRI scans. Methods such as those employed in the FreeSurfer pipeline or in spectral mesh processing algorithms replace the continuous Laplace--Beltrami operator with its discrete counterpart, obtained via the cotangent-weight Laplacian. For a triangulated surface with vertex set </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>V</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, edge set </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>E</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, and area elements </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>, the discrete Laplacian acts on a function </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>f : V </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>d</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> according to
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h f)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{1}{A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{j </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(i)} w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ij} (f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>j - f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>i),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{N}(i)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denotes the neighbors of vertex </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>i</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and the cotangent weights </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>w</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{ij}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> approximate the geometric contributions of adjacent triangles. Convergence of this operator to the continuous Laplace--Beltrami operator has been established under standard mesh regularity assumptions.

Time discretization of the evolution equation
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\partial</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> = L</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span> + N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
requires balancing the stiffness introduced by the Laplacian with the potential nonlinearity of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>N</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Implicit–explicit (IMEX) schemes offer an efficient compromise, treating the linear term implicitly and the nonlinear term explicitly. A first-order IMEX scheme takes the form
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
(I - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> L)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{n+1} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{n} + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span> N(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{n}),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denotes the field at time step </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. Solving the implicit system typically reduces to inverting a sparse linear operator, rendering the procedure computationally tractable even for large meshes.

Higher-order schemes, including semi-implicit Runge--Kutta methods, may be employed to enhance accuracy. Stability analyses indicate that the implicit treatment of the Laplacian renders the scheme unconditionally stable with respect to the linear term, while explicit bounds on the Lipschitz constant of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>N</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> guarantee stability for moderate time steps. These considerations mirror classical analyses in numerical PDE literature and parallel the requirements for simulating large-scale neuronal field models.

Eigenmode computations, essential for the spectral decompositions appearing throughout the main text, rely on numerical eigensolvers capable of handling large sparse matrices. Algorithms such as the Lanczos method or implicitly restarted Arnoldi iterations compute low-frequency eigenmodes efficiently. Empirical work demonstrating the correspondence between spectral modes and functional networks in the brain </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019} verifies that only the first several hundred eigenmodes are typically required for meaningful reconstruction, permitting simulations at biologically realistic scales.

Nonlinear operations derived from the amplitude, twist, and projection components of the amplistwistor require special attention. Amplitude modulation is implemented pointwise, projection operators may require local principal component analyses or precomputed subspaces, and twist operations involving elements of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>SO(d)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> demand numerically stable parameterizations such as Rodrigues' rotation formula or quaternion-based methods. These operators must remain differentiable to preserve the analytic properties of the continuous model.

Finally, validation of the numerical scheme requires demonstration of convergence to the continuous solution. Standard arguments apply: consistency ensures that the discretized operators approximate the continuous ones, stability bounds limit error amplification, and convergence follows by the Lax equivalence theorem extended to manifolds with smooth boundaryless geometry. Convergence may further be evaluated empirically by refining the mesh resolution and time step and observing the stabilization of macroscopic spectral measures.

The computational framework developed here provides a practical means to explore the qualitative and quantitative behavior of the cortical field model, enabling numerical experiments, parameter sweeps, and direct comparison with empirical neural data.
