6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-tex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>*{Appendix G. Extended Discussion, Limitations, and Open Problems}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\addcontentsline</span></span><span>{toc}{section}{Appendix G. Extended Discussion, Limitations, and Open Problems}

Although the theoretical framework developed in this manuscript combines geometric, dynamical, and spectral approaches in a unified manner, its scope and assumptions impose a number of conceptual and empirical limitations. This appendix offers a reflective assessment of these constraints and outlines significant open problems that must be addressed before the model can be regarded as a mature account of cortical computation. The intention is not merely to enumerate shortcomings but to situate the theory within the broader methodological landscape of contemporary neuroscience, applied mathematics, and machine learning, thereby clarifying where the present approach diverges from established frameworks and where it may ultimately converge with them.

One of the most substantial limitations concerns the idealization of the cortex as a smooth Riemannian manifold on which differential operators act continuously. While this representation is mathematically powerful and aligns with spectral analyses of large-scale brain dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{roberts2019}, it abstracts away from the inherently discrete, non-uniform microcircuitry of biological neural tissue. The Laplace--Beltrami operator captures geometric diffusion across the folded cortical surface, but it does not explicitly encode synaptic heterogeneity, laminar structure, or the anisotropic propagation of signals along long-range white-matter tracts. Earlier models of resting-state dynamics </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{deco2009,breakspear2017} attempted to bridge this gap by incorporating structural connectivity explicitly; the present work, in contrast, emphasizes geometry as the fundamental determinant of large-scale modes. Whether such geometric primacy holds empirically remains an open question, particularly when considering interindividual variability in cortical folding or developmental changes in tissue structure.

A related limitation involves the assumption that the nonlinear local operators—the amplistwistors—can be modeled independently of microscopic synaptic processes. The formal results establishing universal approximation borrow heavily from functional analysis and the theory of neural networks </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cybenko1989,hornik1991,saxemc}, yet these results provide no guarantee that biological microcircuits implement the specific families of nonlinear transformations assumed here. In particular, rotation-like operators arising from the twist component of the amplistwistor have no direct analogue in known dendritic or synaptic mechanisms. While neuronal synchrony </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{singer1999} and spectral integration may offer partial biological motivation, the gap between mathematical construct and physiological implementation remains considerable.

Another challenge lies in the temporal structure predicted by the model. The semigroup formulation implies a smoothing effect that distributes information over wider temporal windows in higher-order cortical areas. Although the hierarchical temporal dilation observed in intracranial recordings during language comprehension </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{goldstein2025} supports the broad outline of this prediction, the exact relationship between nonlinear composition depth, temporal latency, and cognitive processing remains theoretically underdeveloped. Existing hierarchical models of cortical inference </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{friston</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>hierarchy} similarly posit multi-timescale dynamics, yet the present theory diverges in attributing these scales primarily to geometric diffusion rather than to feedback loops or recurrent Bayesian updating. Further empirical work will be required to disentangle these mechanisms.

The spectral analysis also gives rise to foundational questions about the interpretability of eigenmodes in the context of neurobiological function. Ultrafast fMRI findings </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{cabral2023} demonstrate that standing-wave modes can be experimentally observed, yet their cognitive relevance remains uncertain. While spectral modes offer an elegant mathematical basis for decomposing cortical dynamics, it is unclear whether cognitive operations actively recruit or modulate specific eigenmodes, or whether these modes simply provide a passive background structure upon which neural activity unfolds. Functional interpretations of eigenmodes must therefore be approached cautiously, even as mathematical and empirical work continues to reveal their ubiquity.

From a methodological standpoint, numerical approximations introduce additional limitations. Discretizations of the Laplace--Beltrami operator rely on mesh quality and resolution, and the stability of IMEX schemes is sensitive to the Lipschitz bounds of nonlinear operators. These numerical issues may distort the dynamics in ways that do not correspond to biological processes, especially when simulating large networks or attempting to recover empirical spectral distributions. Computational studies of deep neural models </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{saxemc} reveal similar sensitivities to architectural choices, suggesting a parallel between numerical fragility and representational biases.

Several open problems arise naturally from these considerations. A first concerns the derivation of biologically plausible microcircuit mechanisms that instantiate amplistwistor-like transformations. The mathematics demonstrates expressive sufficiency but remains silent on biological feasibility. A second open problem involves the integration of long-range structural connectivity with intrinsic geometric modes, potentially requiring hybrid models that combine spectral geometry with network-theoretic approaches </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cite</span></span><span>{honey2010}. A third concerns the recovery of field variables from empirical data; the inverse problem associated with reconstructing continuous cortical fields from sparse or noisy measurements remains ill-posed and demands sophisticated regularization techniques. Finally, the interplay between nonlinear dynamics, eigenmode decomposition, and cognitive function presents a broad area for empirical exploration, particularly in relation to cross-frequency coupling phenomena and the emergence of representational hierarchies.

The limitations and open questions presented here do not diminish the theoretical contribution of the framework. Rather, they underscore the extent to which a rigorous mathematical formulation must be complemented by extensive empirical, computational, and mechanistic work. The challenges identified in this appendix delineate a clear agenda for future research, one that will require interdisciplinary collaboration across neuroscience, mathematics, physics, and artificial intelligence. Only through such integration can the strengths of the present theory be fully realized and its shortcomings appropriately addressed.
