This repository contains the full research monograph, simulation code, notebooks,
and reproducible experiments for the project </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**Amplistwistor Cascades**</span></span><span> —
a mathematical and neurocomputational framework in which <span class="hljs-strong">**cortical columns are
modeled as cascades of geometric amplitwistor transformations**</span> acting on
relativistic scalar–vector fields.  

The project integrates tools from differential geometry, spectral theory,
functional analysis, and large-scale brain modeling to provide a unified account
of language, prediction, and temporal hierarchy in cortex.

The LaTeX manuscript is written in </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**academic style**</span></span><span>, uses </span><span><span class="hljs-strong">**LuaLaTeX**</span></span><span>, and
comes with a full build system and notebooks for reproducing figures and PDE
simulations.

---

</span><span><span class="hljs-section">## Overview</span></span><span>

Amplistwistors generalize the complex-analytic </span><span><span class="hljs-emphasis">*amplitwist*</span></span><span> (Needham, 1997) into
a nonlinear geometric operator acting on high-dimensional fields. A cascade of
such operators models the local dynamics of a cortical column, providing a
continuous-field analogue of deep network layer transformations.  

Combined with RSVP‐style scalar–vector–entropy fields and cortical eigenmode
resonances, amplitwistor cascades yield:

</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> biologically grounded mechanisms for temporal hierarchy formation,  
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> universal approximation properties similar to Hornik–Cybenko networks,  
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> PDE formulations consistent with large-scale neural field models  
  (Breakspear 2017; Deco et al. 2009),  
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> empirical alignment with ECoG studies showing LLM layer–time mapping  
  (Goldstein et al., </span><span><span class="hljs-emphasis">*Nature Communications*</span></span><span>, 2025), and  
</span><span><span class="hljs-bullet">-</span></span><span> compatibility with ultrafast fMRI evidence for standing-wave cortical modes  
  (Cabral, Fernandes &amp; Shemesh, 2023).

This repository provides all code and documentation needed to reproduce the
results.

---

