xt-primary h-3 w-3 rounded-full"></div></div><div class="relative my-1 min-h-6"><div class="origin-top-start relative flex flex-col gap-2 overflow-x-clip" style="opacity: 1; transform: none;"><div class="relative w-full text-start"><div class="flex w-full flex-row items-start justify-between gap-4 text-start"><button class="flex min-w-0 shrink-1 items-center gap-0.5"><span class="font-medium w-full"><span class="flex items-center gap-1 truncate text-start align-middle text-token-text-secondary hover:text-token-text-primary dark:hover:text-token-text-primary dark:text-[var(--interactive-label-tertiary-default)]" style="opacity: 1;"><div class="truncate">Thought for 25s</div></span></span></button></div></div><div class="max-w-[calc(0.8*var(--thread-content-max-width,40rem))]"></div></div></div><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="a4db11a9-5a5e-4563-9a5a-a9d552265ea5" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-1-thinking"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="224">Nice, A1 is the cleanest abstract setting. Since Stone–Weierstrass only really needs “compact Hausdorff,” we do not need to lean hard on the smooth structure; we just keep it around for interpretation as a cortical manifold.</p>
<p data-start="226" data-end="273">Below is a <strong data-start="237" data-end="267">manifold-level Lean module</strong> that:</p>
<ul data-start="275" data-end="724">
<li data-start="275" data-end="347">
<p data-start="277" data-end="347">assumes a general compact Hausdorff space <code data-start="319" data-end="322">M</code> with manifold structure,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="348" data-end="400">
<p data-start="350" data-end="400">assumes a finite-dimensional real field space <code data-start="396" data-end="399">F</code>,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="401" data-end="482">
<p data-start="403" data-end="482">uses your amplitwistor machinery (<code data-start="437" data-end="450">Definitions</code>, <code data-start="452" data-end="461">Algebra</code>, <code data-start="463" data-end="480">UniversalApprox</code>),</p>
</li>
<li data-start="483" data-end="605">
<p data-start="485" data-end="605">packages <strong data-start="494" data-end="526">manifold-flavored hypotheses</strong> (bump fields, partitions of unity) into an abstract <code data-start="579" data-end="594">ManifoldSetup</code> structure,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="606" data-end="724">
<p data-start="608" data-end="724">and then derives <strong data-start="625" data-end="652">universal approximation</strong> as a theorem using the already-defined <code data-start="692" data-end="717">universal_approximation</code> lemma.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="726" data-end="999">I keep this file free of “hard geometry proofs” by pushing the “richness” of your base fields and functionals into explicit hypotheses. That is exactly what you’d want for a first pass: it’s a precise interface between the manifold story and the amplitwistor algebra story.</p>
<p data-start="1001" data-end="1022">You can save this as:</p>
<p data-start="1024" data-end="1053"><code data-start="1024" data-end="1053">Amplistwistor/Manifold.lean</code></p>
