</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\title</span></span><span>{Foundations of the Amplistwistor Program:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
Conceptual, Logical, and Formal Descriptions}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\author</span></span><span>{Flyxion}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\date</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\today</span></span><span>}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{document}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\maketitle</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{abstract}
This essay describes the goals, motivations, and formalization strategy underlying the Amplistwistor Program. Our objective is to explain, in accessible language and with increasing levels of formal rigor, what amplitwistors are meant to model, why they matter for a mathematical description of cortical computation, and how we intend to translate these concepts into first-order logic, Lean, and finally into SpherePOP, a domain-specific calculus for semantic computation.

The document proceeds in four layers: a common-language conceptual overview, a first-order logical reconstruction, a Lean specification intended for mechanized verification, and a SpherePOP implementation sketch. Each layer expresses the same underlying ideas but with progressively stricter constraints and clearer commitments.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{abstract}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Introduction: What We Are Trying to Achieve}

The work described here aims to build a rigorous and fully formal model of a central idea: that cortical computation can be described as a combination of local nonlinear transformations (amplistwistors) and global geometric or spectral constraints (eigenmodes, resonances, and field interactions). 

While previous essays have developed the mathematics of amplitwistors, semigroup methods, and eigenmode structures, we now seek to build an </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{integrated formal program}. The goal is to translate intuitive neurocomputational ideas into:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{enumerate}[label=(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alph</span></span><span>*)]
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a clear conceptual narrative understandable without mathematical training,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a first-order logical theory describing the essential commitments,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> a Lean formalization that can serve as a basis for proof verification and mechanized reasoning,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> and a SpherePOP model that expresses these same concepts in an operational semantic calculus.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{enumerate}

Our overarching aim is to create a pathway from intuition to formal verification to executable semantics, ensuring that each conceptual claim can be expressed, checked, and instantiated at multiple levels of rigor.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{A Common-Language Explanation of the Amplistwistor Framework}

The central intuition is simple to state. When the brain processes information, each small region of cortex---often called a cortical column---performs a transformation on the incoming neural activity. This transformation is not merely a linear amplification or a rigid rotation; rather, it is a mixture of scaling, twisting, warping, and projecting information into more useful forms. We refer to these transformations as </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplistwistors} because they generalize Needham's idea of the ``amplitwist,'' combining amplitude and twist operations into a higher-dimensional nonlinear map.

Each local step is simple, but the power comes from </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{composition}. By applying many such transformations in sequence, the brain can build extremely complex functions. This is analogous to how deep neural networks work: many small nonlinear operations combine to yield a powerful representation.

However, the brain also exhibits global structure. Resonant modes, long-range coupling, and standing-wave phenomena observed in fMRI and MEG studies indicate that local operations are synchronized, guided, or constrained by global patterns. In our framework, these global patterns act as geometric boundary conditions on the local amplitwistor transformations.

Thus the system works at two scales simultaneously:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Local:} Each column applies an amplitwistor to its local field.
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Global:} The entire cortical surface shapes and is shaped by eigenmodes, resonances, and long-range coherence.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Our aim in this essay is not to justify the biological plausibility of this model (though such justification exists in the literature), but rather to explain how to formalize it so it can be analyzed rigorously.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{First-Order Logical Description of the Framework}

We now restate the core commitments using first-order logic. This is not a full axiomatization, but a skeleton from which the Lean development will grow.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Basic Ontology}

We assume three fundamental sorts:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Column}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Field}, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Transformation}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Intuitively:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> elements of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Column}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> represent cortical columns,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> elements of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Field}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> represent states of neural activity,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> elements of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Transformation}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> represent amplitwistors.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Primitive Relations and Functions}

We introduce the following functions:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state} : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Column} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Field}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{apply} : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Transformation} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Field} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Field}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{compose} : </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Transformation} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\times</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Transformation} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Transformation}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

and a global constraint relation:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Resonant}(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\quad</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{meaning that columns } c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ are constrained by a shared eigenmode.}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Axioms}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Axiom 1 (Local Update)}  
Every column evolves by applying a transformation to its state:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Column},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Transformation},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c)' = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{apply}(T, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c)).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Axiom 2 (Compositionality)}  
Transformations are closed under composition:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3 = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{compose}(T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Axiom 3 (Local Nonlinearity)}  
There exists at least one transformation that is not affine:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neg</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is affine}).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\paragraph</span></span><span>{Axiom 4 (Global Coherence)}  
If two columns are resonantly coupled, their updates interact:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Resonant}(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1, c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2) 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1)' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ depends on } </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This captures the empirical fact that resonant modes induce long-range correlations.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Interpretation}

These axioms collectively define a dynamical system: local nonlinear updates constrained by global relations. The system is intentionally minimal; the Lean formalization will enrich types, introduce algebraic structure, and eventually support proofs of expressivity and well-posedness.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Lean Formalization Roadmap}

In the next section (requested separately), we will begin translating these FOL commitments into Lean, including:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> defining types for columns, fields, amplitwistors,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> implementing the composition algebra,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> proving that amplitwistors form a subalgebra of transformations on functions,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> eventually connecting to spectral graph theory for global constraints.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{SpherePOP Implementation Roadmap}

The final layer will express amplitwistor cascades as operations in SpherePOP, using its native metaphors of “bubbles,” “pops,” and “flow constructs” to encode transformation, projection, and resonance.

This will provide an executable, human-readable version of the theory that retains the intuitive structure of amplitwistor dynamics while remaining grounded in rigorous formalism.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{document}
