7 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-latex"><span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Extended First-Order Logical Framework}

The initial logical skeleton introduced above provides an abstract description of how columns, fields, and transformations relate. We now enrich this structure by introducing a series of deeper axioms that capture essential mathematical and neurocomputational constraints. These axioms are expressed in first-order logic, but motivated by the analytic theory of partial differential equations, dynamical systems, and empirical results from neuroscience.

The goal is not to force a particular analytical formalism into first-order logic---which would be impossible in full generality---but rather to isolate the conceptual invariants that any model of amplitwistor dynamics must respect. These invariants will be reinterpreted in the Lean formalization and ultimately instantiated in SpherePOP.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Axiom Schemes for Causality}

We postulate that field evolution at a column is determined only by information available within a finite causal neighborhood. In first-order form, this takes the shape of a bounded dependency schema.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Column},<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> R </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Neighborhood},<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Column},<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bigl</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neg</span></span><span>(c' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> R(c)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c)' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ does not depend on } </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c')</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\bigr</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This expresses finite propagation speed: distant regions cannot influence the next state of a column directly. The notion of ``does not depend on'' can be formalized via a primitive independence predicate </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Indep} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Indep}(x,y) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ meaning ``the update of } x </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ does not depend on } y.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Then causality becomes:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c'</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\neg</span></span><span>(c'</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> R(c)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Indep}(c,c').
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This axiom is inspired by locality in relativistic field theories and by empirical findings on cortical connectivity, which show sharply decaying influence outside a column’s receptive field.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Locality of Transformation Structure}

We also require that amplitwistor transformations respect locality:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Transformation},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Column},<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>T(c),</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c' </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\notin</span></span><span> R</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>T(c),</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{apply}(T, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c)) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ is independent of } </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c').
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This ensures that transforming the field at one location depends only on the relevant local data, consistent with biological constraints of laminar and columnar circuitry, and also consistent with the structure of convolutional, attention-based, and recurrent operators in artificial neural networks.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Axiom Schemes for Well-Posedness}

To prevent pathological update rules, we impose a well-posedness condition. In first-order form we cannot speak of Lipschitz continuity directly, but we can introduce a predicate encoding the concept:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Lipschitz}(T) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ meaning ``}T</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ respects bounded variation of fields.''}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Then we postulate:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> T </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Transformation},<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Lipschitz}(T).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This expresses stability of updates and excludes degenerate transformations that would lead to ill-defined dynamics. In practice, this supports the existence and uniqueness of solutions to the induced discrete-time dynamical system.

We also include a forward-evolution axiom ensuring that iterated transformations do not diverge arbitrarily in one step:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> K </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbb</span></span><span>{R},<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{apply}(T, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leq</span></span><span> K(1 + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

First-order logic cannot quantify over real numbers directly unless taken as an abstract ordered field; here </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is understood as an element of such a field.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Axioms for Spectral and Global Coherence}

We now refine the earlier notion of resonant relations by asserting that resonance classes form equivalence classes under a coherence relation </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Coherent} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Coherent}(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\leftrightarrow</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Mode}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\land</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Mode}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Mode}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m(c) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> means that column </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> participates in eigenmode </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.

We impose:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2,c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3,<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Coherent}(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\land</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Coherent}(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2,c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Coherent}(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>3).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This expresses the transitivity of participation in a global mode. The biological motivation is derived from studies such as Cabral et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>(2023), Breakspear (2017), and Roberts et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>(2019), which demonstrate that global eigenmodes of cortical activity form structured patterns spanning the cortex.

To express that coherence affects updates, we use:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2,<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Coherent}(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rightarrow</span></span><span> 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{CoupledUpdate}(c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

where </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{CoupledUpdate} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is a primitive relation indicating that the new state of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> is influenced by the state of </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span>c</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Axioms for Stability and Regularity}

We assume the existence of stable trajectories:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> s </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{State},</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> T,<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{apply}(T,s) = s.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This asserts the existence of fixed points, analogous to attractor states in dynamical systems and to stable perceptual interpretations in cognition.

We also require:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span> &gt; 0,<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c)' - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

to exclude discontinuous jumps in field evolution.

In first-order form (without reals), we express this using predicates:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{SmallChange}(x,y) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ meaning ``}y</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\text</span></span><span>{ is an infinitesimal update of }x.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Then:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> c,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{SmallChange}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c), </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{state}(c)').
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Axiom of Expressivity}

Finally, we translate the universal approximation principle---proved analytically and expressible in Lean---into a first-order schema.

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Span}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> denote the smallest transformation algebra generated by a set of amplitwistors </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{T} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subseteq</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Transformation} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>.

We postulate:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> f </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{FieldMap},<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span> &gt; 0,<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>,T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Transformation},<span class="hljs-keyword">\
</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{compose}(T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ldots</span></span><span>, T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1) - f </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> &lt; </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Because first-order logic cannot quantify over the real norm or over arbitrary function spaces directly, we use instead a predicate </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Approximates}(T,f,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span>) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\forall</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exists</span></span><span> T,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Approximates}(T,f,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\epsilon</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This encodes that the amplitwistor algebra is dense in the space of permissible field transformations. It is structurally analogous to the universal approximation theorems of Cybenko (1989), Hornik (1991), and Poggio–Girosi (1990), but adapted to the broader amplitwistor context.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Interpretation and Role in Lean}

These axioms prepare the ground for the Lean formalization. While Lean will handle:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> actual metric structures,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> norms and Lipschitz continuity,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> algebras of continuous functions,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> eigenmode decompositions,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

the first-order system isolates the conceptual bone structure of the theory. When imported into Lean, these axioms will receive precise instantiations using typeclasses, continuous linear maps, topological spaces, and semigroup structures.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
