The axioms developed above do not merely specify a logical skeleton for amplitwistor dynamics; they codify, in a transparent and mathematically neutral language, the essential properties that any faithful model of cortical computation must possess. The aim of this section is to elucidate the interpretive significance of these axioms and to situate them within the broader context of theoretical neuroscience, field theory, and computational models of cognition. By elaborating the conceptual motivations behind each axiomic family, we offer an intelligible bridge between the abstract logical formulation and the later analytic and mechanistic developments.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Causality and the Geometry of Cortical Processing}

Causality, in the present framework, does not refer to physical time in a strict relativistic sense, but rather to the structure of influence and propagation on the cortical sheet. The cortex is not a homogeneous medium: it possesses a laminar geometry, a complex web of columnar microcircuits, and long-range white-matter projections. Empirical findings from optical imaging, multi-electrode arrays, and ECoG recordings all indicate that activity spreads in a constrained and anisotropic fashion. Our causality axiom expresses the minimal requirement that any column's evolution must be determined by a bounded causal region, rather than by instantaneous nonlocal dependence.

Such an assumption echoes classical results in the theory of parabolic partial differential equations, where finite propagation speed or light-cone-type bounds emerge from ellipticity or hyperbolicity. In neural field theories, closely related constraints appear in the form of kernels with compact or exponentially decaying support. The axioms thus capture a principle that is both mathematically natural and biologically unavoidable.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Locality and the Structure of Amplistwistor Transformations}

The amplitwistor, as a nonlinear operator acting on the local field configuration, must incorporate the biological fact that neurons cannot process information arbitrarily far away. Even transformations that appear global in effect---such as contrast normalization or gain modulation---are, in concrete biological implementations, assembled from the aggregation of local interactions. The locality axiom encodes this constraint without committing to a specific mechanistic instantiation.

In the mathematical formalism, locality will later appear in the Lean development as a restriction on the support of test functions, the boundedness of operators, and the requirement that amplitwistors map compactly supported perturbations to compactly supported outputs. This will allow a smooth transition from logical axioms to analytic results and ultimately to numerical implementation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Well-Posedness and Dynamical Stability}

A dynamical system defined by recursive local transformations must exhibit stability if it is to represent cognition. Biological systems do not routinely explode into pathological or divergent regimes; instead, they operate within stable attractor basins, exhibiting bounded fluctuations. The well-posedness axioms thus do not impose an artificial mathematical constraint, but rather articulate one of the deep structural regularities of neural computation.

In continuous mathematics, one typically ensures stability via Lipschitz conditions, coercivity, or monotonicity arguments. In functional analysis, such results lead to the generation of contraction semigroups, which guarantee the existence and uniqueness of solutions. Although first-order logic is not expressive enough to encode such analytic concepts directly, our axioms approximate them through the predicates </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{Lipschitz} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\(</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathsf</span></span><span>{SmallChange} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\)</span></span><span>. These predicates will be internally represented in Lean using metric structures and bounded linear maps.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Spectral and Global Coherence}

One of the most remarkable discoveries in modern neuroimaging is the existence of stable, long-range eigenmodes of cortical activity. Ultrafast fMRI studies (Cabral et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>2023; Roberts et al.</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ </span></span><span>2019) reveal that, even in the absence of explicit stimuli, the cortex supports structured patterns of activation that behave like standing waves. These eigenmodes possess characteristic spatial shapes and temporal frequencies, and they form the backbone of resting-state networks.

The coherence axioms express this empirical fact by introducing a global equivalence relation among columns that participate in the same eigenmode. This relation induces coupling constraints that shape the local amplitwistor updates. In field-theoretic language, the eigenmodes act as a background geometry or a slowly varying potential in which the local transformations evolve. In Lean, these relations will later correspond to partitions of unity, orthonormal eigenbases, and modal decompositions of field configurations.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Expressivity and Universal Approximation}

The amplitwistor program aspires to a universal approximation theorem: arbitrary cortical functions can be approximated by compositions of local nonlinear transformations. This idea resonates strongly with classical results in the approximation theory of neural networks, particularly the theorems of Cybenko, Hornik, Poggio, and Girosi. However, amplitwistors differ from standard feedforward networks in two respects: they act on structured fields rather than on vectors, and they are embedded in a geometric substrate governed by spectral constraints.

The expressivity axiom articulates the requirement that amplitwistor compositions are sufficiently rich to approximate the full space of field transformations relevant to cortical computation. In Lean, this axiom will be instantiated by demonstrating the density of the amplitwistor algebra in the space of continuous functions on a compact manifold, using adaptations of the Stone--Weierstrass theorem and its generalizations to manifold-valued and vector-valued functions.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Philosophical and Computational Significance}

The logical axioms collectively describe a system that is neither purely local nor purely global, neither entirely linear nor unboundedly nonlinear. The interplay between locality, nonlinearity, spectral structure, and stability defines a computational architecture that is flexible yet principled. It is well-suited to modelling phenomena such as hierarchical linguistic processing, semantic integration across time, and the synchronization of distributed neural populations.

By encoding these commitments in first-order logic, we have created a blueprint from which a mechanized formal system may be constructed. Lean will provide the mathematical precision, while SpherePOP will provide the operational semantics. Together, they constitute a pipeline from conceptual intuition to executable and verifiable implementation.
