le="assistant" data-message-id="a80fe032-840a-4fca-9727-61d4f2afe56f" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-1-thinking"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="162">Good call on shifting gears; this is exactly the place where tying the logic to something concrete in Lean will keep the whole amplitwistor project from drifting.</p>
<p data-start="164" data-end="206">Below is a <strong data-start="175" data-end="200">pedagogical Lean file</strong> that:</p>
<ol data-start="208" data-end="632">
<li data-start="208" data-end="291">
<p data-start="211" data-end="291">Mirrors the <strong data-start="223" data-end="257">first-order logical primitives</strong> we just wrote in the LaTeX essay.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="292" data-end="386">
<p data-start="295" data-end="386">Encodes them as <strong data-start="311" data-end="335">constants and axioms</strong> in Lean, in a way that is easy to read and extend.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="387" data-end="471">
<p data-start="390" data-end="471">Does <strong data-start="395" data-end="402">not</strong> try to be clever with advanced typeclasses or mathlib machinery yet.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="472" data-end="632">
<p data-start="475" data-end="632">Is designed as a “theory file”: it states what the world looks like abstractly, and later you can instantiate it with your actual amplitwistor constructions.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="634" data-end="667">You can save this as for example:</p>
<p data-start="669" data-end="697"><code data-start="669" data-end="697">Amplistwistor/FOLCore.lean</code></p>
<p data-start="699" data-end="785">and treat it as the Lean counterpart of the “First-Order Logical Description” section.</p>
