 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre! language-lean"><span>/-
  Amplistwistor/FOLCore.lean

  A beginner-friendly Lean translation of the first-order logical framework
  described in the LaTeX essay.

  The goal of this file is not to develop full mathematics yet, but to
  faithfully encode the *primitive sorts, functions, and axioms* of the
  amplitwistor program in a way that is transparent and easy to extend.

  Later files can replace these abstract constants with concrete instances
  (e.g., manifolds, vector fields, amplitwistor operators) and prove that
  those instances satisfy the axioms stated here.
-/

universe u

namespace AmplistwistorFOL

/-
  We begin by declaring the three basic "sorts" from the first-order
  description:

    Column         -- cortical columns or local processing units
    Field          -- states of activity (could be vector-valued)
    Transformation -- amplitwistor-like operators acting on fields

  At this stage we keep them completely abstract.
-/

constant Column         : Type u
constant Field          : Type u
constant Transformation : Type u

/-
  Primitive functions:

    state      : Column → Field
      The current field state at a given column.

    nextState  : Column → Field
      The updated field state (after one global update step).

    apply      : Transformation → Field → Field
      Application of a transformation to a field state.

    compose    : Transformation → Transformation → Transformation
      Composition of two transformations, in the sense of successive
      application.
-/

constant state     : Column → Field
constant nextState : Column → Field
constant apply     : Transformation → Field → Field
constant compose   : Transformation → Transformation → Transformation

/-
  Global relations capturing resonance and coherence.

    Resonant    : Column → Column → Prop
      Two columns are co-participants in some resonant interaction.

    Coherent    : Column → Column → Prop
      A more general coherence / mode-sharing relation.

  We keep them abstract for now; later, they can be instantiated via
  eigenmode overlaps, graph connectivity, or spectral decompositions.
-/

constant Resonant : Column → Column → Prop
constant Coherent : Column → Column → Prop

/-
  Optional auxiliary predicates suggested by the FOL narrative:

    Indep        : Column → Column → Prop
      "The update of the first column does not depend on the second."

    SmallChange  : Field → Field → Prop
      "The second field is a small (infinitesimal) update of the first."

    Lipschitz    : Transformation → Prop
      "The transformation respects a bounded-variation / Lipschitz-like
       condition."

    Approximates : Transformation → (Field → Field) → Prop
      "The transformation approximates a given field-map in the relevant
       sense (e.g., uniformly on a compact domain)."

  These are deliberately left as abstract predicates; they will be
  interpreted concretely when we move to metric and topological
  structures in later files.
-/

constant Indep        : Column → Column → Prop
constant SmallChange  : Field → Field → Prop
constant Lipschitz    : Transformation → Prop
constant Approximates : Transformation → (Field → Field) → Prop

/-
  AXIOM 1: Local update

  Every column has its next state obtained by applying some transformation
  to its current state.

    ∀ c, ∃ T, nextState c = apply T (state c)
-/
axiom local_update :
  ∀ c : Column, ∃ T : Transformation, nextState c = apply T (state c)

/-
  AXIOM 2: Compositionality

  Transformations are closed under composition.

    ∀ T₁ T₂, ∃ T₃, T₃ = compose T₁ T₂

  In a more algebraic development we might define `compose` as a function
  and then prove associativity, identities, etc., but here we keep the
  axiom in the direct FOL style.
-/
axiom compositionality :
  ∀ T₁ T₂ : Transformation, ∃ T₃ : Transformation, T₃ = compose T₁ T₂

/-
  AXIOM 3: Local nonlinearity

  There exists at least one transformation that is not affine.

  We do not try to define "affine" rigorously here; instead, we represent
  it as a primitive predicate `Affine : Transformation → Prop` and state
  that not all transformations are affine.
-/

constant Affine : Transformation → Prop

axiom exists_nonlinear :
  ∃ T : Transformation, ¬ Affine T

/-
  AXIOM 4: Global coherence via resonance

  Resonance (and more generally coherence) induces coupled updates.

  We encode only the logical structure:

    ∀ c₁ c₂, Resonant c₁ c₂ → Coherent c₁ c₂

  and

    ∀ c₁ c₂, Coherent c₁ c₂ → ¬ Indep c₁ c₂

  i.e., if two columns are coherent, the update of one cannot be completely
  independent of the other.

  In later analytic work, these will be grounded in mode decompositions,
  eigenfunctions of Laplacians, or connectivity patterns.
-/
axiom resonant_impl_coherent :
  ∀ c₁ c₂ : Column, Resonant c₁ c₂ → Coherent c₁ c₂

axiom coherent_impl_coupling :
  ∀ c₁ c₂ : Column, Coherent c₁ c₂ → ¬ Indep c₁ c₂

/-
  AXIOM 5: Locality / finite influence

  There exists some notion of "neighborhood" of a column, and outside this
  neighborhood, other columns do not influence the update of the given one.

  We model a neighborhood abstractly as a predicate `Nbr : Column → Column → Prop`
  (read: `Nbr c c'` means "c' lies in the neighborhood of c").

    ∀ c, ∀ c', ¬ Nbr c c' → Indep c c'

  This captures the idea: outside the neighborhood of c, other columns are
  independent of c's update.
-/

constant Nbr : Column → Column → Prop

axiom locality :
  ∀ c c' : Column, ¬ Nbr c c' → Indep c c'

/-
  AXIOM 6: Stability / small updates

  Updates are small in an appropriate sense:

    ∀ c, SmallChange (state c) (nextState c)

  In later metric-based developments, `SmallChange` could mean that the
  distance between current and next state is bounded by some ε, but here it
  remains an abstract regularity condition.
-/
axiom small_update :
  ∀ c : Column, SmallChange (state c) (nextState c)

/-
  AXIOM 7: Lipschitz regularity

  All transformations are assumed to satisfy a Lipschitz-like regularity
  condition.

    ∀ T, Lipschitz T

  In a fully analytic setting, this would support well-posedness of the
  associated dynamical system. Here it is an abstract regularity axiom.
-/
axiom all_Lipschitz :
  ∀ T : Transformation, Lipschitz T

/-
  AXIOM 8: Expressivity / universal approximation (schema)

  For every "ideal" field-map f : Field → Field and every tolerance
  (encoded implicitly), there exists a transformation T that approximates f.

  In the first-order style, we write simply:

    ∀ f, ∃ T, Approximates T f

  Interpreting `Approximates` as "T is sufficiently close to f in the
  operational topology of interest."
-/
axiom universal_approx_schema :
  ∀ f : Field → Field, ∃ T : Transformation, Approximates T f

/-
  AXIOM 9: Coherence as an equivalence relation (optional)

  This reflects the idea that participation in a global mode partitions the
  columns into equivalence classes. We state reflexivity, symmetry, and
  transitivity for `Coherent`.

  These are not strictly necessary for the rest of the theory, but they
  align with the spectral interpretation and the LaTeX formulation.
-/
axiom coherent_refl :
  ∀ c : Column, Coherent c c

axiom coherent_symm :
  ∀ c₁ c₂ : Column, Coherent c₁ c₂ → Coherent c₂ c₁

axiom coherent_trans :
  ∀ c₁ c₂ c₃ : Column,
    Coherent c₁ c₂ → Coherent c₂ c₃ → Coherent c₁ c₃

/-
  At this stage, we have a clean Lean encoding of the first-order logical
  theory: we have declared the basic sorts, primitive operations, and the
  axioms as constants of type `Prop`. There are no proofs yet; this file
  is meant to serve as a *theory specification*.

  Later files can proceed in two complementary ways:

    1. "Model building": Provide concrete types for `Column`, `Field`,
       and `Transformation` (e.g., a finite set of vertices, vector fields
       on a manifold, or signals on a graph), define `state`, `nextState`,
       `apply`, etc., and then *prove* that all the axioms hold in that
       concrete model.

    2. "Theorem proving": Work abstractly from these axioms, proving
       derived properties of the amplitwistor system (e.g., constraints
       on possible dynamics or representation power) in the style of
       axiomatic field theory or modal logic.

  This dual perspective mirrors the LaTeX essay's two missions:
    to articulate conceptual commitments and to support concrete,
    mechanized implementations.
-/

end AmplistwistorFOL
</span></code></div></div></pre>
<hr data-start="9243" data-end="9246">
<p data-start="9248" data-end="9413">If this looks reasonable, the next move is to build a <strong data-start="9302" data-end="9322">second Lean file</strong> that starts to <em data-start="9338" data-end="9351">instantiate</em> this abstract theory with your actual amplitwistor machinery:</p>
<ul data-start="9415" data-end="9758">
<li data-start="9415" data-end="9512">
<p data-start="9417" data-end="9512"><code data-start="9417" data-end="9425">Column</code> becomes something like <code data-start="9449" data-end="9452">M</code> (a finite set, a manifold, or a graph of cortical patches),</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9513" data-end="9569">
<p data-start="9515" data-end="9569"><code data-start="9515" data-end="9522">Field</code> becomes something like <code data-start="9546" data-end="9553">M → F</code> or <code data-start="9557" data-end="9568">Fin d → ℝ</code>,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9570" data-end="9628">
<p data-start="9572" data-end="9628"><code data-start="9572" data-end="9588">Transformation</code> becomes amplitwistor-induced operators,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9629" data-end="9668">
<p data-start="9631" data-end="9668"><code data-start="9631" data-end="9638">apply</code> is just function application,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9669" data-end="9758">
<p data-start="9671" data-end="9758">and we prove that these concrete choices satisfy the axioms where they are supposed to.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="9760" data-end="9783">If you like, I can now:</p>
<ul data-start="9785" data-end="10001" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">
<li data-start="9785" data-end="9880">
<p data-start="9787" data-end="9880">write that <strong data-start="9798" data-end="9833">concrete-instantiation skeleton</strong> for your existing amplitwistor library,<br data-start="9873" data-end="9876">
or</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9881" data-end="10001" data-is-last-node="">
<p data-start="9883" data-end="10001" data-is-last-node="">go back to the LaTeX essay and add a section explaining “How to read this Lean file as a model of the logical theory.”</p>
</li>
