<h3 data-start="10622" data-end="10664">How this connects to your larger stack</h3>
<p data-start="10666" data-end="10689">At this point you have:</p>
<ul data-start="10691" data-end="11109">
<li data-start="10691" data-end="10807">
<p data-start="10693" data-end="10807">A SpherePop syntax (<code data-start="10713" data-end="10722">Process</code>) that can talk about pops, parallel composition, sequential composition, and merges.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="10808" data-end="10900">
<p data-start="10810" data-end="10900">A minimal operad interface (<code data-start="10838" data-end="10846">Operad</code>) and a SpherePop instance where nullary ops are pops.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="10901" data-end="10992">
<p data-start="10903" data-end="10992">An <code data-start="10906" data-end="10930">AmplistwistorAlgebra X</code> that says “SpherePop operations act on a field state type X”.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="10993" data-end="11109">
<p data-start="10995" data-end="11109">A function <code data-start="11006" data-end="11021">interpProcess</code> that turns a SpherePop process into an endomorphism on X, i.e. an amplitwistor cascade.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="11111" data-end="11177">To plug this into RSVP, you later choose <code data-start="11152" data-end="11155">X</code> to be something like:</p>
<ul data-start="11179" data-end="11287">
<li data-start="11179" data-end="11234">
<p data-start="11181" data-end="11234">a discrete RSVP field state (e.g. <code data-start="11215" data-end="11227">Grid → ℝ^d</code>), or</p>
</li>
<li data-start="11235" data-end="11287">
<p data-start="11237" data-end="11287">an abstract <code data-start="11249" data-end="11260">RSVPState</code> type you define elsewhere.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="11289" data-end="11359">Then you instantiate <code data-start="11310" data-end="11334">AmplistwistorAlgebra X</code> with a real action that:</p>
<ul data-start="11361" data-end="11523">
<li data-start="11361" data-end="11429">
<p data-start="11363" data-end="11429">uses the pop parameters (center, time, radius, amplitude, mode),</p>
</li>
<li data-start="11430" data-end="11473">
<p data-start="11432" data-end="11473">updates Φ, v, S along spherical fronts,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="11474" data-end="11523">
<p data-start="11476" data-end="11523">respects your PDE dynamics and lamphrodynamics.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="11525" data-end="11551">If you’d like, I can next:</p>
<ul data-start="11553" data-end="11860" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">
<li data-start="11553" data-end="11668">
<p data-start="11555" data-end="11668">write a LaTeX section that explains this Lean scaffold as the “operadic semantics of amplitwistor cascades,” or</p>
</li>
<li data-start="11669" data-end="11860" data-is-last-node="">
<p data-start="11671" data-end="11860" data-is-last-node="">sketch how <code data-start="11682" data-end="11685">X</code> could be a very simple discretized RSVP state (e.g. scalar field on a small grid) and give a toy <code data-start="11783" data-end="11807">AmplistwistorAlgebra X</code> that actually does something numerically meaningful.</p>
</li>
