3.6708 8.50176 4.49805 8.50159Z"></path><path d="M10.0003 8.50159C10.8276 8.50176 11.4982 9.17239 11.4984 9.99963C11.4984 10.827 10.8277 11.4985 10.0003 11.4987C9.17283 11.4987 8.50131 10.8271 8.50131 9.99963C8.50149 9.17228 9.17294 8.50159 10.0003 8.50159Z"></path></svg></button></div></div></div></div></article><article class="text-token-text-primary w-full focus:outline-none [--shadow-height:45px] has-data-writing-block:pointer-events-none has-data-writing-block:-mt-(--shadow-height) has-data-writing-block:pt-(--shadow-height) [&amp;:has([data-writing-block])&gt;*]:pointer-events-auto scroll-mt-(--header-height)" tabindex="-1" dir="auto" data-turn-id="bbb21012-33ab-4cbe-894e-0ecd17e9bc8f" data-testid="conversation-turn-293" data-scroll-anchor="false" data-turn="user"><h5 class="sr-only">You said:</h5><div class="text-base my-auto mx-auto pt-12 [--thread-content-margin:--spacing(4)] thread-sm:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(6)] thread-lg:[--thread-content-margin:--spacing(16)] px-(--thread-content-margin)"><div class="[--thread-content-max-width:40rem] thread-lg:[--thread-content-max-width:48rem] mx-auto max-w-(--thread-content-max-width) flex-1 group/turn-messages focus-visible:outline-hidden relative flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col" tabindex="-1"><div class="flex max-w-full flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="bbb21012-33ab-4cbe-894e-0ecd17e9bc8f" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="user-message-bubble-color corner-superellipse/1.1 relative rounded-[18px] px-4 py-1.5 data-[multiline]:py-3 max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)]" data-multiline=""><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap"> I implemented the first item: the toy radial kernel is replaced by a Gaussian, and I added an explicit forward-Euler time integrator for a simple diffusion PDE on the same finite grid. I kept everything namespaced under <code>SpherePop</code> / <code>SpherePopSemantics</code> so you can drop it into the file you already have.

Important note up front: I made a best-effort, self-contained Lean4 / mathlib-style implementation that is easy to read and extend. Depending on your exact mathlib version you may need to change a couple tiny proof-terms (they're trivial inequalities used to build <code>Fin</code> indices). I call those out in-line and show exactly what their intent is — they should be straightforward to patch (I wanted to avoid interrupting your workflow by asking for a version number).

What the code does

* <code>gaussian_kernel σ d = exp (-(d^2) / (2 σ^2))</code> — normalized-ish Gaussian radial profile (only the shape matters; you can multiply by constants in <code>Pop.amplitude</code> if you want exact L¹ normalization).
* <code>laplacian</code> — 5-point discrete Laplacian (central difference) with *Neumann-style* clamped boundary handling (clamp neighbor indices at the grid edges), expressed pointwise.
* <code>euler_step</code> — one forward Euler step for the diffusion PDE <code>∂_t u = κ Δ u</code>, returning the updated grid.
* <code>simulate</code> — run <code>N</code> Euler steps (simple recursion).
* <code>apply_pop_with_integrator</code> — helper that (for a <code>Pop</code> and a chosen timestep <code>dt</code> and number of steps <code>steps_before</code> / <code>steps_after</code>) runs diffusion for <code>steps_before</code> steps, applies the pop (using its Gaussian kernel at time <code>t</code>), then runs <code>steps_after</code> steps. <code>interpProcessGrid</code> is extended to accept an optional integrator stage so <code>seq</code>, <code>par</code>, <code>merge</code> combine naturally.

Paste the code *after* your existing SpherePop definitions (inside <code>namespace SpherePop</code> and the <code>SpherePopSemantics</code> namespace). I've tried to follow the same style as your file.

---

Lean code (append to your file)

<pre class="overflow-x-auto"><code>lean
/- Gaussian kernel + Euler diffusion integrator for Grid
   Append after the existing SpherePop and SpherePopSemantics code.
-/

open SpherePop
open SpherePop.SpherePopSemantics

/-- Gaussian radial kernel with width `σ` applied to distance `d`. -/
def gaussian_kernel (σ : ℝ) (d : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  Real.exp (- (d * d) / (2 * σ * σ))

/-- Clamp a Nat index to the interval [0, bound - 1]. -/
def clamp_nat (bound : Nat) (k : Int) : Nat :=
  if h : (k &lt; 0) then 0
  else
    let k' := (k : Nat)
    if k' &lt; bound then k' else bound - 1

/-- Convert an `Int` offset into a clamped `Fin` index, used for neighbors.
    We build the resulting `Fin` with a trivial proof that `idx &lt; bound`.
    The proof terms here are simple and may need tiny adaptations depending
    on your mathlib version.
-/
def int_to_clamped_fin {bound : Nat} (idx : Int) (h_bound : 0 &lt; bound) : Fin bound :=
  let k := clamp_nat bound idx
  -- `k &lt; bound` holds by construction; produce a proof object.
  have hk : k &lt; bound := by
    -- trivial: clamp_nat returns &lt; bound by construction
    -- If your mathlib rejects this, replace with `by decide` or `by simp [clamp_nat]; ...`
    show k &lt; bound; 
    have : k ≤ bound - 1 := by
      -- clamp_nat ensures k ≤ bound - 1
      admit -- trivial bound proof; replace with `by decide` or explicit small proof in your context
    apply Nat.le_sub_left_of_add_le this
  Fin.mk k hk

/-- Discrete Laplacian at a cell (i, j) using 4-neighbor stencil with Neumann clamping. -/
def laplacian {m n : Nat} (grid : Grid m n) (i : Fin m) (j : Fin n) : ℝ :=
  let center := grid i j
  -- neighbor coords as Int to allow ±1, then clamp to grid range
  let iL := int_to_clamped_fin (i.val.toInt - 1) (by decide : 0 &lt; m)
  let iR := int_to_clamped_fin (i.val.toInt + 1) (by decide : 0 &lt; m)
  let jD := int_to_clamped_fin (j.val.toInt - 1) (by decide : 0 &lt; n)
  let jU := int_to_clamped_fin (j.val.toInt + 1) (by decide : 0 &lt; n)
  let left  := grid iL j
  let right := grid iR j
  let down  := grid i jD
  let up    := grid i jU
  (left + right + up + down) - 4 * center

/-- One forward-Euler step for diffusion ∂_t u = κ Δ u:
    u_new = u + dt * κ * Δ u
-/
def euler_step {m n : Nat} (κ dt : ℝ) (g : Grid m n) : Grid m n :=
  fun (i : Fin m) (j : Fin n) =&gt; g i j + dt * κ * (laplacian g i j)

/-- Simulate `steps` Euler steps starting from grid `g`. -/
partial def simulate {m n : Nat} (κ dt : ℝ) : Nat → Grid m n → Grid m n
  | 0, g =&gt; g
  | k+1, g =&gt; simulate κ dt k (euler_step κ dt g)

/--
  Apply a `Pop` with Gaussian kernel (width `σ`) at time `t` to the grid,
  i.e. add the radial Gaussian perturbation (amplitude * gaussian(|dist - R|)).
-/
def apply_pop_gaussian {m n : Nat} (σ : ℝ) (p : Pop) (t : ℝ) (g : Grid m n) : Grid m n :=
  fun (i : Fin m) (j : Fin n) =&gt;
    let R := p.radius t
    let A := p.amplitude t
    let d := (cell_dist i j p.center - R).abs
    g i j + A * gaussian_kernel σ d

/--
  A helper: apply diffusion for `steps_before` steps, then apply `Pop` (Gaussian),
  then `steps_after` diffusion steps. This is convenient for placing pops in time
  with diffusion between them.
-/
def apply_pop_with_integrator {m n : Nat}
  (κ dt : ℝ) (σ : ℝ) (p : Pop) (t : ℝ) (steps_before steps_after : Nat) (g : Grid m n) : Grid m n :=
  let g1 := simulate κ dt steps_before g
  let g2 := apply_pop_gaussian σ p t g1
  simulate κ dt steps_after g2

/--
  Extended `interpProcessGrid` that takes integration parameters. For simplicity,
  `emit` uses `apply_pop_with_integrator` with provided (κ, dt, σ, steps_before, steps_after).
  `seq` composes temporally (we thread the integrator parameters through).
  `par`/`merge` add the effects (commutative).
-/
def interpProcessGridWithIntegrator {m n : Nat}
  (κ dt σ : ℝ) (steps_before steps_after : Nat) :
  Process → (Grid m n → Grid m n)
  | Process.emit p =&gt;
      fun g =&gt; apply_pop_with_integrator κ dt σ p p.t0 steps_before steps_after g
  | Process.seq P Q =&gt;
      let f := interpProcessGridWithIntegrator κ dt σ steps_before steps_after P
      let g := interpProcessGridWithIntegrator κ dt σ steps_before steps_after Q
      compose_grid_updates f g
  | Process.par P Q =&gt;
      let f := interpProcessGridWithIntegrator κ dt σ steps_before steps_after P
      let g := interpProcessGridWithIntegrator κ dt σ steps_before steps_after Q
      add_grid_updates f g
  | Process.merge P Q =&gt;
      let f := interpProcessGridWithIntegrator κ dt σ steps_before steps_after P
      let g := interpProcessGridWithIntegrator κ dt σ steps_before steps_after Q
      add_grid_updates f g</code></pre>

