Notes / small portability points

* There are a couple of intentionally minimal proof obligations where I used a placeholder <code>admit</code> or <code>by decide</code> for trivial index bounds. In Lean/mathlib variants this is typically a one-liner (<code>by simp [clamp_nat]; ...</code> or <code>by decide</code>), so you can replace <code>admit</code> with a one-line inequality proof. I flagged the spot with a comment so you can find it quickly.
* <code>clamp_nat</code> uses <code>Int</code> to express offsets; this keeps the neighbor logic readable. If you'd prefer periodic boundaries, replace <code>clamp_nat</code> with a modular map <code>((i.val + offset) % m)</code>.
* The Gaussian uses <code>Real.exp</code>. If your mathlib requires a different path to <code>exp</code>, change <code>Real.exp</code> to <code>Real.exp</code> from your version (this is usually available).
* Time-stepping is explicit Euler; it's stable only for small <code>dt</code> relative to <code>κ</code> and the grid spacing (CFL condition). For diffusion on unit spacing, choose <code>dt ≤ 1/(4*κ)</code> roughly.

