n-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-1"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="405">Below are <strong data-start="10" data-end="30">Sections C and D</strong>, written in continuous academic prose and ready to drop into the monograph.<br data-start="106" data-end="109">
They follow naturally from the Green’s function and semigroup sections, and deepen the theoretical bridge between the mathematical structure of amplitwistor cascades, the discrete RSVP semigroup, and the empirical neuroscientific literature on cortical waves and hierarchical temporal processing.</p>
<hr data-start="407" data-end="410">
<h1 data-start="412" data-end="481"><strong data-start="414" data-end="481">C. Hybrid Operadic–Semigroup Algebras for Amplitwistor Cascades</strong></h1>
<p data-start="483" data-end="1114">The semigroup interpretation of Euler stepping and Gaussian pop propagators makes it possible to articulate a unified algebraic structure combining operadic composition with continuous flow. This hybrid structure captures the essential features of amplitwistor cascades: discrete spherical-generation events composed operadically, evolving within a continuous dissipative medium governed by a generator <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">L</span></span></span></span>. The resulting mathematical object is best understood as a <em data-start="960" data-end="995">hybrid operadic–semigroup algebra</em>, a construction that permits both discrete and continuous modes of evolution while preserving compositional semantics.</p>
<p data-start="1116" data-end="1703">In this framework, the SpherePop operad <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="script">O</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">P</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal O_{\mathrm{SP}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8333em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">O</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0278em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathrm mtight">SP</span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> provides the syntax of discrete events. Each pop corresponds to a nullary operation whose semantic interpretation is an instantaneous operator <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.08222em;">P</span></span></span></span> acting on the field configuration. Higher-arity SpherePop operations, such as merges or interference cascades, correspond to multilinear operators built from combinations of the fundamental Gaussian propagators. Operadic composition dictates how spherical events may be composed: sequentially by insertion, or in parallel through symmetric monoidal structure.</p>
<p data-start="1705" data-end="2276">On the analytic side, the RSVP evolution defines a strongly continuous semigroup <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> generated by the operator <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">L</span></span></span></span>. The generator <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">L</span></span></span></span> captures the infinitesimal structure of the dynamics, ensuring that for fields <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">ϕ</span></span></span></span> in the appropriate domain, the evolution satisfies the differential equation <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="script">L</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{\phi} = \mathcal L \phi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.1257em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord accent"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.9313em;"><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">ϕ</span></span><span style="top: -3.2634em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="accent-body" style="left: -0.0556em;"><span class="mord">˙</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1944em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">L</span><span class="mord mathnormal">ϕ</span></span></span></span>. The semigroup acts as the background geometric scaffold on which operadic events unfold, providing the dissipative, diffusive, and stabilizing structure needed to maintain coherence in the amplitwistor cascades.</p>
<p data-start="2278" data-end="2550">The key insight is that the instantaneous pop operator <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.08222em;">P</span></span></span></span> can be inserted between semigroup evolutions without violating coherence. More formally, if <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> denotes the continuous evolution and <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.08222em;">P</span></span></span></span> denotes a pop event, then a process of the form</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant="script">P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant="script">P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(t_2 - t_1)\,\mathcal{P}_1\,T(t_1 - t_0)\,\mathcal{P}_0\,T(t_0)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">2</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">−</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.08222em;">P</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0822em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">−</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">0</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.08222em;">P</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0822em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">0</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">0</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="2622" data-end="3011">is well-defined in the semigroup framework and corresponds exactly to the operadic derivation tree expressing the SpherePop program. Sequential operadic composition becomes chronological operator insertion, while parallel composition becomes additive superposition of propagators applied to the same field state. The hybrid algebra is therefore structured by an interaction law of the form</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∘</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="script">P</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∘</mo><mo>⋯</mo><mo>∘</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="script">P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∘</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(P) = T(t_k - t_{k-1}) \circ \mathcal{P}_{k-1} \circ \cdots \circ \mathcal{P}_1 \circ T(t_1),</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">A</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">P</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3361em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03148em;">k</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">−</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3361em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03148em;">k</span><span class="mbin mtight">−</span><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2083em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">∘</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8917em; vertical-align: -0.2083em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.08222em;">P</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3361em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0822em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03148em;">k</span><span class="mbin mtight">−</span><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2083em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">∘</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.4445em;"></span><span class="minner">⋯</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">∘</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8333em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.08222em;">P</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.0822em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">∘</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mpunct">,</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="3123" data-end="3471">where <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal A(P)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">A</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">P</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> denotes the analytic semantics of the SpherePop process <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">P</span></span></span></span>. This realizes SpherePop as an algebra over an operator-valued semigroup, a hybrid object analogous to constructions appearing in quantum field theory (Dyson expansions), stochastic processes (jump semigroups), and the theory of impulsive differential equations.</p>
<p data-start="3473" data-end="4117">What distinguishes the amplitwistor construction is that the propagators themselves are derived from discrete approximations to the Green’s functions of <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">L</span></span></span></span>. Thus the operadic algebra is <em data-start="3671" data-end="3695">geometrically grounded</em>: the operations express not arbitrary symbolic manipulations but physically meaningful transformations of diffusive and oscillatory fields. Parallel merging in the operad corresponds to the linear superposition of Green’s function responses, while sequential insertion corresponds to causal propagation governed by the semigroup. The result is a principled synthesis of algebraic compositionality with geometric dynamics.</p>
<p data-start="4119" data-end="4709">This hybrid operadic–semigroup structure is precisely what enables amplitwistor cascades to be interpreted as computational architectures. Each cascade defines not merely a symbolic composition of events but a physically realizable, temporally extended evolution in a field substrate. The RSVP equations provide the infinitesimal geometry; the semigroup yields temporal coherence; the SpherePop operad gives compositional structure; and the amplitwistor algebra mediates interactions between them. Together, they form a unified theory of structured spherical computation in dynamical media.</p>
<hr data-start="4711" data-end="4714">
<h1 data-start="4716" data-end="4806"><strong data-start="4718" data-end="4806">D. Correspondence With Cortical Traveling Waves and Hierarchical Temporal Processing</strong></h1>
<p data-start="4808" data-end="5319">The hybrid algebraic–geometric structure developed above has a natural and compelling interpretation in the context of cortical dynamics. Neuroscientific evidence increasingly suggests that cortical computation is mediated not by discrete symbolic operations but by spatiotemporal wave patterns propagating across heterogeneous tissue. These waveforms exhibit characteristic scales, speeds, and coherence structures that reflect the underlying connectivity and biophysical constraints of cortical microcircuits.</p>
<p data-start="5321" data-end="6013">A wealth of empirical studies demonstrates that the cortex supports traveling wave phenomena across spatial scales. Ultrafast fMRI reveals large-scale oscillatory modes that can be captured by eigenmode decomposition of the structural connectome, with the lowest-frequency modes extending across the entire cortex and higher modes localized to specific regions. Electrophysiological measurements complement this picture: intracranial EEG and MEG record propagating phase patterns associated with perception, attention, and working memory. These waveforms are not epiphenomena; they encode integrative computation by allowing local information to be coherently pooled across distance and time.</p>
<p data-start="6015" data-end="6588">The RSVP and amplitwistor frameworks naturally map onto these observations. The operator <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">L</span></span></span></span>, linearized around a background state, has eigenmodes whose temporal evolution corresponds to oscillatory and diffusive traveling waves. The Gaussian kernels applied by pop events approximate discrete Green's functions of <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">L</span></span></span></span>, meaning that the elementary SpherePop operations produce precisely the localized perturbations that excite these eigenmodes. Sequential and parallel compositions of pop events then generate structured cascades of wave interactions.</p>
<p data-start="6590" data-end="7363">This mapping becomes particularly salient when one considers hierarchical temporal processing in the cortex. Empirical studies consistently show that early sensory regions exhibit short integration windows and rapid temporal responses, whereas higher-order association cortices exhibit progressively longer integration windows, extending up to several hundred milliseconds. These windows correspond to increasing depth along the cortical hierarchy and are reflected in the broader spatial support and slower decay of their associated eigenmodes. The RSVP semigroup <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> reproduces this structure through the differential action of its generator <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal L</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">L</span></span></span></span>: modes associated with higher-level cortical regions evolve more slowly and integrate over longer timescales.</p>
<p data-start="7365" data-end="8020">Furthermore, the correspondence extends to the hierarchical alignment between deep layers of large language models and latencies in cortical responses reported in recent ECoG studies. In such studies, deeper artificial network layers map systematically onto longer human cortical latencies during language processing, a finding that parallels the RSVP interpretation of amplitwistor cascades as sequences of increasingly deep propagations through eigenmode space. A pop at time <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7651em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">0</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> excites modes that unfold over multiple scales; its operadic descendants represent deeper, more abstract transformations that naturally produce longer-latency responses.</p>
<p data-start="8022" data-end="8705">The essential insight is that cortical computation appears to rely on the same compositional principles embodied in the hybrid operadic–semigroup algebra. Instantaneous local perturbations (analogous to pops) interact with a dissipative background field that integrates information over time and space. Structured temporal cascades of traveling waves manifest as computational operations, with deeper cascades corresponding to more abstract integration. The amplitwistor formalism provides a principled mathematical language for expressing these phenomena, placing cortical dynamics, large-scale field theory, and operadic computational semantics into a unified conceptual framework.</p>
<hr data-start="8707" data-end="8710">
<p data-start="8712" data-end="9066" data-is-last-node="" data-is-only-node="">If you would like, I can now generate <strong data-start="8750" data-end="8763">Section E</strong>, which treats <em data-start="8778" data-end="8848">error bounds and convergence of the discrete semigroup approximation</em>, or <strong data-start="8853" data-end="8866">Section F</strong>, which relates the amplitwistor cascade framework to <em data-start="8920" data-end="8974">empirical spectral decompositions of cortical fields</em> (eigenmodes, connectome harmonics, Laplace–Beltrami operators on folded cortical surfaces).</p></div></div></div></div><div class="z-0 flex min-h-[46px] justify-start"><div class="touch:-me-2 touch:-ms-3.5 -ms-2.5 -me-1 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-y-4 p-1 select-none touch:w-[calc(100%+--spacing(3.5))] -mt-1 w-[calc(100%+--spacing(2.5))] duration-[1.5s] focus-within:transition-none hover:transition-none touch:pointer-events-auto pointer-events-none [mask-image:linear-gradient(to_right,black_33%,transparent_66%)] [mask-size:300%_100%] [mask-position:100%_0%] motion-safe:transition-[mask-position] group-hover/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-hover/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] group-focus-within/turn-messages:pointer-events-auto group-focus-within/turn-messages:[mask-position:0_0] has-data-[state=open]:pointer-events-auto has-data-[state=open]:[mask-position:0_0]" style="mask-position: 0% 0%;"><button class="text-token-text-secondary hover:bg-token-bg-secondary rounded-lg" aria-label="Copy" aria-pressed="false" data-testid="copy-turn-action-button" data-state="closed"><span class="flex items-center justify-center touch:w-10 h-8 w-8"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 