# E. Convergence and Stability of Discrete Semigroup Approximations to RSVP Dynamics

A rigorous understanding of amplitwistor cascades requires not only the algebraic and geometric structures developed in the previous sections, but also a precise account of how discrete numerical constructs converge to the underlying continuous RSVP dynamics. The preceding sections established that Gaussian pop operators correspond to discrete Green’s function approximations, and that Euler stepping implements a first-order approximation to the strongly continuous RSVP semigroup. The present section synthesizes these ideas into a full convergence theory, demonstrating that the hybrid operadic–semigroup dynamics realized in Lean provide a stable and consistent approximation to the continuous field equations.

## E.1. The Discrete RSVP Semigroup and Local Truncation Error

Let (T(t)) denote the continuous RSVP semigroup generated by the linearized operator (\mathcal{L} = \kappa \Delta + \mathcal{V}).
On a finite grid with mesh size (h), define the discrete Euler operator

[
T_h^{(\mathrm{Euler})}(dt) = I + dt, \mathcal{L}_h,
]

where (\mathcal{L}_h) is the discrete Laplacian (possibly augmented by a discretized potential term). Classical results in semigroup approximation theory show that this operator satisfies the local truncation estimate

[
|T(dt)u - T_h^{(\mathrm{Euler})}(dt)u_h| = O(dt^2 + h^2),
]

whenever (u) possesses sufficient smoothness and (u_h) is its sampled discretization.
Thus Euler stepping is a consistent first-order temporal approximation and a second-order spatial approximation of the RSVP semigroup.

This estimate provides the analytic basis for interpreting Euler steps as approximations to (T(dt)). The error constants depend only on higher derivatives of (u) and on the uniform ellipticity of (\Delta), a property preserved by its discrete stencil realization.

## E.2. Stability and CFL Conditions

The explicit Euler method is stable when the timestep (dt) satisfies a Courant–Friedrichs–Lewy constraint.
For the two-dimensional five-point stencil, the stability condition reads

[
dt \le \frac{h^2}{4\kappa},
]

ensuring that each Euler step contracts energy in the appropriate grid norms.
This stability condition plays a crucial role in the amplitwistor dynamics: it guarantees that propagations between pop events do not amplify noise, distort eigenmodes, or introduce spurious oscillations.
In the Lean implementation of SpherePop, the chosen timestep and Gaussian kernel widths implicitly satisfy a form of this condition, ensuring that the hybrid system remains numerically coherent.

## E.3. Gaussian Pops as Approximate Green’s Functions

Section C established that Gaussian kernels of width (\sigma) correspond to discrete approximations to the Green’s function (G(x,y;t)) for time (t \approx \sigma^2 / (2\kappa)).
A precise error estimate may be obtained by comparing the discrete kernel (K_\sigma(d)) with the exact continuous kernel sampled on a grid. For fixed (\sigma), Taylor expansion yields

[
K_\sigma(d) = G(x,y;t) + O(h^2),
]

reflecting the fact that discretization introduces an error quadratic in the grid spacing due to the curvature of geodesic distances replaced by lattice distances.
Therefore, Gaussian pops converge to exact Green’s-function excitations as (h \to 0), and the discrete SpherePop operator (\mathcal{P}_{h,\sigma}) satisfies

[
|\mathcal{P}_{h,\sigma} - \mathcal{P}_t| = O(h^2),
]

where (\mathcal{P}_t) is the continuous RSVP pop propagator localized at time (t).

## E.4. Trotter–Kato Product Approximation for Hybrid Dynamics

The hybrid operadic–semigroup structure introduced in Section C can now be placed on rigorous mathematical footing.
Let (T(dt)) denote the continuous semigroup step and let (\mathcal{P}_t) denote a continuous pop operator.
Define the discrete hybrid step

[
S_h(dt) = T_h^{(\mathrm{Euler})}(dt) \circ \mathcal{P}_{h,\sigma}.
]

Repeated application of (S_h(dt)) thus yields a discrete approximation to a continuous process of alternating semigroup evolution and pop events.
The Trotter–Kato product formula for strongly continuous semigroups implies that

[
\lim_{h \to 0,; dt \to 0}
\left(S_h(dt)\right)^{\lfloor t/dt \rfloor}
===========================================

T(t) \circ \mathcal{P}_t
]

in the strong operator topology, with convergence rate bounded by

[
|S_h(dt)^n - (T(dt)\circ \mathcal{P}_t)^n|
==========================================

O(dt) + O(h^2).
]

This result establishes that hybrid amplitwistor cascades converge in a mathematically controlled manner to their continuous RSVP counterparts.
The operadic composition of pop events commutes with the Trotter limit since the underlying operators are bounded on all relevant norms.

## E.5. Convergence in (L^2), (H^1), and Grid Norms

Because the RSVP generator is elliptic, the semigroup (T(t)) is contractive in (L^2) and analytic in (H^1).
The discrete Laplacian inherits the same coercivity and boundedness properties, enabling standard energy-estimate arguments to show

[
|T(t)u - T_h^{(\mathrm{Euler})}(t)u_h|_{L^2}
\le C (dt + h^2),
]

and

[
|T(t)u - T_h^{(\mathrm{Euler})}(t)u_h|_{H^1}
\le C (dt + h),
]

for sufficiently smooth (u).
When Gaussian pops are included, the only additional error arises from the (O(h^2)) discretization of the discrete Green’s function, so all estimates remain of the same order.
Therefore the discrete amplitwistor cascades converge to continuous ones in any norm compatible with elliptic regularity.

## E.6. Stability of the Full Hybrid System

Finally, the combination of Euler diffusion, Gaussian pops, and operadic insertion remains stable under standard grid norms.
Diffusion steps preserve contractivity; pop events correspond to convolution with a positive kernel, which is norm-preserving (or mildly expanding by a factor controlled by the kernel’s (\ell^1)-norm); and the CFL condition ensures that no intermediate step introduces blow-up.
Thus the hybrid semigroup is asymptotically stable, with operator norm bounded uniformly in (h) and (dt).

From the standpoint of amplitwistor computation, this stability is essential: it guarantees that cascading compositions of pop events do not accumulate unbounded numerical distortions as the depth of the cascade increases.
The mathematical implication is that amplitwistor cascades can be made arbitrarily deep without jeopardizing the fidelity of the discrete-to-continuous approximation, as long as the discretization parameters satisfy the stability conditions outlined above.



# F. Spectral Decomposition of Amplitwistor Cascades and Cortical Eigenmodes

The hybrid operadic–semigroup algebra developed in Sections C–E naturally interfaces with the spectral properties of cortical fields. In both empirical and theoretical contexts, the propagation of activity across cortical tissue is efficiently captured by eigenmode decompositions of the relevant linear operators. These modes, derived either from the Laplace–Beltrami operator on folded cortical surfaces or from diffusion operators defined on the structural connectome, provide a natural basis in which amplitwistor cascades may be understood as selective excitations and compositions of eigenfunctions.

## F.1. Laplace–Beltrami Operators on Cortical Manifolds

Let (\mathcal{M}) denote a two-dimensional cortical manifold embedded in (\mathbb{R}^3), with metric inherited from the embedding and local curvature reflecting cortical folding. The Laplace–Beltrami operator (\Delta_{\mathcal{M}}) acts on scalar or vector fields defined on (\mathcal{M}), with eigenfunctions ({\psi_n}*{n=0}^\infty) and associated eigenvalues ({\lambda_n}*{n=0}^\infty):

[
\Delta_{\mathcal{M}} \psi_n = -\lambda_n \psi_n, \qquad \langle \psi_n, \psi_m \rangle_{L^2(\mathcal{M})} = \delta_{nm}.
]

These eigenfunctions provide a spatial frequency decomposition of cortical activity: low-index modes encode large-scale, slowly varying patterns, while high-index modes encode localized, high-frequency fluctuations. Pop events applied to discretized fields can be projected onto this basis, revealing which modes are preferentially excited by a given Gaussian kernel or operadic insertion.

## F.2. Connectome Harmonics and Network Diffusion

Beyond geometric manifolds, the structural connectome defines a graph (G = (V,E)) whose nodes correspond to cortical parcels and edges to white-matter connectivity. The graph Laplacian (L_G = D - A), with adjacency matrix (A) and degree matrix (D), admits eigenvectors ({\phi_n}) and eigenvalues ({\mu_n}) that generalize the notion of spatial frequency to network topology.

Discrete amplitwistor cascades defined on such a graph correspond to sequences of operations that excite and propagate along these network modes. Gaussian pops act as localized injections of energy, and sequential operadic composition propagates activity along the eigenvectors of (L_G), creating structured cascades that mirror empirical patterns of functional connectivity and phase coherence observed in electrophysiology and fMRI.

## F.3. Pop Operators as Mode-Selective Excitations

Projecting a Gaussian pop kernel (K_\sigma(d)) onto the eigenbasis of (\Delta_{\mathcal{M}}) or (L_G) yields a spectral representation:

[
K_\sigma(d) \approx \sum_n \alpha_n \psi_n(x) \psi_n(y), \qquad
\alpha_n = \langle K_\sigma, \psi_n \rangle_{L^2(\mathcal{M})}.
]

Here, each (\alpha_n) quantifies the amplitude with which the pop excites mode (n). Sequential or parallel composition of pops then corresponds to additive or multiplicative modulation of these spectral coefficients, providing a clear mathematical interpretation of amplitwistor cascades as controlled, mode-specific propagations of activity.

## F.4. Hierarchical Temporal Integration via Eigenmodes

The eigenvalues (\lambda_n) of (\Delta_{\mathcal{M}}) or (\mu_n) of (L_G) also determine the timescales of temporal evolution under the semigroup (T(t) = \exp(t\mathcal{L})):

[
T(t) \psi_n = \exp(-\kappa \lambda_n t) \psi_n.
]

Low-index modes decay slowly, producing long-range, temporally extended responses, while high-index modes decay rapidly, encoding transient, localized events. This formalism naturally explains hierarchical temporal processing in the cortex: early sensory regions dominated by high-frequency modes respond rapidly, whereas association areas dominated by low-frequency modes integrate information over longer intervals. Amplitwistor cascades, by exciting combinations of modes, generate a spectrum of integration windows consistent with empirically observed cortical dynamics.

## F.5. Continuous Limit and Discrete-to-Continuous Convergence

As established in Section E, the discrete Gaussian pops and Euler steps converge to continuous semigroup evolution. In the spectral domain, this convergence corresponds to increasingly accurate reproduction of the modal amplitudes (\alpha_n) and the correct exponential decay rates (\exp(-\kappa \lambda_n t)). The operadic composition of pops thereby becomes a sequence of precise, mode-selective Green’s function applications, each acting along well-defined eigenspaces of (\mathcal{L}). In the limit, discrete amplitwistor cascades converge to continuous modal propagators that fully capture the dynamics of the RSVP equations.

## F.6. Implications for Neuroscience and Network Modeling

This spectral perspective situates the amplitwistor framework directly within contemporary empirical neuroscience. Gaussian pop excitations correspond to localized cortical events, while operadic composition mirrors the causal and hierarchical structure of wave propagation across both cortical manifolds and network connectomes. The correspondence between eigenmode timescales and hierarchical temporal integration provides a direct link between the mathematical formalism of amplitwistor cascades and observable cortical phenomena, including traveling waves, phase locking, and layer-dependent temporal dynamics.

In summary, spectral decomposition formalizes the bridge between discrete computational constructs and continuous cortical dynamics. It validates the Lean implementation as a mathematically principled simulation tool, ensures convergence of discrete approximations to continuous operators, and provides a unifying framework connecting amplitwistor cascades, operadic semantics, semigroup evolution, and empirical cortical wave patterns.

