<h1 data-start="446" data-end="534"><strong data-start="448" data-end="534">E. Convergence and Stability of Discrete Semigroup Approximations to RSVP Dynamics</strong></h1>
<p data-start="536" data-end="1220">A rigorous understanding of amplitwistor cascades requires not only the hybrid algebraic–geometric foundations developed in the preceding sections, but also a mathematically precise analysis of how discrete numerical constructs converge to the continuous RSVP dynamics. The key objective of this section is to demonstrate that the Lean implementation—composed of Gaussian pop operators and forward-Euler diffusion steps—constitutes a consistent, stable, and convergent approximation to the infinitesimal generator of the RSVP semigroup. The resulting hybrid operadic–semigroup system approximates the continuous evolution with controllable error bounds in a variety of analytic norms.</p>
<h2 data-start="1222" data-end="1293"><strong data-start="1225" data-end="1293">E.1. Discrete Semigroup Approximation and Local Truncation Error</strong></h2>
<p data-start="1295" data-end="1377">Let <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(t)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> denote the continuous RSVP semigroup generated by the linear operator</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="script">L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="script">V</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L} = \kappa \Delta + \mathcal{V},</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal">L</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7667em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">κ</span><span class="mord">Δ</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8778em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathcal" style="margin-right: 0.08222em;">V</span><span class="mpunct">,</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="1427" data-end="1595">arising from a linearization of the scalar field equations around a background configuration.<br data-start="1520" data-end="1523">
