.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">O</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal">d</span><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mpunct">,</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="8947" data-end="9004">ensuring convergence of modal amplitudes and decay rates.</p>
<p data-start="9006" data-end="9089">Thus, <em data-start="9012" data-end="9088">discrete amplitwistor cascades converge to continuous spectral propagators</em>.</p>
<h2 data-start="9091" data-end="9132"><strong data-start="9094" data-end="9132">F.6. Implications for Neuroscience</strong></h2>
<p data-start="9134" data-end="9176">This spectral–operadic framework explains:</p>
<ul data-start="9177" data-end="9442">
<li data-start="9177" data-end="9230">
<p data-start="9179" data-end="9230">traveling cortical waves as eigenmode propagations,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9231" data-end="9299">
<p data-start="9233" data-end="9299">hierarchical temporal processing as spectral decay stratification,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9300" data-end="9360">
<p data-start="9302" data-end="9360">stimulus-dependent cascades as mode-selective excitations,</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9361" data-end="9442">
<p data-start="9363" data-end="9442">long-range synchronization as constructive interference of low-frequency modes.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="9444" data-end="9615">Amplitwistor cascades therefore supply a mathematically grounded mechanism for generating—and analyzing—the full spectrum of empirically observed cortical field phenomena.</p>
