</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% E. Convergence and Stability of Discrete Semigroup Approximations to RSVP Dynamics</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Convergence and Stability of Discrete Semigroup Approximations to RSVP Dynamics}

A rigorous understanding of amplitwistor cascades requires not only the hybrid algebraic--geometric foundations developed in the preceding sections, but also a mathematically precise analysis of how discrete numerical constructs converge to the continuous RSVP dynamics. The aim of this section is to demonstrate that the implementation described earlier---consisting of Gaussian pop operators interleaved with explicit Euler diffusion steps---constitutes a consistent, stable, and convergent approximation to the infinitesimal generator of the RSVP semigroup. The resulting hybrid operadic--semigroup system converges in a strong, quantifiable sense to the continuous dynamics and admits stable evolution over long timescales.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Discrete Semigroup Approximation and Local Truncation Error}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote the continuous RSVP semigroup generated by the linear operator
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span> + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V},
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
obtained by linearizing the RSVP field equations around a background state.  
On a grid of mesh size </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> we define the discrete forward--Euler operator
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Euler}}(dt) = I + dt</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denotes the discrete Laplacian (and possibly a discretization of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{V}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).  
For sufficiently smooth functions </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, we obtain the standard local truncation estimate
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>T(dt)u - T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Euler}}(dt)u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> = O</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(dt</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 + h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the grid-sampled representation of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Consequently, the Euler time-stepping scheme yields a first-order temporal approximation and a second-order spatial approximation of the semigroup </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, with constants of proportionality controlled by higher spatial derivatives of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and uniform ellipticity of the Laplacian.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Stability and the CFL Condition}

Stability of the explicit Euler diffusion scheme requires the Courant--Friedrichs--Lewy condition.  
For the two-dimensional five-point stencil, the stability criterion is
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
dt </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2}{4</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span>}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Within this domain, the discrete diffusion step is contractive in the relevant </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> grid norms, preventing spurious oscillations or numerical blow-up.  
In the context of amplitwistor cascades, this CFL constraint ensures that the propagation phases between pop events remain well-behaved, so that numerical artefacts do not accumulate across the operadic hierarchy.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Gaussian Pops as Approximate Green's Functions}

The discrete Gaussian kernel
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(d) = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exp</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{d</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2}{2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
is an approximation to the continuous Green's function for the heat equation,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
G(x,y;t) =
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{1}{(4</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span> t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{d/2}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\exp</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\!</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>x-y</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2}{4</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span> t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> 2</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span> t.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
A Taylor expansion establishes the pointwise approximation
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(d) = G(x,y;t) + O(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
revealing that discretized Gaussian pop events converge quadratically to instantaneous Green's-function excitations on the continuous domain.  
Thus the pop operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{h,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> satisfies the estimate
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{h,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>} - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span> = O(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the continuous RSVP pop propagator.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Trotter--Kato Approximation for Hybrid Dynamics}

Define the discrete hybrid step
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h(dt) = T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Euler}}(dt) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{h,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Repeated composition yields a discrete process alternating between diffusion and pop events.  
The Trotter--Kato product formula guarantees that
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lim</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\substack</span></span><span>{h</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> 0 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span> dt</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\to</span></span><span> 0}}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>(S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h(dt)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\big</span></span><span>)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lfloor</span></span><span> t/dt </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rfloor</span></span><span>}
= T(t) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
in the strong operator topology, with convergence rate bounded by
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>S</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h(dt)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n - (T(dt)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{P}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>
= O(dt) + O(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
This establishes that the discrete amplitwistor cascades converge to their continuous RSVP analogues in a controlled, quantifiable manner.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Convergence in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, and Discrete Norms}

The ellipticity of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{L}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> implies that </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>T(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is contractive in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and analytic in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.
Energy estimates yield the bounds
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>T(t)u - T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Euler}}(t)u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> C(dt + h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span>T(t)u - T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{Euler}}(t)u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>h</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\|</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{H</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\le</span></span><span> C(dt + h),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
for sufficiently smooth </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>u</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>.  
Gaussian pop discretization introduces only an additional </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>O(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> perturbation, so the hybrid amplitwistor system inherits the same asymptotic convergence properties.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Stability of the Hybrid System}

The complete hybrid dynamical system consisting of diffusion steps, Gaussian pop events, and operadic compositions remains uniformly stable provided that:  
(i) the CFL condition is satisfied;  
(ii) Gaussian kernels remain uniformly bounded in </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ell</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>1</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>; and  
(iii) operadic compositions preserve boundedness via multilinear estimates.  
Under these assumptions, amplitwistor cascades may be iterated indefinitely without accumulation of numerical instabilities, enabling arbitrarily deep operadic constructions consistent with continuous RSVP dynamics.

