</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">% F. Spectral Decomposition of Amplitwistor Cascades and Cortical Eigenmodes</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-comment">%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Spectral Decomposition of Amplitwistor Cascades and Cortical Eigenmodes}

The hybrid operadic--semigroup algebra introduced above interacts naturally with the spectral structure of cortical activity. In theoretical neuroscience and empirical neuroimaging, the spatiotemporal propagation of cortical fields is most effectively captured by decomposing the relevant operators into eigenmodes. These eigenmodes---defined on either geometrical cortical manifolds or structural connectome graphs---provide a natural basis for interpreting amplitwistor cascades as structured excitations of distinct dynamical modes.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Laplace--Beltrami Operators on Cortical Manifolds}

Let </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> denote a folded cortical surface endowed with its induced Riemannian metric.  
The Laplace--Beltrami operator </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> admits an orthonormal eigenbasis </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\{</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\}</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n=0}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\infty</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with eigenvalues </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\ge</span></span><span> 0</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n = -</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>m</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{M})} = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{nm}.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Small eigenvalues correspond to large-scale, slowly varying wave patterns; large eigenvalues correspond to localized, high-frequency fluctuations.  
Gaussian pops projected onto this basis reveal which modes are preferentially excited during an amplitwistor cascade, and how these excitations propagate along the cortical geometry.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Connectome Harmonics and Network Diffusion}

At the macroscale, cortical interactions may also be modeled using the structural connectome represented as a weighted graph </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>G=(V,E)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> with Laplacian
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>G = D - A,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>A</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> is the adjacency matrix and </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>D</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> the degree matrix.  
The eigenvectors of </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>L</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>G</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, sometimes called </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{connectome harmonics}, generalize the notion of spatial frequency to neural networks.  
Amplitwistor cascades defined on graph discretizations propagate preferentially along these eigenmodes, reproducing coherent patterns observed in resting-state functional MRI, electrocorticography, and MEG recordings.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Pops as Mode-Selective Excitations}

A Gaussian kernel </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> admits the spectral expansion
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>(x,y)
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\approx</span></span><span> 
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sum</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\,</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n(y),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span>.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Large </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> yields global, low-frequency excitations (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> large for small </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>);  
small </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\sigma</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> yields localized, high-frequency excitations (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> concentrated at large </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>).  
Sequential and parallel operadic compositions therefore act as structured, nonlinear modulations of these spectral coefficients.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Hierarchical Temporal Integration via Spectral Decay Rates}

Under diffusion, each eigenmode decays exponentially:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n = e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
Low-frequency modes survive for long timescales; high-frequency modes decay rapidly.  
This leads naturally to the cortical temporal hierarchy:  
sensory areas dominated by high-frequency modes respond rapidly, whereas association areas dominated by low-frequency modes integrate information over extended time windows.  
Amplitwistor cascades, by selectively exciting combinations of modes, generate temporal integration spectra consistent with these empirical observations.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Discrete-to-Continuous Spectral Convergence}

From the convergence theory in Section~E, discrete Gaussian propagators satisfy
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
|</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{(h)} - </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n| = O(h</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>2),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>|e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n t} - (1-dt</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{t/dt}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>| = O(dt),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
ensuring convergence of modal amplitudes and decay rates in the continuum limit.  
Thus discrete amplitwistor cascades provide accurate approximations to continuous spectral propagators underlying RSVP dynamics.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\subsection</span></span><span>{Implications for Neuroscience}

This spectral view yields a unified explanation of cortical phenomena including traveling waves, phase synchrony, multiscale integration, and hierarchical temporal processing.  
Amplitwistor cascades emerge as mathematically principled mechanisms for generating structured, mode-specific excitations of cortical fields, grounded in the operadic semantics of pop interactions and the analytic properties of semigroup evolution.

