This manuscript develops a unified mathematical and computational framework </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">for</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-title class_">modeling</span></span><span> cortical computation through amplitwistor cascades. The approach synthesizes operadic event semantics, strongly continuous semigroup evolution, spectral decomposition, and discrete Green</span><span><span class="hljs-symbol">'s</span></span><span>-function approximations. By grounding these constructs </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">in</span></span><span> empirical neuroscience, including eigenmode analyses and hierarchical temporal processing, the framework provides a principled account of multiscale cortical dynamics. The resulting theory offers a bridge between discrete computational primitives, continuous field evolution, and the hierarchical organization observed </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">in</span></span><span> biological neural systems.
\end{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">abstract</span></span><span>}
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
