The RSVP framework describes cortical computation through the interaction of scalar fields </span><span><span class="hljs-variable">$</span></span><span>\Phi</span><span><span class="hljs-variable">$</span></span><span>, vector fields </span><span><span class="hljs-variable">$</span></span><span>\bm{v}</span><span><span class="hljs-variable">$</span></span><span>, and entropy</span><span><span class="hljs-operator">-like</span></span><span> fields </span><span><span class="hljs-variable">$S</span></span><span><span class="hljs-variable">$</span></span><span>. Linearization around a background state yields an operator of the form
\[
\</span><span><span class="hljs-type">mathcal</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-type">L</span></span><span>} = \</span><span><span class="hljs-type">kappa</span></span><span> \</span><span><span class="hljs-type">Delta</span></span><span> + \</span><span><span class="hljs-type">mathcal</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-type">V</span></span><span>},
\]
which generates a strongly continuous semigroup governing the evolution of small perturbations. The operator inherits ellipticity from the Laplacian and admits a spectral decomposition </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">in</span></span><span> terms of eigenfunctions of the cortical manifold or connectome. These properties justify interpreting RSVP fields as substrates </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">for</span></span><span> multiscale temporal and spatial computation.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
