\begin{abstract}
This essay describes the goals, motivations, and formalization strategy underlying the Amplistwistor Program. Our objective is to explain, in accessible language and with increasing levels of formal rigor, what amplitwistors are meant to model, why they matter for a mathematical description of cortical computation, and how we intend to translate these concepts into first-order logic, Lean, and finally into SpherePOP, a domain-specific calculus for semantic computation.

The document proceeds in four layers: a common-language conceptual overview, a first-order logical reconstruction, a Lean specification intended for mechanized verification, and a SpherePOP implementation sketch. Each layer expresses the same underlying ideas but with progressively stricter constraints and clearer commitments.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction: What We Are Trying to Achieve}

The work described here aims to build a rigorous and fully formal model of a central idea: that cortical computation can be described as a combination of local nonlinear transformations (amplistwistors) and global geometric or spectral constraints (eigenmodes, resonances, and field interactions). 

While previous essays have developed the mathematics of amplitwistors, semigroup methods, and eigenmode structures, we now seek to build an \emph{integrated formal program}. The goal is to translate intuitive neurocomputational ideas into:

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
  \item a clear conceptual narrative understandable without mathematical training,
  \item a first-order logical theory describing the essential commitments,
  \item a Lean formalization that can serve as a basis for proof verification and mechanized reasoning,
  \item and a SpherePOP model that expresses these same concepts in an operational semantic calculus.
\end{enumerate}

Our overarching aim is to create a pathway from intuition to formal verification to executable semantics, ensuring that each conceptual claim can be expressed, checked, and instantiated at multiple levels of rigor.

\section{A Common-Language Explanation of the Amplistwistor Framework}

The central intuition is simple to state. When the brain processes information, each small region of cortex---often called a cortical column---performs a transformation on the incoming neural activity. This transformation is not merely a linear amplification or a rigid rotation; rather, it is a mixture of scaling, twisting, warping, and projecting information into more useful forms. We refer to these transformations as \emph{amplistwistors} because they generalize Needham's idea of the `<code>amplitwist,'' combining amplitude and twist operations into a higher-dimensional nonlinear map.
</code>
Each local step is simple, but the power comes from \emph{composition}. By applying many such transformations in sequence, the brain can build extremely complex functions. This is analogous to how deep neural networks work: many small nonlinear operations combine to yield a powerful representation.

However, the brain also exhibits global structure. Resonant modes, long-range coupling, and standing-wave phenomena observed in fMRI and MEG studies indicate that local operations are synchronized, guided, or constrained by global patterns. In our framework, these global patterns act as geometric boundary conditions on the local amplitwistor transformations.

Thus the system works at two scales simultaneously:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Local:} Each column applies an amplitwistor to its local field.
  \item \textbf{Global:} The entire cortical surface shapes and is shaped by eigenmodes, resonances, and long-range coherence.
\end{itemize}

Our aim in this essay is not to justify the biological plausibility of this model (though such justification exists in the literature), but rather to explain how to formalize it so it can be analyzed rigorously.

\section{First-Order Logical Description of the Framework}

We now restate the core commitments using first-order logic. This is not a full axiomatization, but a skeleton from which the Lean development will grow.

\subsection{Basic Ontology}

We assume three fundamental sorts:

\[
\mathsf{Column}, \quad \mathsf{Field}, \quad \mathsf{Transformation}.
\]

Intuitively:

\begin{itemize}
  \item elements of \(\mathsf{Column}\) represent cortical columns,
  \item elements of \(\mathsf{Field}\) represent states of neural activity,
  \item elements of \(\mathsf{Transformation}\) represent amplitwistors.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Primitive Relations and Functions}

We introduce the following functions:

\[
\mathsf{state} : \mathsf{Column} \to \mathsf{Field}
\]

\[
\mathsf{apply} : \mathsf{Transformation} \times \mathsf{Field} \to \mathsf{Field}
\]

\[
\mathsf{compose} : \mathsf{Transformation} \times \mathsf{Transformation} \to \mathsf{Transformation}
\]

and a global constraint relation:

\[
\mathsf{Resonant}(c_1, c_2)
\quad\text{meaning that columns } c_1, c_2 \text{ are constrained by a shared eigenmode.}
\]

\subsection{Axioms}

\paragraph{Axiom 1 (Local Update)}  
Every column evolves by applying a transformation to its state:

\[
\forall c \in \mathsf{Column},\ \exists T \in \mathsf{Transformation},\ 
\mathsf{state}(c)' = \mathsf{apply}(T, \mathsf{state}(c)).
\]

\paragraph{Axiom 2 (Compositionality)}  
Transformations are closed under composition:

\[
\forall T_1, T_2,\ \exists T_3,\ T_3 = \mathsf{compose}(T_1, T_2).
\]

\paragraph{Axiom 3 (Local Nonlinearity)}  
There exists at least one transformation that is not affine:

\[
\exists T\ \neg(\text{$T$ is affine}).
\]

\paragraph{Axiom 4 (Global Coherence)}  
If two columns are resonantly coupled, their updates interact:

\[
\forall c_1, c_2,\ \mathsf{Resonant}(c_1, c_2) 
\Rightarrow \mathsf{state}(c_1)' \text{ depends on } \mathsf{state}(c_2).
\]

This captures the empirical fact that resonant modes induce long-range correlations.

\subsection{Interpretation}

These axioms collectively define a dynamical system: local nonlinear updates constrained by global relations. The system is intentionally minimal; the Lean formalization will enrich types, introduce algebraic structure, and eventually support proofs of expressivity and well-posedness.

\section{Lean Formalization Roadmap}

In the next section (requested separately), we will begin translating these FOL commitments into Lean, including:

\begin{itemize}
  \item defining types for columns, fields, amplitwistors,
  \item implementing the composition algebra,
  \item proving that amplitwistors form a subalgebra of transformations on functions,
  \item eventually connecting to spectral graph theory for global constraints.
\end{itemize}

\section{SpherePOP Implementation Roadmap}

The final layer will express amplitwistor cascades as operations in SpherePOP, using its native metaphors of “bubbles,” “pops,” and “flow constructs” to encode transformation, projection, and resonance.

This will provide an executable, human-readable version of the theory that retains the intuitive structure of amplitwistor dynamics while remaining grounded in rigorous formalism.


\section{Amplitwistor Operators and Their Action on RSVP Field Dynamics}

The construction of the SpherePop operad provides a rigorous syntactic and combinatorial foundation for describing discrete generative structures such as spherical emissions, merges, and interference patterns. To connect this structure with the continuous physical and semantic dynamics of the Relativistic Scalar--Vector Plenum (RSVP), one must enrich the operad with a concrete algebra acting upon the RSVP field space. This section develops the mathematical connection between operadic composition and RSVP field evolution, showing how local Amplitwistor operators arise naturally as differential-geometric realizations of SpherePop operations, and how their cascaded action generates coherent dynamical solutions to the RSVP partial differential equations.

\subsection{RSVP Fields as a Semantic State Space}

