</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\title</span></span><span>{Cortical Columns as Amplitwistor Cascades:</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\\</span></span><span>
A Preview of the Framework}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\author</span></span><span>{Flyxion}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\date</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\today</span></span><span>}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{document}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\maketitle</span></span><span>

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{abstract}
This short preview introduces the central idea of the Amplitwistor
Cascades framework: cortical columns behave as localized nonlinear
transformations acting on continuous fields defined across the cortical
manifold. Each column implements an </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplistwistor}---a structured
combination of amplification, twisting, and projection---whose effects
propagate through the surrounding neural tissue according to the
dynamics of the RSVP semigroup. Discrete events (pops) and continuous
field evolution jointly produce cascades of spatiotemporal waves that
match empirical signatures of cortical computation. This document
summarizes the conceptual structure, mathematical principles, and neural
interpretation of this model.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{abstract}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Overview}

Cortical computation can be interpreted as a hybrid dynamical system in
which:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Local columns} apply nonlinear transformations to neural activity;
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Global geometry} imposes spectral and spatial constraints;
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Punctate events} inject structured perturbations;
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\textbf</span></span><span>{Continuous evolution} diffuses and transports these perturbations.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Amplistwistor Cascades unify these components. Local transformations
(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{amplistwistors}) operate pointwise on RSVP fields, while their
effects propagate through the cortex via semigroup evolution. Cascades
of such events generate traveling waves, modal interactions, and
hierarchical temporal processing.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Cortical Columns as Local Amplitwistors}

Each cortical column is modeled as a local operator
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x = (</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
where:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> controls local gain or attenuation,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> encodes twisting or directional realignment in the tangent space,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> regulates entropy, thresholding, or normalization.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

Applied to RSVP fields </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>, an amplitwistor acts as
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S)(x)
=
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\left</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>(x)),</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>
      </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\tau</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v}(x)),</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\;</span></span><span>
      </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\pi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>x(S(x))</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\right</span></span><span>).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

These operators generalize and formalize the intuitive picture of
columns applying nonlinear transformations to neural signals.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Pop Events and Localized Excitation}

SpherePop supplies the discrete part of the architecture: a </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{pop}
is a localized generative event characterized by a kernel </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>p(x)</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> and a
mode structure determining how it injects energy into RSVP fields.

A pop induces:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>p(f) = f + </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>p, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\delta</span></span><span> f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>p </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\propto</span></span><span> K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>p * </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathcal</span></span><span>{A}</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{p} .
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

Gaussian kernels approximate the Green's functions of the linearized
RSVP operator, ensuring that pops excite the same modal structures
observed in empirical cortical wave phenomena.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Continuous Evolution Between Events}

Between pops, RSVP fields evolve according to a semigroup:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\frac</span></span><span>{d}{dt}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S)(t)
=
F</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathrm</span></span><span>{RSVP}}(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S),
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\qquad</span></span><span>
(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S)(t)=T(t)f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This continuous flow integrates local perturbations and transmits them
across the cortical manifold.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Cascades: Composition of Local and Global Dynamics}

A sequence of pop events at times </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1&lt;t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>2&lt;</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span>&lt;t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span> produces:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
(</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\Phi</span></span><span>,</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\mathbf</span></span><span>{v},S)(t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n)
=
T(t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n-t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n-1}) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{n-1}} </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\cdots</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> T(t</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>1) </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\circ</span></span><span> T</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>{p</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0}(f</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>0).
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This defines an </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\emph</span></span><span>{Amplitwistor Cascade}. Its structure mirrors the
compositional behavior of neural computation:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> early pops excite high-frequency, rapidly decaying modes,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> later pops recruit wider, slower global modes,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> long cascades build hierarchical processing analogous to deep network layers.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Spectral Interpretation}

Eigenfunctions of the Laplace--Beltrami operator or the connectome
Laplacian provide a natural basis for analyzing cascades. A pop excites
coefficients
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\alpha</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n = </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\langle</span></span><span> K</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>p, </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\rangle</span></span><span>,
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>
while evolution damps them exponentially:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\[</span></span><span>
T(t)</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n = e</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">^</span></span><span>{-</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\kappa</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n t}</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\psi</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\]</span></span><span>

This reproduces:
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> fast, localized sensory responses (high </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>),
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> slow, distributed association dynamics (low </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\lambda</span></span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">_</span></span><span>n</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">$</span></span><span>),
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> coherent traveling waves across the cortical surface.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Interpretation in Neuroscience}

Amplitwistor Cascades provide a principled mechanistic account of:

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\begin</span></span><span>{itemize}
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> traveling cortical waves,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> multiscale temporal processing,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> long-range coherence,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> modal structure in fMRI and MEG signals,
  </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\item</span></span><span> hierarchical response latencies observed in ECoG studies.
</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\end</span></span><span>{itemize}

The model captures how discrete local transformations interact with
global field geometry to produce the layered, compositional behavior
characteristic of biological computation.

</span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">\section</span></span><span>{Conclusion}

This preview outlines a unified mathematical framework for understanding
cortical computation as a cascade of amplitwistor events propagating on
a dynamic field substrate. Full development includes a rigorous operadic
semantics, numerical implementation in Lean, and integration with the
RSVP formalism. The result is a coherent theory that connects local
microcircuit transformations to large-scale wave dynamics and
hierarchical cognitive processing.

